update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)ㄴ 적이 있다/없다 — Have done / Never done","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Expresses whether you have or have not experienced doing something in the past. Used to talk about past experiences.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄴ 적이 있다 (have done) / 없다 (have never done)","when_to_use":"When talking about past experiences or whether you''ve ever done something. Common in interviews, conversations about life experiences, and personal stories.","watch_out":"Use the past modifier -(으)ㄴ (가다 → 간 적, 먹다 → 먹은 적), never the present -는 or future -(으)ㄹ. It is very often combined with -아/어 보다 (가 본 적이 있어요 ''I''ve (tried) going''). For past states, -았/었던 적이 있다 is common (아팠던 적이 있어요). Don''t use it for everyday routine actions (밥을 먹은 적이 있어요 sounds odd) — it is for experiences.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 한국에 가 본 적이 있어요.","en":"I have been to Korea before."},{"ko":"그 영화를 본 적이 없어요.","en":"I have never seen that movie."},{"ko":"비행기를 타 본 적이 있으세요?","en":"Have you ever been on an airplane?"},{"ko":"해외에서 일한 적이 있는 사람이에요.","en":"He is a person who has worked abroad."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 한국에 가는 적이 있어요.","en":"I have been to Korea. (incorrect)","error_type":"Present modifier -는 used; past experience needs the past modifier -(으)ㄴ","correction":"저는 한국에 간 적이 있어요. / 저는 한국에 가 본 적이 있어요."},{"ko":"그를 만날 적이 있어요.","en":"I have met him. (incorrect)","error_type":"Future modifier -(으)ㄹ used instead of past -(으)ㄴ","correction":"그를 만난 적이 있어요."},{"ko":"김치를 먹은 적을 있어요.","en":"I have eaten kimchi. (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong particle: 적 takes 이 (or no particle), not 을","correction":"김치를 먹은 적이 있어요."}]},{"name":"(으)ㄴ 적이 있다 with frequency expressions","level":"Intermediate-Advanced","exp":"Combines past experience with frequency indicators to show how often something has been done in the past.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄴ 적이 + frequency word (한 번, 여러 번, 몇 번, 많이) + 있다\nor: frequency word + Verb stem + (으)ㄴ 적이 있다","when_to_use":"When you want to express past experiences with how often they occurred (여러 번, 한 번, 많이, etc.)","watch_out":"The frequency word can go either before the verb (여러 번 먹은 적이 있어요) or between 적이 and 있다 (먹은 적이 여러 번 있어요) — both are correct. With 거의 / 한 번도, use 없다: 한 번도 가 본 적이 없어요.","examples":[{"ko":"그 음식을 먹은 적이 여러 번 있어요.","en":"I have eaten that food many times."},{"ko":"눈이 내린 적이 거의 없어요.","en":"It has rarely snowed."},{"ko":"그런 일을 경험한 적이 몇 번 있어요.","en":"I have experienced that kind of situation a few times."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"그 음식을 여러 번 먹는 적이 있어요.","en":"I have eaten that food many times. (incorrect)","error_type":"Present modifier -는 used instead of past -(으)ㄴ","correction":"그 음식을 여러 번 먹은 적이 있어요. / 그 음식을 먹은 적이 여러 번 있어요."}]},{"name":"(으)ㄴ 적이 없다 with negation emphasis","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Strongly emphasizes that you have never experienced something, conveying more emphasis than simple negation.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄴ 적이 + 없다/없어요","when_to_use":"When emphasizing that you have absolutely no experience with something. Commonly used in denials, disclaimers, or expressing inexperience.","watch_out":"This form is more emphatic than ''안 했어요'' (didn''t do). Don''t use with present/future context. ''적이 없다'' always refers to past experience.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 거짓말을 한 적이 없어요.","en":"I have never lied."},{"ko":"그런 실수를 한 적이 없습니다.","en":"I have never made such a mistake."},{"ko":"외국에서 살아 본 적이 없는 사람이에요.","en":"He is someone who has never lived abroad."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 거짓말을 하지 않은 적이 없어요.","en":"Intended: I have never lied. (incorrect)","error_type":"Meaning error: the double negative actually says ''there was never a time I didn''t lie''","correction":"저는 거짓말을 한 적이 없어요."},{"ko":"앞으로 그렇게 할 적이 없을 거예요.","en":"I won''t do it in the future (incorrect - mixing future with past experience pattern)","error_type":"Using pattern with future context","correction":"앞으로는 그렇게 하지 않을 거예요. (Use different structure for future)"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '667904ac-0c2e-420b-bdd5-46452ccabc09' and md5(patterns_json) = '7b688a819a590e593465c9a115001a03';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)ㄴ/는 편이다 — Tend to","level":"Intermediate (B1)","exp":"Expresses a tendency, inclination, or general characteristic. It suggests that something is more often the case than not, or that someone has a habitual tendency.","structure":"Action verb (present habit) + 는 편이다: 자주 먹는 편이다\nAction verb (past occasion) + (으)ㄴ 편이다: 어제는 일찍 잔 편이다\nAdjective + (으)ㄴ 편이다: 매운 편이다, 조용한 편이다\n있다/없다 words + 는 편이다: 재미있는 편이다","when_to_use":"When describing general tendencies, habits, or characteristics softly (''rather / on the ... side'') instead of stating them as absolute facts.","watch_out":"편 here means ''side'' (as in ''on the spicy side''). With action verbs, add a degree or frequency word (많이, 자주, 잘, 일찍): 많이 먹는 편이에요, not just 먹는 편이에요. Adjectives take -(으)ㄴ, never -는 (매운 편, not 맵는 편). Don''t use it for objective, measurable facts (×키가 180cm인 편이에요).","examples":[{"ko":"저는 밤에 늦게 자는 편이에요.","en":"I tend to sleep late at night. (I have a tendency to go to bed late)"},{"ko":"한국 음식은 매운 편이에요.","en":"Korean food tends to be spicy. (It''s generally on the spicy side)"},{"ko":"요즘 날씨가 따뜻한 편이에요.","en":"The weather these days tends to be warm. (It''s relatively warm)"},{"ko":"그 학생은 조용한 편이다.","en":"That student tends to be quiet. (He''s relatively quiet)"},{"ko":"우리 회사는 복지가 좋은 편이에요.","en":"Our company has fairly good benefits. (It''s on the good side)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"한국 음식은 맵는 편이에요.","en":"Korean food tends to be spicy. (incorrect)","error_type":"Adjective + -는: adjectives take -(으)ㄴ 편이다 (and 맵다 is ㅂ-irregular)","correction":"한국 음식은 매운 편이에요."},{"ko":"어제는 밥을 먹은 편이었어요.","en":"Intended: Yesterday I ate quite a lot. (incorrect)","error_type":"Action verb without a degree word — 편이다 compares degree, so it needs 많이/잘/일찍 etc.","correction":"어제는 밥을 많이 먹은 편이에요."}]},{"name":"-는 편이 낫다 — It''s better to (do X rather than Y)","level":"Intermediate (B1)","exp":"Here 편 means ''option/side''. -는 편이 낫다 compares two options and says one of them is the better choice. It is used for advice and decisions, not for describing tendencies.","structure":"Verb stem + 는 편이 낫다 / 나아요 / 낫겠어요\nOften: A보다 B-는 편이 낫다, 차라리 ...-는 편이 낫다","when_to_use":"When giving advice or deciding between options: ''it would be better to...'', ''you''d better...''.","watch_out":"Don''t confuse with 편이다 (tendency). Use -는 with verbs; the past -(으)ㄴ is not used for advice. 낫다 is ㅅ-irregular: 나아요, 나은, 낫겠어요.","examples":[{"ko":"건강을 위해서는 아침에 일찍 일어나는 편이 나아요.","en":"For your health, it''s better to get up early in the morning."},{"ko":"결정하기 전에 한 번 더 생각해 보는 편이 나아요.","en":"It''s better to think it over once more before deciding."},{"ko":"길이 막히니까 택시보다 지하철을 타는 편이 낫겠어요.","en":"The roads are jammed, so taking the subway would be better than a taxi."},{"ko":"차라리 혼자 가는 편이 나을 것 같아요.","en":"I think it would be better to just go alone."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"늦었으니까 지금 출발한 편이 나아요.","en":"It''s late, so it''s better to leave now. (incorrect)","error_type":"Past modifier -(으)ㄴ used for advice; use -는","correction":"늦었으니까 지금 출발하는 편이 나아요."},{"ko":"택시보다 지하철을 타는 편이 낫어요.","en":"Taking the subway is better than a taxi. (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong conjugation of ㅅ-irregular 낫다","correction":"택시보다 지하철을 타는 편이 나아요."}]},{"name":"(으)ㄴ/는 편이 아니다 — Don''t tend to / Not inclined to","level":"Intermediate (B1)","exp":"Negation of the tendency pattern. Expresses that someone doesn''t tend to do something or something is not characteristically a certain way.","structure":"Verb/Adjective + (으)ㄴ/는 편이 아니다\nVerb/Adjective + (으)ㄴ/는 편이 아니에요 (polite form)","when_to_use":"When stating that a characteristic, habit, or tendency is not typically present. Used to clarify what someone doesn''t usually do or what something generally isn''t like.","watch_out":"This is different from simple negation (안/못). Here, you''re saying it''s not typical or characteristic—not that it never happens. Don''t use for absolute negations.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 아침에 일찍 일어나는 편이 아니에요.","en":"I don''t tend to wake up early in the morning. (It''s not my tendency)"},{"ko":"그 영화는 재미있는 편이 아니었어요.","en":"That movie wasn''t particularly interesting. (It wasn''t on the interesting side)"},{"ko":"제 동생은 조용한 편이 아니에요.","en":"My younger sibling isn''t the quiet type."},{"ko":"저는 매운 음식을 잘 먹는 편이 아니에요.","en":"I''m not really someone who handles spicy food well."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 아침을 잘 먹는 편이 안 해요.","en":"I don''t usually eat breakfast much. (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong negation: the negative of 편이다 is 편이 아니다","correction":"저는 아침을 잘 먹는 편이 아니에요."},{"ko":"어제 저는 늦게 자는 편이 아니었어요.","en":"Intended: Yesterday I didn''t go to bed late. (incorrect)","error_type":"-는 편 describes habits; a single past occasion needs a plain past (or -(으)ㄴ 편이다)","correction":"어제 저는 일찍 잤어요. / 저는 늦게 자는 편이 아니에요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '2c87c4d1-c591-4319-b2f7-20ee28fa4019' and md5(patterns_json) = '464b361ba84baf37e864cf4983331f0c';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)ㄴ/는데 — Background/Contrast","level":"Intermediate (A2-B1)","exp":"A connector that provides background information or shows contrast between two clauses. It explains the situation or context before presenting the main point, or highlights a contrast between what was expected and reality.","structure":"Action verbs and 있다/없다: stem + 는데 (가는데, 먹는데, 재미있는데)\nAdjectives: stem + (으)ㄴ데 (좋은데, 큰데; ㄹ drops: 길다 → 긴데; ㅂ-irregular: 춥다 → 추운데)\nNoun + 인데 (학생인데)\nPast (all): -았/었는데 (갔는데, 좋았는데)","when_to_use":"Use when you want to: (1) provide background context before the main clause, (2) show contrast or unexpected situations, (3) explain circumstances that lead to the main point, (4) express mild complaint or concern about a situation","watch_out":"Choose the form by word type, not tense: verbs and 있다/없다 take -는데, adjectives take -(으)ㄴ데, and anything in the past takes -았/었는데. Unlike -아/어서, -는데 CAN be followed by commands, suggestions and questions (날씨가 좋은데 산책할까요?). Whether it means background or contrast comes from context.","examples":[{"ko":"날씨가 좋은데 왜 밖에 안 나가요?","en":"The weather is nice, but why don''t you go outside? / It''s nice weather, (so) why aren''t you going out?"},{"ko":"제가 이걸 잘 못하는데 좀 도와주실 수 있어요?","en":"I''m not very good at this — could you help me a little?"},{"ko":"시간이 없었는데 어떻게 왔어요?","en":"You didn''t have time, but how did you come? / Despite not having time, how did you manage to come?"},{"ko":"동생은 키가 큰데 저는 작아요.","en":"My younger sibling is tall, but I''m short."},{"ko":"그 영화는 재미있었는데 너무 길었어요.","en":"The movie was interesting, but it was too long."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"날씨가 좋는데 산책할까요?","en":"The weather is nice — shall we go for a walk? (incorrect)","error_type":"Adjective + -는데: adjectives take -(으)ㄴ데","correction":"날씨가 좋은데 산책할까요?"},{"ko":"어제 그 식당에 가는데 문을 닫았어요.","en":"I went to that restaurant yesterday, but it was closed. (incorrect)","error_type":"Present -는데 used for a completed past event; use -았/었는데","correction":"어제 그 식당에 갔는데 문을 닫았어요."}]},{"name":"(으)ㄴ/는데 — Giving the situation before a request, suggestion or problem","level":"Intermediate (A2-B1)","exp":"The -는데 clause lays out a situation (often a problem) and the next clause says what follows from it: a problem, a suggestion, a question or a request. It is softer and less explicitly causal than -아/어서 or -(으)니까.","structure":"Verb / 있다·없다 + 는데, Adjective + (으)ㄴ데, Noun + 인데 + problem / suggestion / question","when_to_use":"When explaining why something is a problem, explaining reasons indirectly, expressing mild complaints, or when the cause naturally leads to an unexpected result","watch_out":"Softer than -아/어서 and -(으)니까. It can be followed by suggestions and commands (비가 오는데 우산 가져가세요), unlike -아/어서. Keep the stem rule: 없다 → 없는데 (not 없은데), 배고프다 → 배고픈데.","examples":[{"ko":"비가 오는데 우산이 없어요.","en":"It''s raining, but I don''t have an umbrella. / It''s raining, (and that''s a problem because) I don''t have an umbrella."},{"ko":"배가 고픈데 뭐 좀 먹을까요?","en":"I''m hungry — shall we eat something?"},{"ko":"요즘 많이 바쁜데 운동할 시간이 없어요.","en":"I''m really busy these days, so I have no time to exercise."},{"ko":"제 영어가 부족한데 좀 도와줄 수 있어요?","en":"My English isn''t great — could you help me a bit?"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"집에 음식이 없은데 시켜 먹을까요?","en":"There''s no food at home — shall we order in? (incorrect)","error_type":"없다 is conjugated like a verb: 없는데, not 없은데","correction":"집에 음식이 없는데 시켜 먹을까요?"},{"ko":"너무 배고프는데 밥 먹으러 가요.","en":"I''m so hungry — let''s go eat. (incorrect)","error_type":"Adjective + -는데: adjectives take -(으)ㄴ데","correction":"너무 배고픈데 밥 먹으러 가요."}]},{"name":"(으)ㄴ/는데 — Asking for/Providing Information about State","level":"Intermediate (B1)","exp":"Used in questions to ask about someone''s current state/situation before making a request, or to provide information about a state before asking what should be done about it. Softens requests.","structure":"Adj/Verb stem + (으)ㄴ데 / Verb stem + 는데 + question/request","when_to_use":"When politely asking questions about someone''s state or situation, softening requests, showing concern before asking for help, creating a more conversational and less abrupt tone","watch_out":"This pattern softens the tone, so it sounds more polite and considerate. Don''t use it when a direct statement or command is needed. The first clause describes a state, the second clause is usually a question or request.","examples":[{"ko":"얼굴이 창백한데 괜찮아요?","en":"Your face is pale, are you okay?"},{"ko":"일이 많으신데 우리를 도와주실 수 있어요?","en":"You have a lot of work, but could you help us?"},{"ko":"오늘 저녁에 시간 괜찮은데 만날까요?","en":"I''m free this evening — shall we meet?"},{"ko":"여기가 불편한데 다른 자리로 옮길까요?","en":"This spot is inconvenient, should we move to another seat?"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"얼굴이 창백하는데 괜찮아요?","en":"You look pale — are you okay? (incorrect)","error_type":"Adjective + -는데: 창백하다 is an adjective, so -(으)ㄴ데","correction":"얼굴이 창백한데 괜찮아요?"},{"ko":"제가 여기 처음이는데 좀 알려 주시겠어요?","en":"It''s my first time here — could you show me? (incorrect)","error_type":"Noun + 이다 takes 인데, not 이는데","correction":"제가 여기 처음인데 좀 알려 주시겠어요?"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '62d1f621-a970-449c-b804-843f79c914f9' and md5(patterns_json) = '86e0b3d2beaf35e88982c9fe64501ca4';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)니까 — Because/Since","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Expresses the reason or cause for an action or decision. The (으)니까 clause provides the reason, and the main clause shows the result or consequence.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + (으)니까 + main clause\n\n- Vowel stem: -니까 (가다 → 가니까)\n- Consonant stem: -으니까 (먹다 → 먹으니까)\n- ㄹ stem: ㄹ drops + -니까 (살다 → 사니까, 멀다 → 머니까)\n- ㅂ-irregular: 덥다 → 더우니까; ㄷ-irregular: 듣다 → 들으니까\n- Past: -았/었으니까 (왔으니까); Noun: (이)니까 (학생이니까)","when_to_use":"When explaining why something happens or why you do something; when giving a reason for a suggestion, command, or statement. More direct and emphatic than -어서/아서.","watch_out":"Unlike -아/어서, -(으)니까 CAN be followed by commands and suggestions (-세요, -(으)ㅂ시다, -(으)ㄹ까요). It can take past tense (-았/었으니까). Don''t use it for apologies and thanks: say 늦어서 죄송합니다, not 늦으니까 죄송합니다.","examples":[{"ko":"날씨가 좋으니까 공원에 가요.","en":"Since the weather is nice, let''s go to the park."},{"ko":"시간이 없으니까 빨리 가야 해요.","en":"Because there''s no time, I have to go quickly."},{"ko":"피곤하니까 좀 쉬세요.","en":"Since you''re tired, please rest a bit."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"시간이 없니까 빨리 가야 해요.","en":"Because there''s no time, I have to hurry. (incorrect)","error_type":"Consonant stem without 으","correction":"시간이 없으니까 빨리 가야 해요. (없- ends in a consonant, so add -으니까)"},{"ko":"늦으니까 죄송합니다.","en":"I''m sorry for being late. (incorrect)","error_type":"-(으)니까 is not used with apologies or thanks","correction":"늦어서 죄송합니다."}]},{"name":"(으)니까 — Making Suggestions/Offers","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Used to make gentle suggestions or offers based on the reason provided in the (으)니까 clause. The main clause often contains a suggestion, invitation, or polite command.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + (으)니까 + suggestion/invitation/command\n\nCommon endings: -세요, -지요, -자, -어요/아요","when_to_use":"When you want to suggest something based on a given situation or condition. More polite and naturally flowing than stating reasons with -어서/아서.","watch_out":"Make sure the suggestion logically follows from the reason. Don''t use this pattern with statements about unchangeable facts without a suggestion. The tone should be natural and not forced.","examples":[{"ko":"시간이 늦었으니까 택시를 타세요.","en":"It''s late, so take a taxi."},{"ko":"날이 더우니까 얇은 옷을 입으세요.","en":"Since it''s hot, why don''t you wear light clothes?"},{"ko":"내일 시험이니까 오늘은 열심히 공부하자.","en":"The exam is tomorrow, so let''s study hard today."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"비가 와니까 우산을 가져가세요.","en":"It''s raining, so take an umbrella. (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong stem: 오다 + -니까 = 오니까 (no vowel contraction)","correction":"비가 오니까 우산을 가져가세요."}]},{"name":"(으)니까 — Discovery: ''when I did X, I found...''","level":"Intermediate","exp":"After an action verb, -(으)니까 can also mean ''when/after I did X, I found / realized Y''. The main clause is something the speaker discovered or noticed, usually in the past tense.","structure":"Action verb stem + (으)니까 (no past tense on this verb) + discovered fact\nOften with -아/어 보니까: 먹어 보니까, 살아 보니까","when_to_use":"When telling what you found out or noticed as a result of doing something: arriving somewhere, waking up, trying something.","watch_out":"The discovery meaning uses the plain form (가니까), not -았/었으니까 — 갔으니까 means ''because I went''. The main clause should be something found or noticed, not your own deliberate action.","examples":[{"ko":"집에 와 보니까 아무도 없었어요.","en":"When I got home, there was nobody there."},{"ko":"아침에 일어나니까 눈이 오고 있었어요.","en":"When I woke up in the morning, it was snowing."},{"ko":"한국에 살아 보니까 생각보다 재미있어요.","en":"Now that I''ve lived in Korea, it''s more fun than I expected."},{"ko":"먹어 보니까 별로 맵지 않네요.","en":"Now that I''ve tried it, it isn''t that spicy."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"문을 열었으니까 고양이가 있었어요.","en":"When I opened the door, there was a cat. (incorrect)","error_type":"-았/었으니까 means ''because''; discovery uses the plain -(으)니까","correction":"문을 여니까 고양이가 있었어요."},{"ko":"집에 오니까 제가 저녁을 만들었어요.","en":"When I got home, I made dinner. (incorrect)","error_type":"Discovery -(으)니까 needs something found/noticed in the main clause, not your own planned action","correction":"집에 와서 저녁을 만들었어요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'ae8b5dcc-898f-4f9e-9db5-2364c08d9cc2' and md5(patterns_json) = '67f162b85c5053fc7b353691abe6a1d1';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)ㄹ 거예요 — Future tense","level":"A2","exp":"This is the most common way to express future plans, intentions, or predictions in Korean. It combines the future modifier ''(으)ㄹ'' with ''거예요'' (casual polite form of ''것이다'').","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + (으)ㄹ 거예요\n- Consonant stem: -을 거예요 (먹을 거예요)\n- Vowel stem: -ㄹ 거예요 (갈 거예요)\n- ㄹ stem: just add 거예요 (만들다 → 만들 거예요)\n- Irregulars: 듣다 → 들을, 돕다 → 도울, 짓다 → 지을 거예요","when_to_use":"Use when talking about: future plans, what you will do, predictions about others'' actions, intentions, or expected outcomes in casual polite contexts","watch_out":"In ''I/we'' statements and ''you'' questions it states or asks about a plan (내일 뭐 할 거예요?); about weather, things or other people it is often a prediction (비가 올 거예요 ''It will probably rain''). Don''t confuse with -(으)ㄹ 거 같아요 (seems like it will). 거예요 is the polite 해요 style; -(으)ㄹ 겁니다 is formal.","examples":[{"ko":"내일 뭐 할 거예요?","en":"What will you do tomorrow?"},{"ko":"저는 서울에 갈 거예요.","en":"I will go to Seoul."},{"ko":"비가 올 거예요.","en":"It will rain."},{"ko":"친구를 만날 거예요.","en":"I will meet my friend."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"내일 친구를 만나을 거예요.","en":"I will meet a friend tomorrow. (incorrect)","error_type":"Vowel stem takes -ㄹ 거예요, not -을","correction":"내일 친구를 만날 거예요."},{"ko":"그는 간다 거예요.","en":"He will go. (incorrect)","error_type":"Plain form -다 used before 거예요","correction":"그는 갈 거예요. (Use the future modifier -ㄹ)"}]},{"name":"-(으)ㄹ 거 같아요 — ''Seems like it will...''","level":"A2","exp":"This pattern expresses a prediction or guess about a future event based on current signs or probability, rather than a definite plan. It softens the statement by indicating uncertainty.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ 거 + 같아요\n- If verb stem ends in consonant: add ''-을 거 같아요''\n- If verb stem ends in vowel: add ''-ㄹ 거 같아요''","when_to_use":"Use when making predictions, expressing educated guesses, or when you''re not certain about future events. It shows probability rather than certainty.","watch_out":"This is softer and more speculative than -(으)ㄹ 거예요. Don''t use it for your own firm plans. In writing, 것 같아요 is the standard spelling; 거 같아요 is spoken.","examples":[{"ko":"이 영화는 재미있을 거 같아요.","en":"This movie seems like it will be interesting."},{"ko":"내일은 눈이 올 거 같아요.","en":"It seems like it will snow tomorrow."},{"ko":"그 사람이 이 선물을 좋아할 거 같아요.","en":"I think he/she will like this present."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"그 사람이 이 선물을 좋아하을 거 같아요.","en":"I think he will like this present. (incorrect)","error_type":"Vowel stem takes -ㄹ, not -을","correction":"그 사람이 이 선물을 좋아할 거 같아요."}]},{"name":"-(으)ㄹ 거예요 vs -(으)ㄹ 겁니다 — Politeness levels","level":"A2-B1","exp":"Both express future tense but differ in formality. ''거예요'' is casual polite (conversational), while ''겁니다'' is formal polite (for official situations, announcements, or formal writing).","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ 거예요 (casual polite)\nVerb stem + (으)ㄹ 겁니다 (formal polite)\nVerb stem + (으)ㄹ 거야 (casual, informal)","when_to_use":"Use ''거예요'' in everyday conversations with acquaintances. Use ''겁니다'' in formal settings like news broadcasts, official announcements, or business contexts.","watch_out":"-(으)ㄹ 거예요 is fine in most polite conversation; -(으)ㄹ 겁니다 sounds formal (meetings, announcements, speaking to superiors). Official documents usually use -(으)ㄹ 것입니다 or -(으)ㄹ 예정입니다.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 집에 갈 거예요. (casual polite)","en":"I will go home. (casual polite)"},{"ko":"저는 집에 갈 겁니다. (formal polite)","en":"I will go home. (formal polite)"},{"ko":"우리는 새 건물을 지을 거예요.","en":"We will build a new building. (casual polite)"},{"ko":"우리는 새 건물을 지을 겁니다.","en":"We will build a new building. (formal polite)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"내일 회의에 참석할 거입니다.","en":"I will attend tomorrow''s meeting. (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong contraction: 거 + 입니다 becomes 겁니다 (or 것입니다 in full)","correction":"내일 회의에 참석할 겁니다. / 참석할 것입니다."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '74c14aee-da8b-4a71-8206-a53b2221b44b' and md5(patterns_json) = '9e14e341f0010abe635ba1480fe39e38';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)ㄹ 때 — When","level":"Beginner","exp":"Expresses the time when an action or state occurs. The main clause describes what happens at that time.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + (으)ㄹ 때 + main clause\n- Vowel stem: -ㄹ 때 (갈 때)\n- Consonant stem: -을 때 (먹을 때)\n- ㄹ stem: just add 때 (살 때, 만들 때)\n- Irregulars: 덥다 → 더울 때, 듣다 → 들을 때\n- Completed/past event: -았/었을 때 (갔을 때)\n- Nouns: Noun + 때 (방학 때, 점심 때)","when_to_use":"When describing simultaneous actions or the timing of an event. Used for habitual situations or general truths about when something happens.","watch_out":"The subjects of the two clauses can be the same or different. -(으)ㄹ 때 itself has no tense; the final verb shows the tense. For an event that was already completed at that moment, use -았/었을 때 (비가 왔을 때, 한국에 갔을 때) — this is completely standard. Don''t use the present modifier -는 (×가는 때).","examples":[{"ko":"비가 올 때 우산을 가져가요.","en":"When it rains, I take an umbrella."},{"ko":"학교에 갈 때 친구를 만나요.","en":"When I go to school, I meet my friend."},{"ko":"음식이 너무 매울 때 물을 마셔요.","en":"When food is too spicy, I drink water."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"학교에 가는 때 친구를 만나요.","en":"When I go to school, I meet my friend. (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong modifier: 때 takes -(으)ㄹ, not -는","correction":"학교에 갈 때 친구를 만나요."},{"ko":"날씨가 덥을 때 아이스크림을 먹어요.","en":"When it''s hot, I eat ice cream. (incorrect)","error_type":"ㅂ-irregular not applied: 덥다 → 더울 때","correction":"날씨가 더울 때 아이스크림을 먹어요."}]},{"name":"(으)ㄹ 때마다 — Whenever/Every time when","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Indicates that something happens repeatedly or every time a certain condition occurs. 마다 means ''every'' or ''each time''.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ 때마다 + main clause\n- Same vowel/consonant rules as (으)ㄹ 때\n- 마다 is added to emphasize repetition","when_to_use":"When describing habitual or repeated actions that occur every time something happens. Emphasizes frequency and regularity.","watch_out":"마다 adds ''every time / whenever''. The main clause usually describes a repeated reaction or habit. Use -(으)ㄹ 때마다 even for repeated events in the past (들을 때마다 울었어요). Don''t add another particle after 마다.","examples":[{"ko":"엄마를 볼 때마다 행복해요.","en":"Whenever I see mom, I''m happy."},{"ko":"그 영화를 볼 때마다 울어요.","en":"Every time I watch that movie, I cry."},{"ko":"도움이 필요할 때마다 저한테 전화하세요.","en":"Call me whenever you need help."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"그 노래를 듣을 때마다 울어요.","en":"Every time I hear that song, I cry. (incorrect)","error_type":"ㄷ-irregular not applied: 듣다 → 들을","correction":"그 노래를 들을 때마다 울어요."},{"ko":"밥을 먹을 때마다에 기뻐요.","en":"Every time I eat, I''m happy. (incorrect)","error_type":"Extra particle after 마다","correction":"밥을 먹을 때마다 기뻐요."}]},{"name":"-았/었을 때 — When (something had happened / in the past)","level":"Beginner–Intermediate","exp":"-았/었을 때 means ''when'' for an event that was already completed at that time, or for a past state or period of life. 갈 때 is while going (before arriving); 갔을 때 is after arriving / while there.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + 았/었을 때 (가다 → 갔을 때, 먹다 → 먹었을 때, 하다 → 했을 때)\nNoun + 였을 때 (vowel) / 이었을 때 (consonant): 의사였을 때, 학생이었을 때","when_to_use":"For one-time past moments (''when I arrived'', ''when it rained'') and past periods (''when I was a student''). Compare: 한국에 갈 때 비행기에서 잤어요 (on the way) vs 한국에 갔을 때 김치를 먹어 봤어요 (once there).","watch_out":"The difference between 올 때 and 왔을 때 is whether the action is finished, not just tense. 어릴 때 and 어렸을 때 both mean ''when I was young''. Nouns ending in a consonant take 이었을 때.","examples":[{"ko":"비가 왔을 때 우산이 없어서 다 젖었어요.","en":"When it rained, I didn''t have an umbrella, so I got soaked."},{"ko":"한국에 갔을 때 김치를 처음 먹어 봤어요.","en":"When I went to Korea, I tried kimchi for the first time."},{"ko":"집에 도착했을 때 동생은 자고 있었어요.","en":"When I got home, my younger sibling was sleeping."},{"ko":"학생이었을 때 이 동네에 살았어요.","en":"When I was a student, I lived in this neighborhood."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"학생였을 때 이 동네에 살았어요.","en":"When I was a student, I lived in this neighborhood. (incorrect)","error_type":"Consonant-ending noun needs 이었을 때","correction":"학생이었을 때 이 동네에 살았어요."},{"ko":"집에 도착하었을 때 동생은 자고 있었어요.","en":"When I got home, my sibling was sleeping. (incorrect)","error_type":"하다 + -었- contracts to 했","correction":"집에 도착했을 때 동생은 자고 있었어요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'b752dee2-a026-424a-b83a-1071247e4ca3' and md5(patterns_json) = '4f45e3d86e68598869ebf317dffff907';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)ㄹ 뻔하다 — Almost did","level":"Intermediate (B1)","exp":"Expresses that an action was almost completed or an event almost happened, but didn''t. It indicates a near miss or a close call.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ 뻔하다\n\n• If verb stem ends in consonant: -을 뻔하다\n• If verb stem ends in vowel or ㄹ: -ㄹ 뻔하다","when_to_use":"When describing situations where something almost occurred but was prevented or didn''t actually happen. Common in storytelling, warnings, and explanations of narrow escapes.","watch_out":"• Always -(으)ㄹ before 뻔하다 — never -았/었을 or -는\n• Almost always used in the past (뻔했어요), because it describes something that nearly happened but didn''t\n• Not to be confused with the adjective 뻔하다 ''obvious, predictable'' (뻔한 이야기 ''a predictable story'')\n• Often followed by relief or by what saved you (-는데, -지만)","examples":[{"ko":"차에 치일 뻔했어요.","en":"I almost got hit by a car."},{"ko":"늦을 뻔했는데 택시를 타서 간신히 도착했어요.","en":"I almost was late, but I took a taxi and barely made it."},{"ko":"물에 빠질 뻔했어요.","en":"I almost fell into the water."},{"ko":"시험에 떨어질 뻔했어요.","en":"I almost failed the exam."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"차에 치였을 뻔했어요.","en":"I almost got hit by a car. (incorrect)","error_type":"Past tense before 뻔하다","correction":"차에 치일 뻔했어요. (Use -(으)ㄹ 뻔하다)"},{"ko":"어제 계단에서 넘어질 뻔해요.","en":"I almost fell on the stairs yesterday. (incorrect)","error_type":"Present tense: a near miss has already happened, so 뻔하다 goes in the past","correction":"어제 계단에서 넘어질 뻔했어요."}]},{"name":"-아/어서 죽을 뻔했다 — Exaggeration (''I nearly died of...'')","level":"Intermediate (B1)","exp":"In everyday speech, -(으)ㄹ 뻔했다 is often used for exaggeration: ''(so hungry/hot/funny that) I almost died''. Nothing actually nearly happened; it just stresses how strong the feeling was.","structure":"Reason + -아/어서 + 죽을 뻔했다 / 큰일 날 뻔했다 / 울 뻔했다","when_to_use":"In casual conversation to emphasize hunger, heat, fear, laughter, boredom, etc. 큰일 날 뻔했어요 (''That was close!'') is used after a real near miss.","watch_out":"This is casual and exaggerated — avoid it in formal writing. The verb before 뻔하다 still takes -(으)ㄹ (죽을, 울, 날).","examples":[{"ko":"배가 고파서 죽을 뻔했어요.","en":"I was so hungry I nearly died."},{"ko":"너무 웃겨서 죽을 뻔했어요.","en":"It was so funny I almost died laughing."},{"ko":"영화가 너무 슬퍼서 울 뻔했어요.","en":"The movie was so sad I almost cried."},{"ko":"여권을 두고 올 뻔했어요. 큰일 날 뻔했어요!","en":"I almost left my passport behind. That was close!"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"더워서 죽은 뻔했어요.","en":"It was so hot I nearly died. (incorrect)","error_type":"Past modifier -(으)ㄴ used; 뻔하다 needs -(으)ㄹ","correction":"더워서 죽을 뻔했어요."},{"ko":"너무 무서워서 울 뻔하었어요.","en":"I was so scared I almost cried. (incorrect)","error_type":"하다 + -었- contracts to 했","correction":"너무 무서워서 울 뻔했어요."}]},{"name":"(으)ㄹ 뻔하다 + Counter-action — Almost did, but prevented","level":"Intermediate (B2)","exp":"Combines (으)ㄹ 뻔하다 with expressions showing prevention or avoidance, emphasizing what stopped the near-miss from happening.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ 뻔했는데 / (으)ㄹ 뻔했지만 + what prevented it\n(often with -아/어서, -(으)ㄴ 덕분에)","when_to_use":"When explaining a narrow escape with emphasis on what prevented it. Often includes ''but,'' ''however,'' or explanations of why it didn''t happen. Creates a more complete narrative.","watch_out":"• Requires two actions: almost-action and prevention-action\n• Second verb often shows the saving action (받다, 잡다, 피하다, etc.)\n• Temporal sequence matters: prevention must follow the near-miss\n• Can include reasons why with ''because'' expressions","examples":[{"ko":"떨어질 뻔했는데 친구가 붙잡아 줬어요.","en":"I almost fell, but my friend caught me."},{"ko":"지각할 뻔했지만 택시를 타서 겨우 도착했어요.","en":"I was almost late, but I took a taxi and just made it."},{"ko":"사고가 날 뻔했는데 브레이크를 밟아서 피했어요.","en":"I almost had an accident, but I hit the brakes and avoided it."},{"ko":"헤어질 뻔했는데 대화로 잘 풀었어요.","en":"We almost broke up, but we worked it out by talking."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"떨어질 뻔했는데 친구가 붙잡아 줬어서 괜찮았어요.","en":"I almost fell, but I was fine because my friend caught me. (incorrect)","error_type":"-았/었- cannot go before -아/어서","correction":"떨어질 뻔했는데 친구가 붙잡아 줘서 괜찮았어요."},{"ko":"떨어졌을 뻔했는데 친구가 붙잡아 줬어요.","en":"I almost fell but my friend caught me. (INCORRECT)","error_type":"Using past tense in (으)ㄹ form before 뻔하다","correction":"떨어질 뻔했는데 친구가 붙잡아 줬어요. (Remove -었)"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'e2770d70-d58c-4c47-bbea-5133ac0dbfca' and md5(patterns_json) = '50a06a46bbfdb07b15b7847627f620fd';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)ㄹ 수 있다/없다 — Can/Cannot","level":"A2","exp":"Express ability or possibility to do something, or inability/impossibility","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ 수 있다/없다\n\n• Consonant stem → -을 수 있다 (먹을 수)\n• Vowel stem → -ㄹ 수 있다 (갈 수)\n• ㄹ stem → just add 수 (만들 수, 놀 수)\n• Irregulars: 듣다 → 들을 수, 돕다 → 도울 수","when_to_use":"When expressing: (1) present ability (''can do''), (2) future possibility (''will be able to''), (3) inability (''cannot do''), (4) general capability or permission","watch_out":"• Means ability (''can'') and also possibility (''it can happen'')\n• For permission, -아/어도 되다 is more common (가도 돼요?)\n• Past: -(으)ㄹ 수 있었다 / 없었다 (was / wasn''t able to)\n• Adding 가 is common for emphasis: 할 수가 없어요\n• For ''can''t do (skill)'', 못 + verb is also very common: 수영을 못 해요","examples":[{"ko":"저는 한국어를 조금 할 수 있어요.","en":"I can speak a little Korean."},{"ko":"그는 이 문제를 풀 수 없어요.","en":"He cannot solve this problem."},{"ko":"내일 시간이 있으면 갈 수 있어요.","en":"If I have time tomorrow, I''ll be able to go."},{"ko":"누구나 이것을 할 수 있습니다.","en":"Anyone can do this."},{"ko":"운전면허가 있으면 운전할 수 있어요.","en":"If you have a driver''s license, you can drive."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 김치찌개를 만들을 수 있어요.","en":"I can make kimchi stew. (incorrect)","error_type":"ㄹ-stem: add 수 directly, no 을","correction":"저는 김치찌개를 만들 수 있어요."},{"ko":"이 음악을 듣을 수 있어요?","en":"Can you hear this music? (incorrect)","error_type":"ㄷ-irregular not applied: 듣다 → 들을","correction":"이 음악을 들을 수 있어요?"},{"ko":"저는 한국어를 말하을 수 있어요.","en":"I can speak Korean. (incorrect)","error_type":"Vowel stem takes -ㄹ 수, not -을 수","correction":"저는 한국어를 말할 수 있어요."}]},{"name":"-(으)ㄹ 수밖에 없다 — Have no choice but to / It''s bound to","level":"B1","exp":"Literally ''there is no way other than doing X''. It means ''have no choice but to'', or, with adjectives and descriptions, ''it''s only natural that / it''s bound to be''.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + (으)ㄹ 수밖에 없다\n(탈 수밖에 없었어요, 많을 수밖에 없어요)","when_to_use":"When explaining that circumstances forced a choice, or that a result is inevitable given the situation.","watch_out":"밖에 always pairs with a negative, so the ending is always 없다 (never 있다 or 하다). Write 수밖에 together (밖에 is a particle here).","examples":[{"ko":"버스가 끊겨서 택시를 탈 수밖에 없었어요.","en":"The buses had stopped, so I had no choice but to take a taxi."},{"ko":"이 식당은 맛있고 싸서 손님이 많을 수밖에 없어요.","en":"This restaurant is tasty and cheap, so it''s bound to have lots of customers."},{"ko":"돈이 없어서 집에서 밥을 먹을 수밖에 없어요.","en":"I have no money, so I have no choice but to eat at home."},{"ko":"매일 연습했으니까 잘할 수밖에 없죠.","en":"She practiced every day, so of course she''s good."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"버스가 끊겨서 택시를 탈 수밖에 있었어요.","en":"I had no choice but to take a taxi. (incorrect)","error_type":"밖에 must be followed by a negative: 없다","correction":"버스가 끊겨서 택시를 탈 수밖에 없었어요."},{"ko":"돈이 없어서 집에서 먹을 수밖에 했어요.","en":"I had no choice but to eat at home. (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong verb after 수밖에: always 없다","correction":"돈이 없어서 집에서 먹을 수밖에 없었어요."}]},{"name":"-(으)ㄹ 수 있다 — Possibility (''it can / might happen'')","level":"B1","exp":"Besides ability, -(으)ㄹ 수 있다 expresses possibility: something can or may happen. Adding 도 (-(으)ㄹ 수도 있다) makes it ''might / could also''.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + (으)ㄹ 수 있다 (possibility)\nVerb/Adjective stem + (으)ㄹ 수도 있다 (might)\nPast possibility that didn''t happen: -(으)ㄹ 수도 있었다","when_to_use":"For warnings, side effects, reassurance (''that can happen''), and guesses about what might happen.","watch_out":"Context decides between ability and possibility. For ''could have happened (but didn''t)'', -(으)ㄹ 수도 있었다 makes the meaning clear (or -(으)ㄹ 뻔했다 for a near miss); plain -(으)ㄹ 수 있었다 is usually read as ''was able to'', though news style also uses it for a possibility (큰 사고로 이어질 수 있었다).","examples":[{"ko":"길이 막힐 수 있으니까 일찍 출발하세요.","en":"Traffic can be heavy, so leave early."},{"ko":"이 약을 먹으면 졸릴 수 있어요.","en":"This medicine may make you drowsy."},{"ko":"누구나 실수할 수 있어요.","en":"Anyone can make mistakes."},{"ko":"내일 비가 올 수도 있어요.","en":"It might rain tomorrow."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"이 약을 먹으면 졸리을 수 있어요.","en":"This medicine may make you drowsy. (incorrect)","error_type":"Vowel stem takes -ㄹ 수, not -을 수 (졸리다 → 졸릴 수)","correction":"이 약을 먹으면 졸릴 수 있어요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '7708dbbb-2b2f-4fcc-87ab-5e9e2bc3e4cc' and md5(patterns_json) = '594eb81cb678adfafdb529b1726d8aa9';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)ㄹ 줄 알다/모르다 — Know how to","level":"Intermediate","exp":"This pattern expresses the ability or knowledge of how to do something. ''(으)ㄹ 줄'' means ''a way to'' or ''how to,'' and when combined with ''알다'' (to know) it means ''to know how to do something.'' With ''모르다'' (to not know), it means ''to not know how to do something.'' It indicates both capability and awareness.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ 줄 알다/모르다\n- Consonant stem: -을 줄 (읽을 줄)\n- Vowel stem: -ㄹ 줄 (탈 줄)\n- ㄹ stem: just add 줄 (만들 줄)\n- Irregulars: 굽다 → 구울 줄, 짓다 → 지을 줄","when_to_use":"Use this pattern when expressing knowledge or ignorance of a skill or ability. It''s commonly used for explaining what someone can or cannot do, particularly emphasizing the knowledge/awareness aspect rather than mere capability.","watch_out":"Compare -(으)ㄹ 수 있다 (can, is able/possible): -(으)ㄹ 줄 알다 is about having learned the skill or method. Careful in the past tense: -(으)ㄹ 줄 알았다 usually means ''I thought (that...)'', not ''I knew how to'' — see the third lesson.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 한국어를 읽을 줄 알아요.","en":"I know how to read Korean."},{"ko":"그 친구는 수영을 할 줄 몰라요.","en":"That friend doesn''t know how to swim."},{"ko":"아이가 자전거를 탈 줄 알게 되었어요.","en":"The child has learned how to ride a bicycle."},{"ko":"저는 김치찌개를 끓일 줄 알아요.","en":"I know how to make kimchi stew."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 한국 음식을 만들을 줄 알아요.","en":"I know how to make Korean food. (incorrect)","error_type":"ㄹ-stem: add 줄 directly, no 을","correction":"저는 한국 음식을 만들 줄 알아요."},{"ko":"저는 노래를 할 줄 할 수 있어요.","en":"I can know how to sing. (INCORRECT)","error_type":"Pattern mixing/redundancy","correction":"저는 노래를 할 줄 알아요 - Don''t combine ''(으)ㄹ 줄 알다'' with ''-(으)ㄹ 수 있다''"}]},{"name":"(으)ㄹ 줄 모르다 — Not know how to / Unaware of","level":"Intermediate","exp":"The negative form using ''모르다'' (not to know) expresses the inability or lack of knowledge about how to do something. This form emphasizes that someone hasn''t learned or doesn''t understand how to perform an action, rather than simply being unable to do it.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ 줄 모르다\n- Present: (으)ㄹ 줄 몰라요\n- Past: (으)ㄹ 줄 몰랐어요","when_to_use":"Use when stating that someone doesn''t know how to do something, either currently or in the past. It''s useful for explaining limitations in skills or knowledge. Often used in casual conversation and storytelling.","watch_out":"모르다 already means ''not know'', so don''t add 안 or 못. 모르다 is 르-irregular: 몰라요, 몰랐어요. Note that -(으)ㄹ 줄 몰랐어요 often means ''I had no idea (that...)'' rather than ''I didn''t know how to'' — context decides.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 운전을 할 줄 몰라요.","en":"I don''t know how to drive."},{"ko":"그때 저는 영어를 할 줄 몰랐어요.","en":"Back then, I didn''t know how to speak English."},{"ko":"아직도 컴퓨터를 사용할 줄 몰라요.","en":"I still don''t know how to use a computer."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 운전을 할 줄 안 몰라요.","en":"I don''t know how to drive. (INCORRECT)","error_type":"Redundant negation","correction":"저는 운전을 할 줄 몰라요 - ''모르다'' already contains negation; don''t add ''안''"},{"ko":"그는 수영할 줄 못 몰라요.","en":"He doesn''t know how to swim. (INCORRECT)","error_type":"Double negation/wrong auxiliary","correction":"그는 수영을 할 줄 몰라요 - Use only ''모르다,'' not ''못'' with it"}]},{"name":"-(으)ㄹ 줄 알았다/몰랐다 — I thought... / I had no idea...","level":"Intermediate","exp":"In the past tense this pattern often expresses an expectation: -(으)ㄹ 줄 알았다 ''I thought (it would...)'' and -(으)ㄹ 줄 몰랐다 ''I didn''t think / had no idea (it would...)''. It is typically used when reality turned out differently, or to say ''I knew it!''.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + (으)ㄹ 줄 알았다 (I thought it would...)\nVerb/Adjective stem + (으)ㄹ 줄 몰랐다 (I had no idea it would...)\nPresent state / completed fact: -(으)ㄴ/는 줄 알았다 (학생인 줄 알았어요)","when_to_use":"When talking about what you expected versus what happened: surprises, misunderstandings, or confirming a prediction (''I knew you''d come!'').","watch_out":"For ''learned how to'', use -(으)ㄹ 줄 알게 되다, not -(으)ㄹ 줄 알았다. Irregulars apply: 어렵다 → 어려울 줄, 덥다 → 더울 줄.","examples":[{"ko":"비가 올 줄 알았어요. 그래서 우산을 가져왔어요.","en":"I thought it would rain, so I brought an umbrella."},{"ko":"시험이 이렇게 어려울 줄 몰랐어요.","en":"I had no idea the exam would be this hard."},{"ko":"네가 올 줄 알았어!","en":"I knew you''d come!"},{"ko":"길이 이렇게 막힐 줄 몰랐어요.","en":"I had no idea the traffic would be this bad."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"시험이 이렇게 어렵을 줄 몰랐어요.","en":"I had no idea the exam would be this hard. (incorrect)","error_type":"ㅂ-irregular not applied: 어렵다 → 어려울","correction":"시험이 이렇게 어려울 줄 몰랐어요."},{"ko":"작년에 피아노를 칠 줄 알았어요.","en":"Intended: I learned to play the piano last year. (incorrect)","error_type":"Meaning: -(으)ㄹ 줄 알았다 = ''knew how to / thought that''; ''learned to'' is -(으)ㄹ 줄 알게 되다","correction":"작년에 피아노를 칠 줄 알게 됐어요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '9662a286-555a-43dc-9ed7-8ed96f562239' and md5(patterns_json) = 'fa0c5b88f7d502c9c37d5c72ca9633bf';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)ㄹ 텐데 — Probably / I expect","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Expresses the speaker''s expectation or assumption about a present, future or (with -았/었을 텐데) past situation, based on their judgment or experience. It usually sets up a following clause.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + (으)ㄹ 텐데\n- Consonant stem: -을 텐데 (좋을 텐데)\n- Vowel stem: -ㄹ 텐데 (바쁠 텐데)\n- Past guess: -았/었을 텐데 (도착했을 텐데)\n- Noun: 일 텐데 (학생일 텐데)","when_to_use":"When stating an assumption (''surely...'', ''must be...'') that sets up what follows; at the end of a sentence it often implies a wish or regret (''it would be nice if...'').","watch_out":"For a guess about the past use -았/었을 텐데 (지금쯤 도착했을 텐데 ''He must have arrived by now''). Always write 텐데 with a space (좋을 텐데). For your own intention followed by a request, use -(으)ㄹ 테니까, not -(으)ㄹ 텐데.","examples":[{"ko":"내일 날씨가 좋으면 좋을 텐데.","en":"It would be nice if the weather were good tomorrow."},{"ko":"그는 지금쯤 집에 있을 텐데.","en":"He is probably at home by now."},{"ko":"시험이 어려웠을 텐데 다들 잘 봤네요.","en":"The exam must have been hard, but everyone did well."},{"ko":"피곤할 텐데 왜 계속 일해?","en":"You must be tired — why do you keep working?"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"내일 날씨가 좋을텐데.","en":"It would be nice tomorrow... (incorrect)","error_type":"Spacing: 텐데 is written separately","correction":"내일 날씨가 좋을 텐데."},{"ko":"지금쯤 집에 도착하는 텐데.","en":"He must be home by now. (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong modifier: 텐데 always takes -(으)ㄹ (or -았/었을)","correction":"지금쯤 집에 도착했을 텐데."}]},{"name":"(으)ㄹ 텐데 + 이유/설명 — Expectation with reason/context","level":"Intermediate","exp":"The -(으)ㄹ 텐데 clause states what the speaker assumes, and the second clause asks, suggests, or reacts in light of that assumption (often ''you must be..., but/so...'').","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ 텐데 + [reason/contrast clause]","when_to_use":"When contrasting what you expected with what actually happened; when asking for explanation; when expressing mild complaint or puzzlement","watch_out":"The assumption usually concerns the listener or a third party. The second clause is often a question, suggestion, or thanks: 바쁘실 텐데 와 주셔서 감사합니다.","examples":[{"ko":"비가 올 텐데 왜 우산을 안 가져왔어?","en":"It''s probably going to rain, so why didn''t you bring an umbrella?"},{"ko":"시간이 없을 텐데 어떻게 왔어?","en":"I expected you to be busy, but how did you come?"},{"ko":"힘들 텐데 혼자 하니?","en":"It must be hard — are you doing it all by yourself?"},{"ko":"바쁠 텐데 전화해 줘서 고마워.","en":"You must be busy — thanks for calling."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"바쁘을 텐데 와 줘서 고마워요.","en":"You must be busy — thanks for coming. (incorrect)","error_type":"Vowel stem takes -ㄹ 텐데, not -을","correction":"바쁠 텐데 와 줘서 고마워요."}]},{"name":"(으)ㄹ 텐데 — Softening a request or opinion","level":"Intermediate-Advanced","exp":"Used to soften statements, requests, or opinions by framing them as assumptions rather than direct statements. Creates a more polite, indirect, or tentative tone.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ 텐데 + [request/suggestion/opinion]","when_to_use":"When politely suggesting something; when making a gentle complaint; when giving indirect advice; when expressing an opinion without sounding too assertive","watch_out":"This usage is more indirect and polite—use when you want to be tactful; can sound hesitant if overused; context and intonation affect politeness level","examples":[{"ko":"이 방법이 더 쉬울 텐데.","en":"This way might be easier. (Indirect suggestion)"},{"ko":"일찍 자는 게 좋을 텐데.","en":"It would be good if you went to bed early. (Gentle advice)"},{"ko":"너도 좋아할 텐데.","en":"I think you would like it too. (Indirect statement)"},{"ko":"가는 게 좋을 텐데 왜 안 가?","en":"Wouldn''t it be better to go? (Polite question/suggestion)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"제가 도와줄 텐데 걱정하지 마세요.","en":"Intended: I''ll help you, so don''t worry. (incorrect)","error_type":"Speaker''s own promise + request needs -(으)ㄹ 테니까; -(으)ㄹ 텐데 states an assumption","correction":"제가 도와줄 테니까 걱정하지 마세요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '16509007-67ea-4640-8269-e16b0d76449f' and md5(patterns_json) = '683e5344d2d51696fede175e06daffd8';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)ㄹ까요? — Shall we?","level":"Beginner","exp":"This pattern is used to make polite suggestions or ask for agreement to do something together. It expresses the speaker''s intention to include the listener in an action.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ까요?\n- Vowel stem: -ㄹ까요? (갈까요?)\n- Consonant stem: -을까요? (먹을까요?)\n- ㄹ stem: just add -까요? (만들까요?)\n- Irregulars: 듣다 → 들을까요?, 걷다 → 걸을까요?, 돕다 → 도울까요?","when_to_use":"When suggesting an activity to someone, asking ''shall we...?'' in a polite way, or proposing to do something together. Common in everyday conversation for making plans or suggestions.","watch_out":"-(으)ㄹ까요? asks the listener''s opinion (''shall we?''); -(으)ㄹ래요? asks what they want and is a bit more casual; -(으)ㅂ시다 is ''let''s'' (not used to superiors). -(으)ㄹ까? without 요 is casual (friends).","examples":[{"ko":"우리 영화를 볼까요?","en":"Shall we watch a movie?"},{"ko":"내일 공원에 갈까요?","en":"Shall we go to the park tomorrow?"},{"ko":"함께 밥을 먹을까요?","en":"Shall we eat together?"},{"ko":"이제 시작할까요?","en":"Shall we start now?"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"우리 영화를 보을까요?","en":"Shall we watch a movie? (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong consonant choice","correction":"우리 영화를 볼까요? (보 + ㄹ까요, not 보을까요)"},{"ko":"내일 공원에 가을까요?","en":"Shall we go to the park tomorrow? (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong consonant choice","correction":"내일 공원에 갈까요? (가 + ㄹ까요, not 가을까요)"},{"ko":"같이 음악을 듣을까요?","en":"Shall we listen to music together? (incorrect)","error_type":"ㄷ-irregular not applied: 듣다 → 들을까요","correction":"같이 음악을 들을까요?"}]},{"name":"(으)ㄹ까요? — Shall I...? / What should we...? (asking the listener''s opinion)","level":"Beginner","exp":"With ''I'' as the subject it offers to do something (''Shall I...?''); with a question word it asks the listener''s advice (''What/When should we...?'').","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ까요?\n- Same structural pattern as pattern 1, but different pragmatic function","when_to_use":"When asking ''What do you think we should do...?'' or ''Do you think...?'' Seeking the listener''s advice, opinion, or suggestion about a course of action.","watch_out":"Context determines whether it''s a suggestion (speaker proposing) or a question (speaker asking for opinion). Intonation and context are key. Don''t confuse with -ㄹ까? (wondering to oneself).","examples":[{"ko":"이 문제를 어떻게 풀까요?","en":"How do you think we should solve this problem?"},{"ko":"제가 창문을 열까요?","en":"Shall I open the window?"},{"ko":"언제쯤 갈까요?","en":"When do you think we should go?"},{"ko":"누가 할까요?","en":"Who do you think should do it?"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"이 문제를 어떻게 푸을까요?","en":"How do you think we should solve this problem? (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong vowel/consonant combination","correction":"이 문제를 어떻게 풀까요? (풀 + ㄹ까요)"},{"ko":"뭘 입을 까요?","en":"What do you think I should wear? (incorrect - spacing error)","error_type":"Spacing error","correction":"뭘 입을까요? (을까요 should be written together)"}]},{"name":"(으)ㄹ까요? — Wondering/Speculating (formal version of -(으)ㄹ까)","level":"Intermediate","exp":"-(으)ㄹ까요? can also express wondering or asking for the listener''s guess about something uncertain (''Do you think...?'', ''I wonder if...''). It is the polite form of -(으)ㄹ까?.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + (으)ㄹ까요? (future/present guess)\nVerb/Adjective stem + 았/었을까요? (guess about the past)","when_to_use":"When expressing curiosity, speculation, or wondering about an outcome; when thinking aloud about possibilities in a polite or formal context; when you''re uncertain and questioning yourself or others rhetorically.","watch_out":"When ANSWERING a guess question, use -(으)ㄹ 거예요 or -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요, not -(으)ㄹ까요. For something that may already have happened, use -았/었을까요?","examples":[{"ko":"그 사람이 올까요?","en":"Do you think he''ll come?"},{"ko":"날씨가 좋을까요?","en":"I wonder if the weather will be nice?"},{"ko":"시험에 합격할까요?","en":"I wonder if I''ll pass the exam?"},{"ko":"이게 맞을까요?","en":"I wonder if this is correct?"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"A: 내일 비가 올까요? B: 네, 올까요.","en":"A: Do you think it''ll rain tomorrow? B: Yes, it will. (incorrect)","error_type":"-(으)ㄹ까요 used in an answer; answer with -(으)ㄹ 거예요","correction":"A: 내일 비가 올까요? B: 네, 올 거예요."},{"ko":"그 사람이 벌써 도착할까요?","en":"Do you think he has already arrived? (incorrect)","error_type":"Guess about a completed event needs -았/었을까요","correction":"그 사람이 벌써 도착했을까요?"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '87de5045-c985-4f2a-b9f5-335aaca679df' and md5(patterns_json) = '4af875a908bf28ac3b042cfd53030fd8';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)ㄹ래요 — Shall/Want to","level":"A2","exp":"Expresses the speaker''s intention, suggestion, or desire to do something. Used for first-person statements and polite suggestions to second person.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ래요\n- Vowel stem: -ㄹ래요 (갈래요)\n- Consonant stem: -을래요 (먹을래요)\n- ㄹ stem: just add -래요 (만들래요, 놀래요)\n- Irregulars: 듣다 → 들을래요, 걷다 → 걸을래요","when_to_use":"1) To say what you want / have decided to do (I)\n2) To ask what the listener wants to do (you?)\n3) To make a friendly suggestion or invitation (Do you want to...?)\n4) Friendly, casual-polite tone; with superiors -(으)시겠어요? is safer","watch_out":"1) Only for ''I'' in statements and ''you'' in questions — not for third persons (use -고 싶어 해요 / -(으)려고 해요)\n2) No past tense form (×갔을래요); use -고 싶었어요\n3) -(으)ㄹ래 (no 요) is casual speech; -(으)ㄹ래요 is polite. Don''t confuse with quoted -래요 (간대요, 가래요)\n4) Not for objective statements or predictions","examples":[{"ko":"저는 오늘 집에서 쉴래요.","en":"I want to rest at home today."},{"ko":"영화 볼래요?","en":"Do you want to watch a movie?"},{"ko":"우리 내일 만날래요?","en":"Shall we meet tomorrow?"},{"ko":"저는 커피를 마실래요.","en":"I want to drink coffee."},{"ko":"뭘 먹을래요?","en":"What do you want to eat?"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"그는 책을 읽을래요.","en":"He wants to read a book. (incorrect)","error_type":"Third-person subject","correction":"그는 책을 읽고 싶어 해요."},{"ko":"저는 어제 갔을래요.","en":"I wanted to go yesterday. (WRONG — cannot express past)","error_type":"Incorrect tense usage","correction":"저는 어제 가고 싶었어요. (I wanted to go yesterday.)"},{"ko":"저는 음악을 듣을래요.","en":"I want to listen to music. (incorrect)","error_type":"ㄷ-irregular not applied: 듣다 → 들을래요","correction":"저는 음악을 들을래요."}]},{"name":"-(으)ㄹ래(요) — Willingness/Preference","level":"A2","exp":"Indicates willingness or preference between options. Often used in negative form -지 않을래요 (don''t want to) to express refusal or reluctance.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)ㄹ래요 (I''ll / I want to)\n안 + Verb + (으)ㄹ래요 or Verb stem + 지 않을래요 (I don''t want to)\nQuestion: Verb stem + 지 않을래요? = ''Won''t you...?'' (invitation)","when_to_use":"1) To show preference or willingness\n2) To refuse politely with -지 않을래요\n3) To express determination or decision\n4) In context of offering choices","watch_out":"1) 안 할래요 is the everyday way to refuse; 하지 않을래요 is a bit more deliberate\n2) As a question, -지 않을래요? is a polite INVITATION (''Would you like to...?''), not a refusal: 같이 가지 않을래요?\n3) Repeated refusals with -래요 can sound stubborn\n4) First person (statements) / second person (questions) only","examples":[{"ko":"저는 이거 안 할래요.","en":"I don''t want to do this."},{"ko":"어느 걸로 할래요?","en":"Which one will you have?"},{"ko":"저는 버스를 탈래요. 택시는 안 탈래요.","en":"I''ll take the bus. I don''t want to take a taxi."},{"ko":"이번에는 제가 낼래요!","en":"This time I''ll pay!"},{"ko":"주말에 같이 영화 보지 않을래요?","en":"Would you like to see a movie together this weekend?"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"그 사람은 밥을 먹을래요.","en":"That person wants to eat rice. (WRONG — third person)","error_type":"Third person usage error","correction":"그 사람은 밥을 먹고 싶어 해요. (That person wants to eat rice.)"}]},{"name":"-(으)ㄹ래요 vs -(으)려고 해요 — Intention Nuance","level":"B1","exp":"Contrasts immediate willingness/choice (-ㄹ래요) with planned intention/purpose (-(으)려고 해요). Both express intention but with different nuances and contexts.","structure":"- (으)ㄹ래요: Verb + -ㄹ래요 (spontaneous choice)\n- -(으)려고 해요: Verb + -(으)려고 해요 (planned intention)","when_to_use":"-ㄹ래요: Immediate decision, preference, or casual announcement\n-(으)려고 해요: Pre-planned intention, purpose, or reason\nBoth can express willingness but -래요 is more personal/spontaneous","watch_out":"1) -(으)ㄹ래요 sounds decisive and personal (''I''ll...'')\n2) -(으)려고 해요 states a plan or intention (''I''m planning to...'')\n3) -(으)려고 해요 works for any subject; -(으)ㄹ래요 only for I (statement) / you (question)\n4) In questions: -(으)ㄹ래요? asks what someone wants; -(으)려고 해요? asks about their plans","examples":[{"ko":"저는 집에 갈래요. (vs) 저는 집에 가려고 해요.","en":"I want to go home. (immediate choice) vs I''m going to go home. (planned)"},{"ko":"뭘 할래요? (vs) 뭘 하려고 해요?","en":"What do you want to do? (casual choice) vs What are you planning to do? (about plans)"},{"ko":"피자를 먹을래요. (vs) 피자를 먹으려고 해요.","en":"I want to eat pizza. (right now) vs I''m going to eat pizza. (plan/intention)"},{"ko":"이 영화를 볼래요.","en":"I want to watch this movie. (immediate choice)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"제 친구는 내일 서울에 갈래요.","en":"My friend wants to go to Seoul tomorrow. (incorrect)","error_type":"-(으)ㄹ래요 used for a third person; use -(으)려고 해요 or -고 싶어 해요","correction":"제 친구는 내일 서울에 가려고 해요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '3de80621-98d7-4033-94cd-0b497199201d' and md5(patterns_json) = '55cefd66eb8d394547b549c8e2aef91d';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)려고 — In order to","level":"Intermediate (A2-B1)","exp":"Expresses intention or purpose - what someone does or will do in order to achieve a goal","structure":"Verb stem + (으)려고 + main clause\n\n- Vowel stem or ㄹ stem: -려고 (가다 → 가려고, 만들다 → 만들려고)\n- Consonant stem: -으려고 (먹다 → 먹으려고)\n- Irregulars: 듣다 → 들으려고, 돕다 → 도우려고","when_to_use":"When explaining the purpose or intention behind an action; what you plan to do or what someone does to accomplish something","watch_out":"- Only with action verbs (for adjectives use -아/어지려고: 건강해지려고)\n- The subject of both clauses is the same\n- No tense on the -(으)려고 verb itself — the final verb carries the tense (만나려고 나갔어요, not 만났으려고)\n- The main clause can''t be a command or suggestion: use -(으)러 가다 or -(으)려면 instead\n- -(으)러 is only used with movement verbs (가다/오다/다니다)","examples":[{"ko":"영어를 배우려고 학원에 등록했어요.","en":"I signed up at an academy (hagwon) to learn English."},{"ko":"시험에 합격하려고 매일 공부해요.","en":"In order to pass the exam, I study every day."},{"ko":"건강해지려고 운동을 시작했어요.","en":"To become healthy, I started exercising."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"행복하려고 많이 웃어요.","en":"I laugh a lot in order to be happy. (incorrect)","error_type":"Adjective + -(으)려고","correction":"행복해지려고 많이 웃어요. (Change the adjective to -아/어지다)"},{"ko":"점심을 먹으려고 식당에 갈까요?","en":"Shall we go to a restaurant to eat lunch? (incorrect)","error_type":"-(으)려고 can''t be followed by a suggestion or command","correction":"점심을 먹으러 식당에 갈까요?"},{"ko":"친구를 만났으려고 일찍 나갔어요.","en":"I went out early to meet a friend. (incorrect)","error_type":"Tense added to the -(으)려고 verb","correction":"친구를 만나려고 일찍 나갔어요."}]},{"name":"-(으)려고 하다 — To intend to/try to","level":"Intermediate (A2-B1)","exp":"Expresses an intention or plan (''I''m going to / I intend to''). In the past it often implies the plan didn''t work out. With things or nature as the subject it means ''about to'' (비가 오려고 해요 ''It''s about to rain'').","structure":"Verb stem + (으)려고 하다\n하다 carries the tense: -(으)려고 해요 (plan), -(으)려고 했어요 (intended / tried), -(으)려고 하는데 (I''m trying to, but...)","when_to_use":"When expressing an intention, attempt, or plan; softer than stating a direct goal; commonly used in both spoken and written Korean","watch_out":"- -(으)려고 stays fixed; only 하다 conjugates\n- -(으)려고 했어요 often suggests the plan failed or changed\n- Spelling: 만나려고, not 만날려고 (adding ㄹ is a common spoken but nonstandard form)","examples":[{"ko":"일찍 자려고 하는데 자꾸 잠이 안 와요.","en":"I''m trying to sleep early, but I can''t fall asleep."},{"ko":"담배를 끊으려고 했어요.","en":"I tried to quit smoking (past attempt)."},{"ko":"이번 주말에는 집에서 쉬려고 해요.","en":"I''m planning to rest at home this weekend."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"내일 친구를 만날려고 해요.","en":"I''m planning to meet a friend tomorrow. (incorrect)","error_type":"Nonstandard extra ㄹ (vowel stems take -려고 directly)","correction":"내일 친구를 만나려고 해요."},{"ko":"더 아름답으려고 해요.","en":"I''m trying to be more beautiful. (incorrect)","error_type":"Adjective + -(으)려고 하다","correction":"더 아름다워지려고 해요. (Change the adjective to -아/어지다)"}]},{"name":"-(으)려던 — Was going to/intended to (retrospective)","level":"Upper-Intermediate (B1-B2)","exp":"Expresses a past intention that was not completed; something you planned to do but didn''t","structure":"Verb stem + (으)려던 + noun\nCommon: -(으)려던 참에/참이었다 (just as I was about to), -(으)려던 계획/일","when_to_use":"When describing an incomplete past intention; explaining what you were about to do when something interrupted; creates a narrative of unfulfilled plans","watch_out":"- Always refers to past incomplete action\n- -(으)려던 is attributive form (cannot stand alone)\n- Often used with expressions of interruption or prevention\n- Different from simple past tense -(으)려고 했다","examples":[{"ko":"전화를 걸려던 참에 친구가 연락했어요.","en":"Just as I was about to call, my friend contacted me."},{"ko":"그 영화를 보려던 차에 표가 매진됐어요.","en":"When I was intending to watch that movie, it sold out."},{"ko":"나가려던 참에 비가 내리기 시작했어요.","en":"Just as I was about to go out, it started raining."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"전화를 걸으려던 참에 친구가 연락했어요.","en":"Just as I was about to call, my friend contacted me. (incorrect)","error_type":"ㄹ-stem 걸다 takes -려던, no 으","correction":"전화를 걸려던 참에 친구가 연락했어요."},{"ko":"내일 여행을 가려던 계획이에요.","en":"It''s my plan to travel tomorrow. (incorrect)","error_type":"Retrospective -(으)려던 used for a current future plan","correction":"내일 여행을 갈 계획이에요. / 내일 여행을 가려고 해요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'd8a5a758-dc2c-4c11-96bb-1b6bc4db5e3d' and md5(patterns_json) = 'd5e298a4ce4bd57472185db35c04baed';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)면 — If/When","level":"Beginner","exp":"This is a conditional ending that expresses ''if'' or ''when'' something happens. It shows a cause-and-effect relationship where the second clause depends on the condition in the first clause.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + (으)면\n- Vowel stem: -면 (가면, 크면)\n- Consonant stem: -으면 (먹으면)\n- ㄹ stem: keep ㄹ and add -면 (살면, 만들면)\n- Irregulars: 덥다 → 더우면, 듣다 → 들으면\n- Noun: (이)면 (학생이면)","when_to_use":"When you want to express conditional situations, hypothetical scenarios, or general ''if/when'' statements in everyday conversation","watch_out":"-(으)면 is also used with -았/었- : for a completed condition (다 먹었으면 가자 ''If you''ve finished eating, let''s go'') and in wishes/hypotheticals (돈이 많았으면 좋겠어요). Watch the ㅂ- and ㄷ-irregulars.","examples":[{"ko":"비가 오면 집에서 쉬어요.","en":"If it rains, I rest at home."},{"ko":"옷이 너무 크면 바꿔 드릴게요.","en":"If the clothes are too big, we''ll exchange them for you."},{"ko":"시간이 있으면 영화를 봐요.","en":"When I have time, I watch movies."},{"ko":"따뜻한 물을 마시면 목이 좀 나아질 거예요.","en":"If you drink warm water, your throat will feel better."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"날씨가 덥으면 에어컨을 켜요.","en":"If it''s hot, I turn on the air conditioner. (incorrect)","error_type":"ㅂ-irregular not applied: 덥다 → 더우면","correction":"날씨가 더우면 에어컨을 켜요."},{"ko":"시간이 있으면에 영화를 봐요.","en":"When I have time, I watch movies. (extra particle)","error_type":"Extra particle","correction":"시간이 있으면 영화를 봐요. (Remove 에)"},{"ko":"옷이 너무 크으면 바꿔 드릴게요.","en":"If the clothes are too big, we''ll exchange them for you. (incorrect)","error_type":"Vowel stem takes -면, not -으면","correction":"옷이 너무 크면 바꿔 드릴게요."}]},{"name":"(으)면서 — While/As","level":"Intermediate","exp":"This ending expresses simultaneous actions or concurrent situations. It shows that two actions happen at the same time, often with a cause-and-effect nuance or describing accompanying circumstances.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)면서\n- Vowel stem: -면서 (보면서)\n- Consonant stem: -으면서 (먹으면서)\n- ㄹ stem: keep ㄹ and add -면서 (살면서, 만들면서)","when_to_use":"When describing two actions happening simultaneously, or when one action accompanies another; often used when discussing habitual or repeated simultaneous actions","watch_out":"Don''t confuse with -(으)면 (if). Both actions must have the same subject; for different subjects use -는 동안. In this ''while'' meaning there is no tense on the -(으)면서 verb (the last verb shows tense); -았/었으면서 appears only in the ''although / accusing'' use (알았으면서 왜 말 안 했어요?).","examples":[{"ko":"음악을 들으면서 공부해요.","en":"I study while listening to music."},{"ko":"웃으면서 말했어요.","en":"He spoke while smiling."},{"ko":"운전하면서 전화하지 마세요.","en":"Don''t talk on the phone while driving."},{"ko":"밥을 먹으면서 TV를 봐요.","en":"I watch TV while eating."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"음악을 들으면 공부해요.","en":"If I listen to music, I study. (wrong meaning)","error_type":"Wrong conditional form","correction":"음악을 들으면서 공부해요. (Use -으면서 for simultaneous action)"},{"ko":"제가 운전하면서 친구가 전화했어요.","en":"While I was driving, my friend called. (incorrect)","error_type":"Different subjects: -(으)면서 needs the same subject; use -는 동안","correction":"제가 운전하는 동안 친구가 전화했어요."}]},{"name":"(으)려면 — In order to/If you want to","level":"Intermediate","exp":"This ending combines 려 (intention) with 면 (conditional) to express the condition or requirement needed to achieve a goal. It shows what must happen for a desired outcome to occur.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)려면\n- Vowel stem: -려면 (가려면)\n- Consonant stem: -으려면 (받으려면)\n- ㄹ stem: keep ㄹ and add -려면 (만들려면, 살려면)","when_to_use":"When explaining prerequisites, requirements, or conditions necessary for achieving a goal; commonly used for advice, instructions, or explaining what''s needed","watch_out":"Used with action verbs; for adjectives use -아/어지려면 (건강해지려면). The main clause states what is needed, usually with -아/어야 해요/돼요, -(으)세요, or -는 게 좋아요.","examples":[{"ko":"성공하려면 열심히 공부해야 해요.","en":"If you want to succeed, you have to study hard."},{"ko":"좋은 성적을 받으려면 숙제를 다 해야 해요.","en":"In order to get good grades, you must do all your homework."},{"ko":"한국어를 잘하려면 매일 연습해야 해요.","en":"To speak Korean well, you must practice every day."},{"ko":"건강해지려면 운동을 해야 해요.","en":"To get healthy, you need to exercise."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"성공하면 열심히 공부해야 해요.","en":"If I succeed, I must study hard. (wrong logic)","error_type":"Wrong condition type","correction":"성공하려면 열심히 공부해야 해요. (Use -으려면 for intention)"},{"ko":"좋으려면 숙제를 해야 해요.","en":"To be good, I must do homework. (adjective misuse)","error_type":"Using with adjective","correction":"Use with action verbs only: 좋은 성적을 받으려면 숙제를 해야 해요."},{"ko":"한국어를 잘하려면 매일 연습했어요.","en":"To be good at Korean, I practiced every day. (incorrect)","error_type":"Main clause must state a requirement or advice, not a past fact","correction":"한국어를 잘하려면 매일 연습해야 해요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '96fd1e1c-2f48-4f13-b907-7a13a61ccc72' and md5(patterns_json) = '20b4e8018543e1fd9c02b5d713058a70';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)면서 — While doing","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Expresses simultaneous actions or contrasting situations occurring at the same time. The subject performs two actions concurrently or describes a state while another action happens.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)면서\n- Vowel stem: -면서 (보면서)\n- Consonant stem: -으면서 (먹으면서)\n- ㄹ stem: keep ㄹ and add -면서 (살면서, 만들면서)","when_to_use":"When describing two actions happening simultaneously, or expressing a contradiction between actions/states","watch_out":"Both actions share the same subject (for different subjects use -는 동안). Don''t confuse with -(으)면 (if). In the ''while'' meaning there is no tense on the -(으)면서 verb — the final verb shows the tense. It can also mean ''although'' (알면서 모른 척해요), and only in that use can it take -았/었- (알았으면서 왜 말 안 했어요?).","examples":[{"ko":"음악을 들으면서 공부해요.","en":"I study while listening to music."},{"ko":"웃으면서 눈물을 흘렸어요.","en":"She smiled through her tears."},{"ko":"걸으면서 전화해요.","en":"I talk on the phone while walking."},{"ko":"운전하면서 음악을 들어요.","en":"I listen to music while driving."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"음악을 들으면 공부해요.","en":"Intended: I study while listening to music. (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong ending: -(으)면 means ''if'', not ''while''","correction":"음악을 들으면서 공부해요."},{"ko":"제가 요리하면서 동생이 청소했어요.","en":"While I cooked, my sibling cleaned. (incorrect)","error_type":"Different subjects with -(으)면서","correction":"제가 요리하는 동안 동생이 청소했어요."},{"ko":"어제 공부했으면서 음악을 들었어요.","en":"Yesterday I listened to music while studying. (incorrect)","error_type":"Past tense on the -(으)면서 verb","correction":"어제 공부하면서 음악을 들었어요."}]},{"name":"(으)면서도 — While doing AND/Despite doing","level":"Intermediate-Advanced","exp":"Indicates simultaneous actions with emphasis on contradiction or unexpected contrast. Means ''while doing'' with the nuance of ''even though'' or ''despite.''","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + (으)면서도\n- Vowel stem: -면서도\n- Consonant stem: -으면서도\n- ㄹ stem: keep ㄹ (알면서도, 살면서도)","when_to_use":"When emphasizing the contrast or unexpected nature of simultaneous actions or conditions","watch_out":"Stronger contrast than -(으)면서 (which can also mean ''although''). Common with 알다, 좋아하다, 미안하다. The same-subject rule still applies.","examples":[{"ko":"알면서도 말하지 않았어요.","en":"Despite knowing, I didn''t tell (anyone)."},{"ko":"피곤하면서도 계속 일했어요.","en":"While being tired, he continued working (despite fatigue)."},{"ko":"좋아하면서도 표현을 안 해요.","en":"She likes it but doesn''t express it (despite liking)."},{"ko":"웃으면서도 마음은 슬펐어요.","en":"While smiling, her heart was sad inside."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"알으면서도 말하지 않았어요.","en":"Even though I knew, I didn''t say anything. (incorrect)","error_type":"ㄹ-stem: no 으 (알다 → 알면서도)","correction":"알면서도 말하지 않았어요."}]},{"name":"N(이)면서 — While being/As a","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Used with nouns to express simultaneous states or dual roles. Describes a noun serving multiple purposes or existing in multiple states at the same time.","structure":"Noun + (이)면서\n- Consonant-ending noun: 이면서 (학생이면서)\n- Vowel-ending noun: 면서 or 이면서 (가수면서 / 가수이면서)","when_to_use":"When describing someone/something that has multiple roles or exists in multiple states simultaneously","watch_out":"This is noun form, not verb form. Don''t mix up with verb conjugation. The noun can refer to role, status, or characteristic.","examples":[{"ko":"그는 학생이면서 직장인이에요.","en":"He is a student while being (also) a working person."},{"ko":"그분은 엄마이면서 배우예요.","en":"She is a mother and also an actress."},{"ko":"가수이면서 작곡가이기도 해요.","en":"He is a singer while also being a composer."},{"ko":"친구이면서 경쟁자예요.","en":"They are friends while being competitors."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"그는 학생면서 직장인이에요.","en":"He is a student and also an office worker. (incorrect)","error_type":"Missing 이 after a consonant-ending noun","correction":"그는 학생이면서 직장인이에요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '8d1781ab-a48d-4186-b8ac-4a990d7f1202' and md5(patterns_json) = '6f441fc246388128a8393119f57fb8fe';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(으)세요 — Honorific imperative","level":"A2","exp":"Polite command form used to respectfully request or instruct someone of higher status or to show politeness in general situations.","structure":"Verb stem + (으)세요\n- Consonant stem: -으세요 (앉으세요)\n- Vowel stem: -세요 (가세요)\n- ㄹ stem: ㄹ drops + -세요 (만들다 → 만드세요, 열다 → 여세요)\n- Special honorific verbs: 먹다/마시다 → 드세요, 자다 → 주무세요, 있다 → 계세요","when_to_use":"When giving instructions, requests, or commands to someone older, higher in status, or in formal situations; shows respect while maintaining directive function","watch_out":"The same -(으)세요 form is also an honorific statement or question (어디 가세요? ''Where are you going?'') — context and intonation decide. Adjectives can''t be commands: say 조용히 하세요, not 조용하세요. Watch the irregulars (ㄹ, ㄷ, ㅂ, ㅅ). 해요 style (앉아요) can also be a polite request, but -(으)세요 is more respectful.","examples":[{"ko":"커피를 마시세요.","en":"Please drink the coffee. (to someone respected)"},{"ko":"여기에 앉으세요.","en":"Please sit here. (respectful request)"},{"ko":"천천히 걸으세요.","en":"Please walk slowly. (giving polite instruction)"},{"ko":"이 책을 읽으세요.","en":"Please read this book. (respectful directive)"},{"ko":"물 좀 드세요.","en":"Please have some water. (드시다: honorific of 먹다/마시다)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"앉아세요.","en":"Please sit. (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong form: consonant stem takes -으세요","correction":"앉으세요."},{"ko":"도서관에서는 조용하세요.","en":"Please be quiet in the library. (incorrect)","error_type":"Adjective used as a command; use 조용히 하세요","correction":"도서관에서는 조용히 하세요."}]},{"name":"Irregular verbs with (으)세요","level":"A2","exp":"Several stem types change before -(으)세요. Regular stems just follow the vowel/consonant rule (닫다 → 닫으세요, 받다 → 받으세요).","structure":"Irregular stem + (으)세요\n- ㄹ stem: ㄹ drops + -세요 (만들다 → 만드세요, 살다 → 사세요)\n- ㄷ-irregular: ㄷ → ㄹ + -으세요 (듣다 → 들으세요, 걷다 → 걸으세요)\n- ㅂ-irregular: ㅂ → 우 + -세요 (돕다 → 도우세요, 굽다 → 구우세요)\n- ㅅ-irregular: ㅅ drops + -으세요 (짓다 → 지으세요, 낫다 → 나으세요)","when_to_use":"Whenever you give a polite instruction with one of these verbs.","watch_out":"Not every verb ending in ㄷ, ㅂ or ㅅ is irregular: 닫다 → 닫으세요, 받다 → 받으세요, 입다 → 입으세요, 씻다 → 씻으세요 are regular. For ''please help me'', 도와주세요 is far more common than 도우세요.","examples":[{"ko":"천천히 걸으세요.","en":"Please walk slowly. (걷다: ㄷ-irregular → 걸으세요)"},{"ko":"문을 닫으세요.","en":"Please close the door. (닫다: regular → 닫으세요)"},{"ko":"저 좀 도와주세요.","en":"Please help me. (돕다 → 도와 + 주세요)"},{"ko":"이 노래를 들어 보세요.","en":"Try listening to this song. (듣다: ㄷ-irregular → 들어 보세요)"},{"ko":"케이크를 직접 만드세요?","en":"Do you make the cake yourself? (만들다: ㄹ drops → 만드세요)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"천천히 걷으세요.","en":"Please walk slowly. (incorrect)","error_type":"ㄷ-irregular not applied","correction":"천천히 걸으세요. (걷다 → 걸으세요)"},{"ko":"이 음악을 듣으세요.","en":"Please listen to this music. (incorrect)","error_type":"ㄷ-irregular not applied","correction":"이 음악을 들으세요."},{"ko":"빵을 만들으세요.","en":"Please make bread. (incorrect)","error_type":"ㄹ-stem: ㄹ drops before -세요, no 으","correction":"빵을 만드세요."}]},{"name":"(으)세요 as an honorific question, and -지 마세요","level":"A2","exp":"With rising intonation, -(으)세요? is a polite honorific question about the listener (''Are you...? / Do you...?''). For negative commands (''please don''t''), use -지 마세요.","structure":"Honorific question: Verb stem + (으)세요? (어디 가세요?)\nNegative command: Verb stem + -지 마세요 (가지 마세요)\nVery formal: -지 마십시오","when_to_use":"Use -(으)세요? to ask respectfully about someone''s actions or state; use -지 마세요 to tell someone politely NOT to do something.","watch_out":"안 + -(으)세요 is a statement or question (''You aren''t...?''), not a prohibition — for ''please don''t'' always use -지 마세요. 말다 + -세요 is 마세요 (not 마시세요).","examples":[{"ko":"주말에 뭐 하세요?","en":"What do you do on weekends? (honorific question)"},{"ko":"담배를 피우지 마세요.","en":"Please don''t smoke. (Negative honorific imperative)"},{"ko":"늦게 자지 마세요.","en":"Please don''t go to bed late. (Respectful negative command)"},{"ko":"여기서 큰 소리를 내지 마세요.","en":"Please don''t make loud noises here. (Polite prohibition)"},{"ko":"혼자 가지 마세요.","en":"Please don''t go alone. (Caring negative directive)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"여기서 사진을 안 찍으세요.","en":"Please don''t take photos here. (incorrect)","error_type":"안 + -(으)세요 is not a prohibition; use -지 마세요","correction":"여기서 사진을 찍지 마세요."},{"ko":"가지 마시세요.","en":"Don''t go (double honorific attempt)","error_type":"Over-honorification","correction":"가지 마세요. (Use -지 마세요, not double -시-)"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '64453410-4c62-4544-bb8a-408f703ff7a9' and md5(patterns_json) = 'cf22926e867e84485972ec73c93acade';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(이)나 — Or / As many as","level":"Intermediate","exp":"A particle used to indicate alternatives or to express a quantity that is surprisingly large or noteworthy","structure":"Noun + (이)나 / Noun + (이)나 Noun + (이)나","when_to_use":"When presenting alternative choices or when emphasizing an unexpectedly large amount/number","watch_out":"Use 이나 after consonants and 나 after vowels. The ''as many as'' meaning comes after numbers/amounts and shows surprise. Don''t confuse with (이)나마 (''at least / even if only'') or (이)라도 (''even, at least'').","examples":[{"ko":"커피나 차 중에 뭘 드시겠어요?","en":"Would you like coffee or tea?"},{"ko":"100명이나 왔어요!","en":"As many as 100 people came!"},{"ko":"버스나 택시로 갈 수 있어요.","en":"You can go by bus or taxi."},{"ko":"5시간이나 기다렸어요.","en":"I waited as long as 5 hours."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"커피나 차를 중에 뭘 드시겠어요?","en":"Would you like coffee or tea?","error_type":"Extra object particle before 중에","correction":"커피나 차 중에 뭘 드시겠어요? (Remove 를 after 차)"},{"ko":"100명나 왔어요!","en":"As many as 100 people came!","error_type":"Particle selection","correction":"100명이나 왔어요! (Use 이나 after consonant 명)"},{"ko":"버스나 택시 갈 수 있어요.","en":"You can go by bus or taxi.","error_type":"Missing particle","correction":"버스나 택시로 갈 수 있어요. (Add 로 particle for ''by'')"}]},{"name":"(이)나마 — At least / Even if only","level":"Intermediate","exp":"(이)나마 means ''even if only / at least'': the thing is not much, but the speaker is grateful or satisfied with it. It is somewhat formal and common in set phrases.","structure":"Noun + (이)나마 (조금이나마, 잠시나마)\nAdverb + 나마 (늦게나마)","when_to_use":"When acknowledging something is limited but better than nothing; expressing a consolation or minimum satisfaction","watch_out":"Different from (이)나 ''as many as'' (which stresses a large amount). (이)나마 is formal/written; in everyday speech (이)라도 is more common (물이라도 마시세요). Use 이나마 after consonants and 나마 after vowels.","examples":[{"ko":"늦게나마 생일 축하드려요.","en":"Happy belated birthday. (lit. even if late)"},{"ko":"조금이나마 도움이 되었으면 좋겠어요.","en":"I hope this helps, even if only a little."},{"ko":"잠시나마 쉴 수 있어서 다행이에요.","en":"I''m glad I could rest, even if only for a moment."},{"ko":"한두 시간이나마 쉴 수 있어서 좋았어요.","en":"It was nice to rest, even if only for an hour or two."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"조금나마 도움이 되었으면 좋겠어요.","en":"I hope this helps, even if only a little. (incorrect)","error_type":"Consonant-ending word needs 이나마","correction":"조금이나마 도움이 되었으면 좋겠어요."},{"ko":"한두 시간이나 쉴 수 있어서 좋았어요.","en":"Intended: It was nice to rest, even if only for an hour or two. (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong meaning: (이)나 after an amount means ''as much as'', the opposite nuance","correction":"한두 시간이나마 쉴 수 있어서 좋았어요."}]},{"name":"(이)나 — ''...or something'' / just (settling for an option)","level":"Intermediate","exp":"(이)나 after a noun can mean ''...or something (like that)'' or ''just'', presenting the option casually or as a modest second-best choice. It is very common in invitations and suggestions.","structure":"Noun + (이)나 + verb (often -(으)ㄹ까요?, -(으)ㄹ래요?, -자)\n커피나, 영화나, 잠이나","when_to_use":"For casual suggestions (''want to grab coffee or something?'') or when you settle for something simple because nothing better is available.","watch_out":"Because it downplays the noun, it can sound dismissive when the thing is important or a gift: use 을/를 instead (이 선물을 받아 주세요). It replaces 을/를 and 이/가 — don''t add them.","examples":[{"ko":"심심한데 영화나 볼까요?","en":"I''m bored — shall we watch a movie or something?"},{"ko":"커피나 한잔할래요?","en":"Want to grab a coffee or something?"},{"ko":"주말에는 집에서 잠이나 잘래요.","en":"This weekend I''ll just sleep at home."},{"ko":"시간 있으면 밥이나 먹자.","en":"If you have time, let''s grab a bite."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"주말에는 집에서 잠나 잘래요.","en":"This weekend I''ll just sleep at home. (incorrect)","error_type":"Consonant-ending noun needs 이나","correction":"주말에는 집에서 잠이나 잘래요."},{"ko":"커피를나 한잔할래요?","en":"Want to grab a coffee or something? (incorrect)","error_type":"(이)나 replaces the object particle — don''t stack them","correction":"커피나 한잔할래요?"},{"ko":"생일 선물로 이 반지나 받으세요.","en":"Please accept this ring as a birthday present. (incorrect)","error_type":"Nuance: (이)나 downplays the ring and sounds dismissive","correction":"생일 선물로 이 반지를 받아 주세요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '3b3ee981-a2af-472c-a51c-48fa0c3900aa' and md5(patterns_json) = '669bfe78ff27df77e833ba911dff9650';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"(이)라서 — Because it is","level":"Beginner (A2)","exp":"This pattern gives a reason based on what something IS: Noun + 이다 + -아/어서. 이라서 follows consonant-ending nouns and 라서 follows vowel-ending nouns. (이)어서/여서 is an equivalent form.","structure":"Noun (consonant) + 이라서: 학생이라서\nNoun (vowel) + 라서: 의사라서\nNegative: Noun + 이/가 아니라서 (학생이 아니라서)","when_to_use":"Use this pattern when you want to explain why something happens because of what something IS (a noun). Common in casual and formal speech.","watch_out":"Only with nouns — verbs and adjectives use -아/어서 (좋아서, 바빠서). Like -아/어서, the main clause can''t be a command or suggestion: use (이)니까 there (주말이니까 같이 가요). Not used with past tense: 학생이었어서 ×.","examples":[{"ko":"오늘은 공휴일이라서 은행이 문을 닫았어요.","en":"Because today is a public holiday, the bank is closed."},{"ko":"제 친구는 의사라서 항상 바빠요.","en":"My friend is a doctor, so she''s always busy."},{"ko":"학생이라서 할인을 받았어요.","en":"Because I''m a student, I got a discount."},{"ko":"한국인이라서 한글을 알아요.","en":"Because I''m Korean, I know Hangul."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"날씨가 좋아라서 공원에 갔어요.","en":"Because the weather was nice, I went to the park. (incorrect)","error_type":"(이)라서 attached to an adjective; adjectives use -아/어서","correction":"날씨가 좋아서 공원에 갔어요."},{"ko":"학생라서 할인을 받았어요.","en":"Because I''m a student, I got a discount.","error_type":"Missing ''이'' after consonant","correction":"학생이라서 할인을 받았어요. — Add ''이'' after consonant-ending nouns."}]},{"name":"Noun + (이)라서 — Used in explanations and reasoning","level":"Beginner (A2)","exp":"This pattern specifically explains reasons in everyday conversations. It shows cause-and-effect relationships where the cause is the nature or identity of something (a noun).","structure":"Noun (with copula identity) + (이)라서 + consequential statement","when_to_use":"Use in casual conversations when explaining why something is the case because of its identity or category. Often found in excuses, explanations, or justifications.","watch_out":"Consonant-ending nouns add 이, vowel-ending nouns don''t. Don''t use it with verbs or adjectives (use -아/어서). Words like 피곤하다, 행복하다 are adjectives, not nouns: 피곤해서, not 피곤이라서.","examples":[{"ko":"초보자라서 실수가 많아요.","en":"Because I''m a beginner, I make a lot of mistakes."},{"ko":"외국인이라서 한국말이 어려워요.","en":"Because I''m a foreigner, Korean is difficult."},{"ko":"주말이라서 사람이 많네요.","en":"Because it''s the weekend, there are many people."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"어제 피곤이라서 일찍 잤어요.","en":"Because I was tired yesterday, I went to bed early. (incorrect)","error_type":"(이)라서 used where the adjective 피곤하다 is needed","correction":"어제 피곤해서 일찍 잤어요."},{"ko":"오늘 바빠라서 못 가요.","en":"I''m busy today, so I can''t go. (incorrect)","error_type":"(이)라서 attached to an adjective form","correction":"오늘 바빠서 못 가요."}]},{"name":"(이)라서 in formal vs. casual contexts","level":"Intermediate (B1)","exp":"While the basic form ''(이)라서'' works in both formal and casual speech, there are slight variations in politeness levels. Understanding when to use it in different contexts is important for natural communication.","structure":"Noun + (이)라서 + [verb/adjective] + [formal/casual ending]","when_to_use":"Use ''(이)라서'' in both formal documents, presentations, and casual daily conversations. The formality comes from the ending verb, not from ''(이)라서'' itself.","watch_out":"The pattern itself doesn''t change, but combine it with appropriate speech levels (존댓말, 반말, etc.). Don''t think ''(이)라서'' becomes more formal—it stays the same regardless of context.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 학생이라서 학비 할인 프로그램을 신청했습니다. (Formal/Written)","en":"Because I am a student, I applied for a tuition discount program."},{"ko":"방학이라서 학교에 안 가. (Casual/Spoken)","en":"It''s vacation, so I''m not going to school."},{"ko":"고객님은 VIP 회원이라서 배송비가 무료입니다. (Formal)","en":"Because you are a VIP member, shipping is free."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"오늘은 휴일이라서 같이 놀러 갑시다.","en":"It''s a holiday today, so let''s go out together. (incorrect)","error_type":"(이)라서 + suggestion; use (이)니까 before suggestions/commands","correction":"오늘은 휴일이니까 같이 놀러 갑시다."},{"ko":"학생라서 공부를 많이 해요.","en":"Because I''m a student, I study a lot.","error_type":"Missing ''이'' after consonant-ending noun","correction":"학생이라서 공부를 많이 해요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'aa3c1992-2c87-439c-90c4-775d4a323601' and md5(patterns_json) = '4c3e935c7a409579bc5ad31b04d5e0b1';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"저는 일주일에 세 번 운동을 해요.","en":"I exercise three times a week."},{"ko":"보통 아침에 조깅을 해요.","en":"I usually jog in the morning."},{"ko":"친구는 헬스장에서 자주 운동해요.","en":"My friend often works out at the gym."},{"ko":"운동 후에 먹는 피자는 정말 맛있어요.","en":"Pizza after a workout is really tasty."},{"ko":"우리는 모두 건강을 위해서 운동해요.","en":"We all exercise for our health."}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'a1710b55-d6fc-4553-888c-19b7d1272e03' and md5(patterns_json) = '146b97b698602373ad2d17805e731f09';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"안녕하세요, 게임 좋아해요?","en":"Hi, do you like games?"},{"ko":"네, 저는 매일 게임을 해요.","en":"Yes, I play games every day."},{"ko":"어떤 게임을 좋아해요?","en":"What kind of games do you like?"},{"ko":"저는 모바일 게임이 재미있어요.","en":"Mobile games are fun for me."},{"ko":"함께 게임을 할래요?","en":"Do you want to play games together?"}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'f48c687d-ba9e-4665-8689-7272dcf32e6f' and md5(patterns_json) = '0e0bb8eac65e3ace5bac898adb785f75';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"저는 서울에서 가족과 살아요.","en":"I live with my family in Seoul."},{"ko":"우리 집은 아파트예요.","en":"Our house is an apartment."},{"ko":"방이 세 개 있어요.","en":"There are three rooms."},{"ko":"엄마는 부엌에서 요리해요.","en":"Mom cooks in the kitchen."},{"ko":"저는 거실에서 가족과 시간을 보내요.","en":"I spend time with my family in the living room."}]', qa_at = now() where id = '0fcb15ee-8619-41b9-8fc2-1f981f54a413' and md5(patterns_json) = 'cd16cd37e583a42df6ca1751c52c7ec6';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"저희 가족을 소개할게요.","en":"Let me introduce my family."},{"ko":"엄마, 아빠, 오빠, 그리고 저예요.","en":"Mom, Dad, my older brother, and me."},{"ko":"그러니까 우리 가족은 네 명이에요.","en":"So our family has four people."},{"ko":"할머니도 함께 살고 있어요.","en":"Grandmother also lives with us."},{"ko":"그래서 모두 다섯 명이에요.","en":"So we are five people in total."}]', qa_at = now() where id = '9def31bd-5198-441c-b356-8c0e5aeede81' and md5(patterns_json) = 'df4e0816bcc86625d48f48a3d6e6629c';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"핸드폰 배경화면이 뭐예요?","en":"What is a phone wallpaper?"},{"ko":"배경화면은 핸드폰 화면에 보이는 바탕 사진이에요.","en":"A wallpaper is the background picture you see on your phone screen."},{"ko":"저는 예쁜 배경화면을 좋아해요.","en":"I like pretty wallpapers."},{"ko":"친구는 좋아하는 연예인 사진을 배경화면으로 해요.","en":"My friend sets a picture of her favorite celebrity as her wallpaper."},{"ko":"여러분도 마음에 드는 사진으로 바꿔 보세요.","en":"You should all try changing it to a photo you like."}]', qa_at = now() where id = '6cb97c02-71f4-414e-9233-35a98a9dbc2d' and md5(patterns_json) = 'd4be5d5cd861b5335d960dadd50fc1a4';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"오늘 한국어 수업에서 새 단어를 배워요.","en":"Today I learn new Korean words in class."},{"ko":"선생님이 ''커피''라는 단어를 가르쳐 줘요.","en":"The teacher teaches us the word ''coffee''."},{"ko":"저는 이 단어가 좋아요.","en":"I like this word."},{"ko":"친구도 함께 공부해요.","en":"My friend studies with me too."},{"ko":"우리는 내일 또 배울 거예요.","en":"We will learn again tomorrow."}]', qa_at = now() where id = '7bfbe067-9e1d-41e3-8099-fe25c8d1a7a7' and md5(patterns_json) = '3c1ebaaa6653e4809bba1942461f2bb6';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"저는 책 읽는 것을 좋아해요.","en":"I like reading books."},{"ko":"매일 저녁에 한 시간씩 읽어요.","en":"I read for one hour every evening."},{"ko":"지금 읽고 있는 책은 정말 재미있어요.","en":"The book I''m reading now is really interesting."},{"ko":"친구는 책 읽는 것을 별로 안 좋아해요.","en":"My friend doesn''t really like reading."},{"ko":"그래도 같이 도서관에 가기로 했어요.","en":"Still, we decided to go to the library together."}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'ca9a3ee8-c353-41bb-a3ad-fb4f76936e39' and md5(patterns_json) = '773e2c297414a6eea3ebe33781180156';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"explanation":"-지도 않다 means ''not even'' or ''doesn''t even'' and is used to emphasize the negation of an action or state. It combines the conjunctive ending -지 with the particle 도 (even) and the negation 않다.","questions":[{"q":"그는 물 한 잔 ___ 않았다.","choices":["마시지도","마셨지도","마시도"],"correct":0,"hint":"Use -지도 않다 form: verb stem + 지도 않다. ''마시지도 않았다'' = ''didn''t even drink''"},{"q":"Which sentence uses -지도 않다 correctly?","choices":["저는 밥을 먹지도 않습니다.","저는 밥을 먹도 않습니다.","저는 밥을 먹지 않습니다도."],"correct":0,"hint":"The correct form is verb stem + 지도 않다. Option A follows the proper word order and structure."},{"q":"그녀는 전화를 ___ 않았다.","choices":["걸지도","걸도","걸었지도"],"correct":0,"hint":"Verb stem + 지도 않다; the tense goes on 않다: 걸지도 않았다 = ''didn''t even call''."},{"q":"Identify the error: 아이들은 채소를 먹지 않도 했다.","choices":["Should be ''먹지도 않았다'' — 도 attaches to -지, before 않다","Should be ''먹도 않았다'' - remove the 지","The sentence is correct as written"],"correct":0,"hint":"도 must attach right after -지: 먹 + 지도 + 않았다. In the given sentence 도 is in the wrong place."},{"q":"선생님은 학생의 질문에 대답___ 않으셨다.","choices":["하지도","하도","했지도"],"correct":0,"hint":"Use -지도 않다 with verb stem 하: 하지도 않으셨다 = ''didn''t even answer''. Remember 않 changes to 않으 with -으셨다."}]}', qa_at = now() where id = 'd5ac3c81-ebcc-4c91-b870-d3a30492e9e4' and md5(patterns_json) = 'f9601d8711e18c5ca0325482e818077e';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"explanation":"~았어요/었어요 is the polite past tense ending. Use ~았어요 when the last vowel of the stem is ㅏ or ㅗ (갔어요, 봤어요), ~었어요 for other vowels (먹었어요), and 했어요 for 하다 verbs.","questions":[{"q":"어제 친구를 ___. (I met my friend yesterday.)","choices":["만났어요","만나요","만날래요"],"correct":0,"hint":"만나 (to meet) + ㅏ vowel = 만났어요 for past tense"},{"q":"Which sentence has the correct past tense form?","choices":["저는 밥을 먹었어요.","저는 밥을 먹아요.","저는 밥을 먹을 거예요."],"correct":0,"hint":"먹다 (to eat) + ㅓ vowel = 먹었어요 for completed past action"},{"q":"Choose the correct past tense ending: 그는 학교에 ___.","choices":["갔어요","가요","가겠어요"],"correct":0,"hint":"가다 (to go) + ㅏ vowel = 갔어요 for past tense"},{"q":"Identify the error: 저는 어제 책을 읽었어. (I read a book yesterday.)","choices":["읽었어요 should be used (저 is humble, so the ending must be polite)","읽었어 is correct","읽어야 해 should be used instead"],"correct":0,"hint":"~았어/었어 is casual speech (반말); with 저 you need the polite ~았어요/었어요."},{"q":"Complete: 엄마가 저를 학교에 ___.","choices":["데려갔어요","데려가요","데려갈 거예요"],"correct":0,"hint":"데려가다 (to take someone) + ㅏ vowel = 데려갔어요 for past"}]}', qa_at = now() where id = 'c09baa1d-0bbf-4ee1-83c7-4e460d44cee7' and md5(patterns_json) = 'dc066a72fd412b4c94a40498815ce9f9';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"explanation":"-아/어지다 has two main uses: with adjectives it means ''to become'' (추워지다, 좋아지다 — a change of state); with some action verbs it forms a passive (만들어지다 ''to be made'', 이루어지다 ''to be achieved''). The questions below practise the ''become'' use.","questions":[{"q":"The room became messy. (방이 지저분해___.) Which ending completes this sentence?","choices":["졌어요","됐어요","냈어요"],"correct":0,"hint":"Adjective + -아/어지다 = ''became''; past: 지저분해졌어요. (지저분해됐어요 is not a word; ''became'' with 되다 would be 지저분하게 됐어요.)"},{"q":"Which sentence uses ~어지다 correctly?","choices":["나는 피곤해졌다. (I became tired.)","나는 밥을 먹어졌다. (I ate rice passively.)","나는 책을 읽어졌다. (I read a book passively.)"],"correct":0,"hint":"With adjectives -아/어지다 means ''become''; it can''t be added to transitive verbs like 먹다/읽다 with the doer as subject."},{"q":"The weather got cold. (날씨가 추워___.) Fill in the blank.","choices":["졌다","났다","겼다"],"correct":0,"hint":"추워 + 어지다 becomes 추워졌다 (became cold)"},{"q":"Identify the sentence with an error in ~어지다 usage:","choices":["내 꿈이 현실어졌다. (My dream became reality.)","상황이 나빠졌다. (The situation got worse.)","문제가 해결되었다. (The problem was solved.)"],"correct":0,"hint":"현실 is a noun; you cannot attach ~어지다 directly to nouns without proper verb conjugation"},{"q":"Complete the dialogue: A: 왜 우울해 보여? (Why do you look sad?) B: 일이 많아___서 스트레스를 받아. (Work piled up and I''m stressed.)","choices":["져","해","나"],"correct":0,"hint":"많다 + -아/어지다 = 많아지다 ''to increase''; before -아/어서 it becomes 많아져서. -아/어서 never takes past tense, so not 많아졌서/많아졌어서."}]}', qa_at = now() where id = '1a27673f-a115-47e7-8d79-819f64e06791' and md5(patterns_json) = '251a146ecc6470486d716be13b0581aa';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"patterns":[{"name":"은/는 vs 이/가","level":"Beginner","exp":"은/는 marks the topic of a sentence (what you''re talking about), while 이/가 marks the grammatical subject (who/what performs the action or has the quality). Use 은/는 when introducing or contrasting a topic; use 이/가 when answering ''who?'' or ''what?'' or when emphasizing the subject itself.","structure":"Noun + 은/는 (topic marker) vs Noun + 이/가 (subject marker)","when_to_use":"Use 은/는 when you''re establishing what you want to talk about (e.g., ''As for coffee, I like it''). Use 이/가 when someone asks ''Who did this?'' or ''What is this?'' and you need to identify the subject directly.","watch_out":"Learners often use 은/는 everywhere, but question words (누가, 뭐가, 어디가) and their answers take 이/가, and the subject inside a connected clause (-아/어서, -(으)면, -(으)ㄹ 때) usually takes 이/가. 은/는 sets the topic or shows contrast; 이/가 identifies or introduces new information.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 학생입니다.","en":"As for me, I am a student. (Topic: me; identifying my role)"},{"ko":"누가 왔어요? 제 친구가 왔어요.","en":"Who came? My friend came. (이/가 directly identifies who came)"},{"ko":"커피는 맛있어요.","en":"As for coffee, it''s delicious. (Topic: coffee; statement about it)"},{"ko":"어떤 색이 좋아요? 파란색이 좋아요.","en":"Which color do you like? Blue is nice. (이/가 identifies and emphasizes the subject ''blue'')"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"누가 왔어요? 제 친구는 왔어요.","en":"Who came? My friend came.","error_type":"PARTICLE","correction":"누가 왔어요? 제 친구가 왔어요."},{"ko":"날씨는 좋아서 산책했어요.","en":"The weather was nice, so I went for a walk.","error_type":"PARTICLE","correction":"날씨가 좋아서 산책했어요."}]}]}', qa_at = now() where id = 'cb6aa90e-4bb5-4842-9f35-6e267b91d7e1' and md5(patterns_json) = '9ffbd478cd64c8060c0ff470ee1b8c19';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"patterns":[{"name":"(이)에요/예요 — It is / I am","level":"Starter","exp":"Use (이)에요/예요 to identify or describe what someone or something is — like saying ''is'' or ''am'' in English. This ending is polite and casual at the same time, making it perfect for everyday conversation. It attaches directly to a noun, with the form depending on whether the noun ends in a consonant or vowel.","structure":"Noun (consonant ending) + 이에요 / Noun (vowel ending) + 예요","when_to_use":"Use it when you meet someone new and want to introduce yourself or ask what something is — for example, saying your name or job: ''저는 학생이에요.'' (I''m a student.)","watch_out":"Learners often use 예요 after a noun that ends in a consonant — for example, writing ''학생예요'' instead of the correct ''학생이에요''. Always check whether the noun ends in a consonant or vowel before choosing the form.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 학생이에요.","en":"I''m a student."},{"ko":"이게 뭐예요?","en":"What is this?"},{"ko":"오빠는 의사예요.","en":"My older brother is a doctor."},{"ko":"여기가 화장실이에요.","en":"This is the restroom."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 선생님예요.","en":"I''m a teacher.","error_type":"CONJUGATION","correction":"저는 선생님이에요."},{"ko":"저는 의사이에요.","en":"I''m a doctor.","error_type":"CONJUGATION","correction":"저는 의사예요."}]}]}', qa_at = now() where id = 'b9dd75c5-c8ec-414f-a69d-99d5af1bc55c' and md5(patterns_json) = 'c66a6733b115e0804e6e50b4057fa4fe';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"patterns":[{"name":"하고 / 와/과 / (이)랑 — And / With","level":"Starter","exp":"Use 하고, 와/과, or (이)랑 to connect two or more nouns (''A and B'') or to say you did something together with someone (''with A''). All three mean the same thing, but they differ in formality: 와/과 is formal/written, 하고 is neutral everyday speech, and (이)랑 is casual and friendly. Choose based on who you are talking to and the situation.","structure":"Noun A + 하고/와/과/(이)랑 + Noun B  |  Noun + 하고/와/과/(이)랑 같이 [verb]","when_to_use":"When ordering food and listing items — e.g., telling a server ''비빔밥하고 된장찌개 주세요'' — use 하고 to link the two dishes naturally in everyday speech.","watch_out":"Learners often mix up 와 and 과: 와 follows vowel-ending nouns (친구와, 사과와) and 과 follows consonant-ending nouns (밥과, 책과) — so 밥와 is wrong. (이)랑 works the same way: 랑 after a vowel (오빠랑), 이랑 after a consonant (동생이랑). 하고 never changes.","examples":[{"ko":"가방하고 지갑을 샀어요.","en":"I bought a bag and a wallet."},{"ko":"오늘 친구랑 카페에 갔어요.","en":"I went to a café with a friend today."},{"ko":"점심에 밥과 국을 먹었어요.","en":"I had rice and soup for lunch."},{"ko":"동생이랑 같이 숙제해요.","en":"I do homework together with my younger sibling."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"오빠이랑 영화를 봤어요.","en":"I watched a movie with my older brother.","error_type":"PARTICLE","correction":"오빠랑 영화를 봤어요."},{"ko":"사과와 바나나와 먹었어요.","en":"I ate apples and bananas.","error_type":"PARTICLE","correction":"사과와 바나나를 먹었어요."}]}]}', qa_at = now() where id = '4e58519f-0b19-462f-b043-ef5d28504385' and md5(patterns_json) = '68d5eb477a5d72b2693b5857674be47b';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"그는 보통 버스에 학교에 가요.","en":"He usually goes to school by bus.","error_type":"PARTICLE","correction":"그는 보통 버스로 학교에 가요."},{"ko":"저는 아침에 보통 빵으로 먹어요.","en":"I usually eat bread for breakfast.","error_type":"PARTICLE","correction":"저는 아침으로 보통 빵을 먹어요."}]', qa_at = now() where id = '93f846cf-69fd-4e42-b9f6-e980df490c47' and md5(patterns_json) = '10ea833f0bcc667a015570bc5d87a329';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"내일 뭐 합니다?","en":"What are you doing tomorrow?","error_type":"CONJUGATION","correction":"내일 뭐 합니까?"},{"ko":"주말에 뭐 하습니까?","en":"What do you do on the weekend?","error_type":"CONJUGATION","correction":"주말에 뭐 합니까?"}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'a5aa6808-e268-418b-a243-1afd8a26a470' and md5(patterns_json) = '18d65235f8b2082265a2af3193edea00';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"저는 내일에 숙제를 할 거예요.","en":"I will do homework tomorrow.","error_type":"PARTICLE","correction":"저는 내일 숙제를 할 거예요."},{"ko":"우리는 3시에 도착했어요 학교에.","en":"We arrived at school at 3 o''clock.","error_type":"WORD_ORDER","correction":"우리는 3시에 학교에 도착했어요."}]', qa_at = now() where id = '76e3002f-d189-4df9-b8ad-b0d6976819a4' and md5(patterns_json) = '0792c27f8107dfce7d93cb955454a0c0';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"좋아하는 배우를 있어요.","en":"I have a favorite actor.","error_type":"PARTICLE","correction":"좋아하는 배우가 있어요."},{"ko":"좋아하는 배우이 있어요.","en":"I have a favorite actor.","error_type":"PARTICLE","correction":"좋아하는 배우가 있어요."}]', qa_at = now() where id = '9106865c-96a2-4103-9dac-9d778a101e09' and md5(patterns_json) = 'e622606a43909d19ca2f9d8a10efdd6c';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"형제자매이 있어요?","en":"Do you have siblings?","error_type":"PARTICLE","correction":"형제자매가 있어요?"},{"ko":"형제자매를 있어요?","en":"Do you have siblings?","error_type":"PARTICLE","correction":"형제자매가 있어요?"}]', qa_at = now() where id = '03297e61-b0a7-42cf-9fdf-d0894d2c2e2c' and md5(patterns_json) = '95ba3fe109d146d161903b18ec9bb469';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"ko":"저는 저녁을 좋아해.","en":"I like dinner.","error_type":"FORMALITY","correction":"저는 저녁을 좋아해요."},{"ko":"저는 저녁을 좋아하요.","en":"I like dinner.","error_type":"CONJUGATION","correction":"저는 저녁을 좋아해요."}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'a34334ed-cff6-443f-9d9a-d69a5d27c03a' and md5(patterns_json) = '564a1804723eb4675c93e99e3f040002';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"expressions":[{"ko":"~하고 싶어요","rom":"~hago sip-eo-yo","en":"I want to ~; I feel like ~-ing","parts":[{"ko":"~하고","en":"V + 고 (connective)"},{"ko":"싶어요","en":"want to, feel like (desire suffix + polite ending)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 게임을 하고 싶어요.","en":"I want to play games."},{"ko":"친구와 축구를 하고 싶어요.","en":"I feel like playing soccer with my friend."}]},{"ko":"~을/를 자주 해요","rom":"~eul/reul jaju hae-yo","en":"I often/frequently ~; I do ~ a lot","parts":[{"ko":"~을/를","en":"object particle (을 after a consonant, 를 after a vowel)"},{"ko":"자주","en":"often, frequently (adverb)"},{"ko":"해요","en":"do (present tense, polite)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 게임을 자주 해요.","en":"I play games often."},{"ko":"저는 운동을 자주 해요.","en":"I work out often."}]},{"ko":"~하는 것이 취미예요","rom":"~haneun geosi chwimiyeyo","en":"~-ing is my hobby; I''m into ~","parts":[{"ko":"~하는","en":"V + 는 (present-tense modifier)"},{"ko":"것이","en":"the act of; that thing (nominalizer + subject particle)"},{"ko":"취미예요","en":"is a hobby (noun + copula, polite)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"게임을 하는 것이 취미예요.","en":"Gaming is my hobby."},{"ko":"책 읽는 것이 취미예요.","en":"Reading books is my hobby."}]},{"ko":"시간이 날 때마다 ~해요","rom":"sigan-i nal ttae-mada ~hae-yo","en":"Whenever I have time/free time, I ~","parts":[{"ko":"시간이 나다","en":"to have free time (idiom: lit. ''time comes out'')"},{"ko":"~ㄹ 때마다","en":"every/whenever time that V (temporal pattern)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"시간이 날 때마다 게임을 해요.","en":"Whenever I have free time, I play games."},{"ko":"시간이 날 때마다 음악을 들어요.","en":"Whenever I have time, I listen to music."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는 것 같아요","rom":"~(eu)n/neun geot gat-a-yo","en":"It seems/looks like ~; I think ~","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는","en":"modifier: -는 after action verbs (present) and after 있다/없다 words (재미있는), -(으)ㄴ after other adjectives or for past actions"},{"ko":"것","en":"thing (nominalizer)"},{"ko":"같아요","en":"seems, looks like, appears (polite)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"그 게임은 재미있는 것 같아요.","en":"That game looks fun."},{"ko":"민수 씨는 게임을 좋아하는 것 같아요.","en":"It seems like Minsu likes games."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ 때 시간 가는 줄 몰라요","rom":"~(eu)l ttae sigan ganeun jul mollayo","en":"When I ~, I lose track of time; Time flies when I ~","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ 때","en":"when/at the time of ~ (temporal clause)"},{"ko":"시간 가는 줄 모르다","en":"not notice time passing (idiom: lit. ''don''t know time goes'')"}],"examples":[{"ko":"게임을 할 때 시간 가는 줄 몰라요.","en":"When I play games, I lose track of time."},{"ko":"영화를 볼 때 시간 가는 줄 몰라요.","en":"When I watch movies, time flies."}]}]}', qa_at = now() where id = '264b33d1-4091-4a1d-b3de-14cf269f3c27' and md5(patterns_json) = '03cb90e33ba71469b6800378ac41f803';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"expressions":[{"ko":"우리 가족은 ~명이에요","rom":"uri gajogeun ~myeongieyo","en":"There are ~ people in our family","parts":[{"ko":"우리 가족은","en":"our family (as topic)"},{"ko":"~명이에요","en":"is/are ~ people (counter + copula)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"우리 가족은 5명이에요.","en":"There are 5 people in our family."},{"ko":"우리 가족은 4명이에요.","en":"There are 4 people in our family."}]},{"ko":"~이/가 있어요","rom":"~i/ga isseoyo","en":"I/we have ~; there is/are ~","parts":[{"ko":"~이/가","en":"subject particle (marks the object possessed)"},{"ko":"있어요","en":"there is/are; have (present indicative)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 언니가 있어요.","en":"I have an older sister."},{"ko":"형이 두 명 있어요.","en":"I/we have two older brothers."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는 편이에요","rom":"~(eu)n/neun pyeon-i-eyo","en":"tend(s) to be ~; is on the ~ side","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는","en":"modifier: -(으)ㄴ after adjectives (큰, 조용한), -는 after action verbs (먹는)"},{"ko":"편이에요","en":"side; tendency (usually implying slightly more than average)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"우리 가족은 식구가 많은 편이에요.","en":"Our family is on the large side (we have a lot of family members)."},{"ko":"저희 가족은 조용한 편이에요.","en":"My family is on the quiet side."}]},{"ko":"~이/가 있으면 좋겠어요","rom":"~i/ga isseumyeon jokesseoyo","en":"I wish I had ~; it would be nice to have ~","parts":[{"ko":"~이/가 있으면","en":"if (I) had ~ (있다 + -(으)면 ''if'')"},{"ko":"좋겠어요","en":"it would be nice (좋다 + -겠- for a wish)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"형제가 있으면 좋겠어요.","en":"I wish I had siblings."},{"ko":"동생이 한 명 더 있으면 좋겠어요.","en":"I wish I had one more younger sibling."}]},{"ko":"~에는 ~이/가 있어요","rom":"~eneun ~i/ga isseoyo","en":"In/at ~, there is/are ~","parts":[{"ko":"~에는","en":"in/at ~ (location + particle indicating scope/contrast)"},{"ko":"~이/가 있어요","en":"there is/are (existential)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"우리 집에는 고양이도 한 마리 있어요.","en":"At our house, there''s also a cat."},{"ko":"우리 집에는 할머니도 계세요.","en":"My grandmother lives at our house too. (계세요 = honorific 있어요)"}]},{"ko":"~은/는 ~(으)로 이루어져 있어요","rom":"~eun/neun ~(eu)ro irueojyeo isseoyo","en":"is made up of ~; consists of ~","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)로","en":"of (what something is made up of; 으로 after a consonant, 로 after a vowel or ㄹ)"},{"ko":"이루어져 있어요","en":"is made up of (이루어지다 + -아/어 있다, a resulting state)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"우리 가족은 부모님과 아이들로 이루어져 있어요.","en":"Our family is made up of parents and children."},{"ko":"우리 가족은 할머니, 부모님, 그리고 저로 이루어져 있어요.","en":"My family is made up of my grandmother, my parents, and me."}]}]}', qa_at = now() where id = '03bc6ab6-5007-4ad3-9e40-276840440904' and md5(patterns_json) = 'c1e73f3e98ed28f2f10fb23d408c6c5a';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"expressions":[{"ko":"~은/는 ~이에요/예요","rom":"~eun/neun ~ieyo/yeyo","en":"~ is ~; introduces a family member and their role/relationship","parts":[{"ko":"~은/는","en":"topic particle (은 after consonant, 는 after vowel)"},{"ko":"~이에요/예요","en":"copula, polite present (이에요 after a consonant, 예요 after a vowel)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"제 언니는 학생이에요.","en":"My older sister is a student."},{"ko":"우리 아버지는 의사예요.","en":"My father is a doctor."}]},{"ko":"~하고 싶어요","rom":"~hago sipeo-yo","en":"I want to ~; expresses desire (1st person only)","parts":[{"ko":"~고","en":"connective ending linking action verb to 싶다"},{"ko":"싶어요","en":"want to (psych-verb in 1st person bare form)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 엄마와 쇼핑하고 싶어요.","en":"I want to go shopping with my mom."},{"ko":"저는 가족과 여행 가고 싶어요.","en":"I want to travel with my family."}]},{"ko":"~는 편이에요","rom":"~neun pyeon-ieyo","en":"tends to ~; describes a habitual or characteristic tendency","parts":[{"ko":"~는","en":"present-tense modifier for verbs"},{"ko":"편이에요","en":"tends to; leans toward (habitual or general pattern)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"우리 할머니는 일찍 주무시는 편이에요.","en":"My grandmother tends to go to bed early."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ 때마다","rom":"~(eu)l ttae-mada","en":"whenever ~; each time that ~ (every time a situation happens)","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ","en":"future/prospective modifier (을 after consonant, ㄹ after vowel)"},{"ko":"때마다","en":"every time that; whenever (each occasion)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"가족이 모일 때마다 같이 노래를 불러요.","en":"Whenever my family gets together, we sing together."}]},{"ko":"시간이 나다","rom":"sigan-i nada","en":"to have free time; time becomes available","parts":[{"ko":"시간이","en":"time (subject particle)"},{"ko":"나다","en":"comes out, appears, becomes available"}],"examples":[{"ko":"주말에 시간이 나면 엄마와 함께 요리해요.","en":"When I have time on weekends, I cook with my mom."},{"ko":"오늘 시간이 나서 할아버지를 만났어요.","en":"I had time today, so I met my grandfather."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는 것 같아요","rom":"~(eu)n/neun geot gata-yo","en":"seems to ~; it appears that ~; softly expresses observation or inference","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는","en":"modifier: -(으)ㄴ after adjectives (present) or for past actions, -는 for present actions"},{"ko":"것","en":"thing; nominalizes the clause"},{"ko":"같아요","en":"seems, appears (looks like)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"우리 엄마는 요즘 피곤한 것 같아요.","en":"My mom seems tired these days."},{"ko":"남동생이 숙제를 다 한 것 같아요.","en":"It seems my younger brother has finished his homework."}]}],"qa":true}', qa_at = now() where id = '284aba70-4f5e-4f78-ba1b-94f8b550b074' and md5(patterns_json) = '3be05b0f31c6ab3246bec101d7d9536f';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"expressions":[{"ko":"~하는 것이 취미다","rom":"~haneun geosi chwimida","en":"The act of ~ing is my hobby","parts":[{"ko":"~하는","en":"V + 는 (present-tense modifier; describes ongoing action)"},{"ko":"것이","en":"the act/thing of (nominalizer + subject particle)"},{"ko":"취미다","en":"is a hobby"}],"examples":[{"ko":"그림 그리는 것이 취미예요.","en":"Drawing pictures is my hobby."},{"ko":"노래 부르는 것이 취미예요.","en":"Singing is my hobby."}]},{"ko":"~을/를 자주 하다","rom":"~eul/reul jaju hada","en":"to do ~ often; to frequently ~","parts":[{"ko":"~을/를","en":"object particle (을 after consonant, 를 after vowel)"},{"ko":"자주","en":"often, frequently (adverb)"},{"ko":"하다","en":"to do (action verb)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 게임을 자주 해요.","en":"I play games often."},{"ko":"저는 요가를 자주 해요.","en":"I do yoga often."}]},{"ko":"~고 싶어요","rom":"~go sip-eo-yo","en":"I want to ~","parts":[{"ko":"~고","en":"attaches to the verb stem before 싶다"},{"ko":"싶다","en":"to want (first-person psych-verb; 1st person uses bare form)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 요리하고 싶어요.","en":"I want to cook."},{"ko":"저는 악기를 배우고 싶어요.","en":"I want to learn an instrument."}]},{"ko":"~는 편이에요","rom":"~neun pyeon-i-e-yo","en":"I tend to ~; I''m the type who ~s","parts":[{"ko":"~는","en":"present-tense modifier (ongoing habit/tendency)"},{"ko":"편이다","en":"to be the type/side (habitual tendency marker)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 혼자 있는 시간을 좋아하는 편이에요.","en":"I tend to like being alone."},{"ko":"저는 손으로 뭔가 만드는 것을 좋아하는 편이에요.","en":"I tend to enjoy making things with my hands."}]}],"qa":true}', qa_at = now() where id = '4f45bf99-b5c8-418d-806c-be61f83a5d15' and md5(patterns_json) = 'd83ae725a472409614047be31e2d7ca9';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"expressions":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는 것 같아요","rom":"~(eu)n/neun geot gatayo","en":"It seems/appears that ~; I think ~","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는","en":"past/present tense modifier (attaches to verb or adjective stem)"},{"ko":"것","en":"thing; nominalizer that turns a clause into a noun"},{"ko":"같아요","en":"seems, appears, looks like (expresses inference or subjective impression)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저녁 루틴을 지키는 것이 더 중요한 것 같아요.","en":"It seems that maintaining an evening routine is more important."},{"ko":"아침에 충분히 준비하는 것이 하루를 편하게 만드는 것 같아요.","en":"It seems that preparing adequately in the morning makes the day more comfortable."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ 때마다","rom":"~(eu)l ttaemada","en":"whenever ~; every time ~","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ","en":"future/prospective modifier on the verb"},{"ko":"때","en":"time; occasion"},{"ko":"마다","en":"every; each time (distributive particle)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저녁에 명상할 때마다 스트레스가 줄어드는 것 같아요.","en":"Whenever I meditate in the evening, my stress seems to go down."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는 편이에요","rom":"~(eu)n/neun pyeonieyo","en":"tend to ~; am inclined to ~; on the side of ~","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는","en":"modifier: -는 after action verbs, -(으)ㄴ after adjectives (not a topic particle)"},{"ko":"편","en":"side; tendency (implies a general inclination or habit)"},{"ko":"이에요","en":"is; linking verb in 해요-form"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 아침 루틴을 꾸준히 지키는 편이에요.","en":"I tend to keep my morning routine consistently."}]},{"ko":"~고 싶어요","rom":"~go sipeoyo","en":"want to ~; would like to ~","parts":[{"ko":"~고","en":"attaches to the verb stem before 싶다"},{"ko":"싶","en":"want (psych-adj expressing desire; 1st-person uses bare form, 3rd-person uses ~어 하다)"},{"ko":"어요","en":"polite present ending in 해요-form"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 아침에 더 일찍 일어나고 싶어요.","en":"I want to wake up earlier in the morning."},{"ko":"동생은 저녁에 독서하고 싶어 해요.","en":"My younger sibling wants to read in the evening."}]}],"qa":true}', qa_at = now() where id = '8557abb1-4a2b-4c4d-84c3-d992a040d822' and md5(patterns_json) = '6b5b10306407ddc15c75a8844e0a99df';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"expressions":[{"ko":"매일 ~을/를 하는 편이에요","rom":"maeil ~eul/reul haneun pyeonieyo","en":"I tend to do ~ every day / Every day I usually do ~","parts":[{"ko":"매일","en":"every day"},{"ko":"~을/를","en":"object particle: 을 after consonant-final stem, 를 after vowel-final stem"},{"ko":"하는","en":"V + 는 (present-tense modifier)"},{"ko":"편이에요","en":"tend to / usually (habitual tendency)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 매일 운동을 하는 편이에요.","en":"I usually work out every day."}]},{"ko":"~고 나서 ~","rom":"~go naseo ~","en":"after doing ~, then ~ / ~ and then ~","parts":[{"ko":"~고 나서","en":"after/once V is completed (sequential action marker)"},{"ko":"~","en":"the next action that follows"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 일어나고 나서 샤워를 해요.","en":"After I wake up, I take a shower."},{"ko":"아침을 먹고 나서 양치를 해요.","en":"After I eat breakfast, I brush my teeth."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ 때마다 ~","rom":"~(eu)l ttaemada ~","en":"every time ~, / whenever ~","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ 때마다","en":"every time / each time that V happens (repeated temporal trigger)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"일어날 때마다 휴대폰을 확인해요.","en":"Every time I wake up, I check my phone."},{"ko":"아침에 나갈 때마다 가방을 챙겨요.","en":"Whenever I leave in the morning, I pack my bag."}]},{"ko":"~는 것 같아요","rom":"~neun geot gatayo","en":"it seems like ~, / it appears that ~","parts":[{"ko":"~는 것","en":"the act/fact that ~ (nominalization with present-tense modifier)"},{"ko":"같아요","en":"it seems, it appears (perception/inference)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"그 사람은 매일 일찍 일어나는 것 같아요.","en":"It seems like that person wakes up early every day."}]},{"ko":"시간이 나면 ~","rom":"sigan-i namyeon ~","en":"if/when I have time, I ~","parts":[{"ko":"시간이 나면","en":"if time becomes available / when I have spare time (fixed collocation: 시간 + 나다)"},{"ko":"~","en":"the action taken during that free time"}],"examples":[{"ko":"시간이 나면 신문을 읽어요.","en":"When I have time, I read the newspaper."},{"ko":"시간이 나면 명상을 해요.","en":"If I have time, I meditate."}]},{"ko":"~고 싶어요","rom":"~go sipeoyo","en":"I want to ~","parts":[{"ko":"~고 싶-","en":"want to V (desire / aspiration marker)"},{"ko":"어요","en":"polite present-tense ending"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 아침에 더 자고 싶어요.","en":"In the morning, I want to sleep more."}]}],"qa":true}', qa_at = now() where id = '3506e499-5ded-49a5-8e61-9a4a7f4b31c4' and md5(patterns_json) = 'f3c60d71c4941ef32aa0e4c078cf541c';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"expressions":[{"ko":"~하고 싶었어요","rom":"~hago sipeosseoyo","en":"wanted to ~; had a dream of ~-ing","parts":[{"ko":"~하고","en":"V + 고 (conjunctive form)"},{"ko":"싶었어요","en":"wanted to (past tense of 싶다)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"어릴 때는 의사가 되고 싶었어요.","en":"When I was young, I wanted to become a doctor."},{"ko":"동생은 유명한 가수가 되고 싶어 했어요.","en":"My younger sibling wanted to become a famous singer."}]},{"ko":"~하는 것이 꿈이었어요","rom":"~haneun geosi kkumieosseoyo","en":"my dream was to ~; I dreamed of ~-ing","parts":[{"ko":"~하는","en":"V + 는 (present-tense modifier)"},{"ko":"것이","en":"the act of; thing (nominalizer + subject marker)"},{"ko":"꿈이었어요","en":"was a dream (past tense)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"우주 비행사가 되는 것이 꿈이었어요.","en":"My dream was to become an astronaut."},{"ko":"아버지처럼 운동선수가 되는 것이 제 꿈이었어요.","en":"Becoming an athlete like my father was my dream."}]},{"ko":"~을/를 잘하고 싶었어요","rom":"~eul/reul jalhago sipeosseoyo","en":"wanted to be good at ~; dreamed of doing ~ well","parts":[{"ko":"~을/를","en":"object marker (chosen based on final consonant)"},{"ko":"잘","en":"well (adverb)"},{"ko":"하고 싶었어요","en":"wanted to do (past tense)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"수영을 잘하고 싶었어요.","en":"I wanted to be good at swimming."},{"ko":"형은 축구를 잘하고 싶어 했어요.","en":"My older brother wanted to be good at soccer."}]},{"ko":"~는 편이었어요","rom":"~neun pyeonieosseoyo","en":"tended to ~; was the type to ~","parts":[{"ko":"~는","en":"V + 는 (present-tense modifier)"},{"ko":"편이었어요","en":"was the type/tendency (past tense)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"어릴 때는 꿈을 자주 꾸는 편이었어요.","en":"When I was young, I tended to dream often."},{"ko":"어릴 때는 책을 많이 읽는 편이었어요.","en":"When I was young, I tended to read a lot of books."}]},{"ko":"~할 때마다 기뻤어요","rom":"~hal ttaemada gippeosseoyo","en":"felt happy whenever I ~; got happy every time I ~","parts":[{"ko":"~할 때마다","en":"every time V; whenever V (temporal pattern)"},{"ko":"기뻤어요","en":"was/felt happy (past tense)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"그 꿈에 대해 생각할 때마다 기뻤어요.","en":"I felt happy whenever I thought about that dream."},{"ko":"칭찬을 들을 때마다 기뻤어요.","en":"I felt happy every time I was praised."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는 것 같았어요","rom":"~(eu)n/neun geot gatasseoyo","en":"it seemed like ~; I think ~ was the case","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는","en":"modifier: -(으)ㄴ after adjectives, -는 after action verbs and 있다/없다 words (멋있는, 좋아하는)"},{"ko":"것 같았어요","en":"seemed to be; it appeared (past tense)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"그때는 선생님이 제일 멋있는 것 같았어요.","en":"Back then, teachers seemed like the coolest people."},{"ko":"형은 그림 그리는 것을 정말 좋아하는 것 같았어요.","en":"My older brother seemed to really like drawing."}]}]}', qa_at = now() where id = '950c0aee-73f4-4430-a968-2e663a5884e0' and md5(patterns_json) = '827c8b6a870654f08e06bca22e5aba59';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"expressions":[{"ko":"~을/를 좋아하다","rom":"~eul/reul joahada","en":"to like ~; to be fond of ~","parts":[{"ko":"~을/를","en":"direct object particle (object of liking)"},{"ko":"좋아하다","en":"to like, to prefer"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 피자를 좋아해요.","en":"I like pizza."},{"ko":"누나는 김밥을 좋아해요.","en":"My older sister likes kimbap."}]},{"ko":"~는 것 같다","rom":"~neun geot gatda","en":"it seems/appears that ~; looks like ~","parts":[{"ko":"~는","en":"present-tense modifier (present facts)"},{"ko":"것","en":"thing, act (nominalizer)"},{"ko":"같다","en":"seems, appears"}],"examples":[{"ko":"엄마는 한식을 가장 좋아하는 것 같아요.","en":"It seems Mom likes Korean food the most."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ 때마다 먹고 싶어요","rom":"~(eu)l ttaemada meokgo sipeo-yo","en":"I want to eat ~ every time ~ (happens)","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ 때마다","en":"every time, whenever (frequency with condition)"},{"ko":"먹고 싶어요","en":"want to eat (desire + action)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"비가 올 때마다 국수를 먹고 싶어요.","en":"Every time it rains, I want to eat noodles."},{"ko":"친구를 만날 때마다 치킨을 먹고 싶어요.","en":"Whenever I meet a friend, I want to eat chicken."}]},{"ko":"~을/를 자주 먹어요","rom":"~eul/reul jaju meogeo-yo","en":"to often/frequently eat ~","parts":[{"ko":"~을/를","en":"direct object particle"},{"ko":"자주","en":"often, frequently (adverb of frequency)"},{"ko":"먹어요","en":"eat (present habitual)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 떡볶이를 자주 먹어요.","en":"I often eat tteokbokki."}]}],"qa":true}', qa_at = now() where id = '058964fa-f899-4a17-8fc7-c64859b04c65' and md5(patterns_json) = '96ae1b6069e4463fbb74b67e68879200';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"expressions":[{"ko":"~을/를 먹을 때마다 ~아/어지다","rom":"~eul/reul meogeul ttaemada ~a/eojida","en":"every time I eat ~, (I feel/become ~); whenever I have ~, (something happens)","parts":[{"ko":"~을/를 먹을 때마다","en":"whenever/every time (I) eat ~ (temporal marker + frequentative)"},{"ko":"~아/어지다","en":"adjective + 아/어지다: become/get (change of state, e.g. 좋다 → 좋아지다)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"그곳의 스테이크를 먹을 때마다 기분이 좋아져요.","en":"Every time I eat their steak, my mood gets better."}]},{"ko":"~는 편이다","rom":"~neun pyeoni-da","en":"tend(s) to ~; have a tendency to ~; on the side of being ~","parts":[{"ko":"~는","en":"present-tense modifier"},{"ko":"편이다","en":"is on the side of; tends to be (habitual/dispositional marker)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 매운 음식을 자주 찾는 편이에요.","en":"I tend to seek out spicy food often."},{"ko":"그 셰프는 건강한 재료를 중요하게 생각하는 편이에요.","en":"That chef tends to prioritize healthy ingredients."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ 것 같다","rom":"~(eu)n geot gatda","en":"seems/appears to be ~; looks like ~","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ","en":"past/completed-state modifier"},{"ko":"것","en":"thing; fact (nominalizer)"},{"ko":"같다","en":"(1) to be the same, alike; (2) to seem/appear/look like (inferential)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"그 식당의 육수는 정말 오래 우린 것 같아요.","en":"Their broth appears to have been simmered for a very long time."},{"ko":"이 디저트는 주인이 직접 만든 것 같은데 정말 맛있어요.","en":"This dessert looks like the owner made it themselves, and it''s really delicious."}]},{"ko":"~기 때문에","rom":"~gi ttaemune","en":"because ~; since ~; due to the fact that ~","parts":[{"ko":"~기","en":"gerund/nominalizer (action turned into a noun)"},{"ko":"때문에","en":"because of; due to"}],"examples":[{"ko":"그 카페는 분위기가 따뜻하기 때문에 항상 사람이 많아요.","en":"That café is always crowded because its atmosphere is warm."}]},{"ko":"시간이 나면 ~곤 하다","rom":"sigan-i namyeon ~gon hada","en":"whenever I have time, I ~ (habitual action); I tend to ~ when I get the chance","parts":[{"ko":"시간이 나면","en":"when(ever) I have/find time (condition: 나다 = to have/arise)"},{"ko":"~곤 하다","en":"tend to do ~; make a habit of ~ (habitual/iterative action)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"시간이 나면 그 식당에 가곤 해요.","en":"Whenever I have time, I tend to go to that restaurant."},{"ko":"주말에 시간이 나면 새로운 음식점을 찾아보곤 합니다.","en":"When I have free time on weekends, I make a habit of looking for new restaurants."}]}],"qa":true}', qa_at = now() where id = 'fc813510-5242-4884-9c62-02411c81b52a' and md5(patterns_json) = '12a3d3b9391aab6a89ff0cbe2fd5618d';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"expressions":[{"ko":"~하는 것이 취미예요","rom":"~haneun geosi chwimiyeyo","en":"the act of ~-ing is my hobby","parts":[{"ko":"~하는","en":"V + 는 (present-tense modifier; turns action into description)"},{"ko":"것이","en":"thing / the act of (nominalizer + subject particle)"},{"ko":"취미예요","en":"is a hobby"}],"examples":[{"ko":"영화 보는 것이 취미예요.","en":"Watching movies is my hobby."},{"ko":"노래 부르는 것이 취미예요.","en":"Singing is my hobby."}]},{"ko":"~을/를 좋아해요","rom":"~eul/reul joahaeyo","en":"I like ~","parts":[{"ko":"~을/를","en":"object particle (을 after consonant, 를 after vowel)"},{"ko":"좋아해요","en":"like, enjoy (present tense, polite)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"도서관을 좋아해요.","en":"I like the library."},{"ko":"역사책을 좋아해요.","en":"I like history books."}]},{"ko":"~는 것 같아요","rom":"~neun geot gatayo","en":"it seems that ~; I think ~","parts":[{"ko":"~는","en":"present-tense modifier (V + 는)"},{"ko":"것","en":"thing / the act"},{"ko":"같아요","en":"seems, appears (present tense, polite)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"친구가 책을 많이 읽는 것 같아요.","en":"It seems my friend reads a lot of books."},{"ko":"시간이 빨리 가는 것 같아요.","en":"Time seems to pass quickly."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ 때마다","rom":"~(eu)l ttaemada","en":"whenever ~; every time ~","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ","en":"prospective modifier (을 after a consonant, ㄹ after a vowel; ㄹ-stem verbs add nothing: 살다 → 살 때마다)"},{"ko":"때마다","en":"every time, whenever"}],"examples":[{"ko":"책을 읽을 때마다 행복해요.","en":"Every time I read a book, I''m happy."},{"ko":"도서관에 갈 때마다 시간이 빨리 가요.","en":"Whenever I go to the library, time flies."}]},{"ko":"~는 편이에요","rom":"~neun pyeonieyo","en":"I tend to ~; I''m the type who ~s","parts":[{"ko":"~는","en":"present-tense modifier (V + 는)"},{"ko":"편이에요","en":"am the kind / tend to (indicates habit or disposition)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"책을 많이 읽는 편이에요.","en":"I tend to read a lot of books."},{"ko":"문학책을 선호하는 편이에요.","en":"I''m the type who prefers literature."}]},{"ko":"시간이 나다","rom":"sigan-i nada","en":"to have free time; to have time available","parts":[{"ko":"시간이","en":"time (subject particle)"},{"ko":"나다","en":"come out, be available (idiomatic: have time)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"시간이 나면 책을 읽어요.","en":"When I have time, I read books."},{"ko":"주말에 시간이 나요.","en":"I have free time on weekends."}]}]}', qa_at = now() where id = '508bbe77-c10f-41b0-9e80-1195002e513f' and md5(patterns_json) = 'ad874ec1d760c4c3c771d357bc2570b5';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"expressions":[{"ko":"~하는 것을 좋아해요","rom":"~haneun geoseul joahaeyo","en":"I like the act of ~ing / I like to ~","parts":[{"ko":"~하는","en":"V + 는 (present-tense modifier; turns the verb into a modifier of 것)"},{"ko":"것을","en":"thing, act (nominalizer + object particle)"},{"ko":"좋아해요","en":"like, enjoy (present indicative)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 책 읽는 것을 좋아해요.","en":"I like reading books."},{"ko":"저는 영화 보는 것을 좋아해요.","en":"I like watching movies."}]},{"ko":"~을/를 자주 V","rom":"~eul/reul jaju V","en":"often ~; frequently do ~ with [object]","parts":[{"ko":"~을/를","en":"object particle (을 after consonant, 를 after vowel)"},{"ko":"자주","en":"often, frequently (adverb)"},{"ko":"V","en":"action verb in present or past form"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 책을 자주 읽어요.","en":"I often read books."},{"ko":"제 친구는 신문을 자주 읽어요.","en":"My friend often reads newspapers."}]},{"ko":"~는 편이에요","rom":"~neun pyeonieyo","en":"I tend to ~; I''m the type who ~s (habit or tendency)","parts":[{"ko":"~는","en":"present-tense modifier for action verbs (turns verb into modifier; adjectives use ~ㄴ/은 instead)"},{"ko":"편이에요","en":"tend to be, am on the ~ side (indicating habitual tendency)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 책을 많이 읽는 편이에요.","en":"I tend to read a lot of books."},{"ko":"저는 밤에 공부하는 편이에요.","en":"I tend to study at night."}]},{"ko":"~(으)면서 ~","rom":"~(eu)myeonseo ~","en":"while ~ing; at the same time as ~ (simultaneous actions)","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)면서","en":"while, as (connective ending linking simultaneous actions; -면서 after vowel, -으면서 after consonant)"},{"ko":"~","en":"another verb phrase following in the same time frame"}],"examples":[{"ko":"책을 읽으면서 커피를 마셔요.","en":"I drink coffee while reading a book."},{"ko":"음악을 들으면서 공부해요.","en":"I study while listening to music."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ 때마다","rom":"~(eu)l ttaemada","en":"whenever I/you/one ~s; every time ~ (habitual recurrence)","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ","en":"prospective modifier (-을 after a consonant stem, -ㄹ after a vowel stem; marks anticipated or recurring action)"},{"ko":"때마다","en":"every time, whenever (each instance)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"책을 읽을 때마다 행복해요.","en":"Every time I read a book, I feel happy."},{"ko":"도서관에 갈 때마다 많은 책을 빌려요.","en":"Whenever I go to the library, I borrow many books."}]},{"ko":"시간이 날 때 ~","rom":"sigan-i nal ttae ~","en":"when I have time, ~; whenever time permits, ~","parts":[{"ko":"시간이 나다","en":"to have free time (literally: time comes out/appears)"},{"ko":"~ㄹ 때","en":"prospective temporal clause: ''when (one) ~s'' (used for anticipated or recurring situations)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"시간이 날 때 책을 읽어요.","en":"When I have time, I read books."},{"ko":"시간이 날 때 친구들을 만나요.","en":"When I have time, I meet my friends."}]}],"qa":true}', qa_at = now() where id = 'c3900d43-5985-4eea-a413-ea950978f0b7' and md5(patterns_json) = 'cd0d9791f987639c3b5985bec8fadab6';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"expressions":[{"ko":"~할 때가 제일 좋아요","rom":"~hal ttaega jeil joayo","en":"The moment when I ~ is the best","parts":[{"ko":"~할 때","en":"when/the time (prospective modifier + 때)"},{"ko":"가","en":"subject particle (이 after consonant, 가 after vowel)"},{"ko":"제일 좋아요","en":"is the best / I like it most (제일 = most, number one; 좋다 = good/to like)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"친구들을 만날 때가 제일 좋아요.","en":"I like it best when I meet my friends."},{"ko":"음악을 들을 때가 제일 좋아요.","en":"I like it best when I listen to music."}]},{"ko":"~는 것을 좋아해요","rom":"~neun geoseul joahaeyo","en":"I like doing/the act of ~","parts":[{"ko":"~는 것","en":"the act of ~ / ~ing (present-tense modifier + nominalizer)"},{"ko":"을/를","en":"object particle (을 after consonant, 를 after vowel)"},{"ko":"좋아해요","en":"like it (psych-verb in 1st-person present habitual)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"아침에 산책하는 것을 좋아해요.","en":"I like taking a walk in the morning."},{"ko":"밤에 책 읽는 것을 좋아해요.","en":"I like reading books at night."}]},{"ko":"~(으)면 기분이 좋아져요","rom":"~(eu)myeon gibuni joajyeoyo","en":"When ~ [happens], my mood becomes good / I feel happy","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)면","en":"if/when (conditional ending: 면 after a vowel or ㄹ, 으면 after a consonant)"},{"ko":"기분이","en":"mood/feeling (subject)"},{"ko":"좋아져요","en":"becomes good / improves (좋아지다 = to become better/improve)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"해가 나면 기분이 좋아져요.","en":"When the sun comes out, my mood gets better."},{"ko":"퇴근하면 기분이 좋아져요.","en":"When I leave work, my mood improves."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는 편이에요","rom":"~(eu)n/neun pyeonieyo","en":"I tend to ~; ~ is on the ~ side","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄴ/는","en":"modifier: -는 after action verbs, -(으)ㄴ after adjectives"},{"ko":"편이에요","en":"tend to / it''s usually so (편 = side/tendency; 이 + -에요)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"저는 아침에 일찍 일어나는 편이에요.","en":"I tend to get up early in the morning."},{"ko":"저녁 시간은 조용한 편이에요.","en":"The evenings are fairly quiet."}]},{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ 때마다 행복해요","rom":"~(eu)l ttaemada haengbokaeyo","en":"Every time ~, I am/feel happy","parts":[{"ko":"~(으)ㄹ 때마다","en":"every time / whenever ~ (prospective + 때 + 마다 = each time)"},{"ko":"행복해요","en":"am happy / feel happy (행복하다 = to be happy)"}],"examples":[{"ko":"가족과 함께할 때마다 행복해요.","en":"Every time I''m with my family, I feel happy."}]}],"qa":true}', qa_at = now() where id = '7f0a3f7a-efed-4dc6-9ddc-94b9b5a16a70' and md5(patterns_json) = '862e498ca5d169579fee92639f869799';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"lessons":[{"example":{"ko":"우리 가족은 4명이에요.","en":"There are 4 people in our family.","parts":[{"ko":"우리","en":"our (possessive)"},{"ko":"가족","en":"family (noun)"},{"ko":"은","en":"topic particle (subject emphasis)"},{"ko":"4명","en":"4 people (number + counter)"},{"ko":"이에요","en":"is/are (polite present copula)"}],"grammar_note":"Use ~은/는 after a noun to mark the topic of your sentence (''as for ...''). Use ~은 after a consonant (가족은) and ~는 after a vowel (할머니는)."},"practice":{"ko":"제 할머니는 70살이에요.","en":"My grandmother is 70 years old.","parts":[{"ko":"제","en":"my (possessive)"},{"ko":"할머니","en":"grandmother (noun)"},{"ko":"는","en":"topic particle (subject emphasis)"},{"ko":"70살","en":"70 years old (age)"},{"ko":"이에요","en":"is (polite present copula)"}],"choices":["제 할머니는 70살이에요.","제 할머니를 70살이에요.","제 할머니은 70살이에요.","제 할머니에 70살이에요."]}},{"example":{"ko":"엄마도 아빠도 회사에서 일해요.","en":"Both Mom and Dad work at a company.","parts":[{"ko":"엄마","en":"mom (noun)"},{"ko":"도","en":"also (particle — adds to first item)"},{"ko":"아빠","en":"dad (noun)"},{"ko":"도","en":"also (particle — adds to second item)"},{"ko":"회사에서","en":"at a company (location + particle)"},{"ko":"일해요","en":"work (present polite verb)"}],"grammar_note":"~도 means ''also'' or ''too'' — attach it directly to nouns to add multiple items to the same action. When used twice (X도 Y도), it means ''both X and Y'' do the same thing."},"practice":{"ko":"오빠도 언니도 대학생이에요.","en":"Both my older brother and my older sister are university students.","parts":[{"ko":"오빠","en":"older brother (noun)"},{"ko":"도","en":"also (particle)"},{"ko":"언니","en":"older sister (noun)"},{"ko":"도","en":"also (particle)"},{"ko":"대학생","en":"university student (noun)"},{"ko":"이에요","en":"is (polite present copula)"}],"choices":["오빠도 언니도 대학생이에요.","오빠도 언니를 대학생이에요.","오빠는 언니도 대학생이에요.","오빠도 언니도 대학생을 해요."]}},{"example":{"ko":"형이 3명인데 여동생은 없어요.","en":"I have 3 older brothers, but I don''t have a younger sister.","parts":[{"ko":"형","en":"older brother (said by a male) (noun)"},{"ko":"이","en":"subject particle"},{"ko":"3명","en":"3 people (number + counter)"},{"ko":"인데","en":"noun + 이다 + -ㄴ데 (''..., but'')"},{"ko":"여동생","en":"younger sister (noun)"},{"ko":"은","en":"topic particle (contrast)"},{"ko":"없어요","en":"don''t have / there isn''t (polite present)"}],"grammar_note":"-(으)ㄴ데/는데 links a background fact to what comes next; here it shows contrast (''..., but ...''). After a noun, use 인데 (noun + 이다 + -ㄴ데): 3명인데. It sounds softer than ~지만."},"practice":{"ko":"누나가 2명인데 형은 없어요.","en":"I have 2 older sisters, but no older brothers.","parts":[{"ko":"누나","en":"older sister (said by a male) (noun)"},{"ko":"가","en":"subject particle"},{"ko":"2명","en":"2 people (number + counter)"},{"ko":"인데","en":"noun + 이다 + -ㄴ데 (''..., but'')"},{"ko":"형","en":"older brother (said by a male) (noun)"},{"ko":"은","en":"topic particle (contrast)"},{"ko":"없어요","en":"don''t have / there isn''t (polite present)"}],"choices":["누나가 2명인데 형은 없어요.","누나가 2명인데 형을 없어요.","누나를 2명인데 형은 없어요.","누나가 2명는데 형은 없어요."]}}]}', qa_at = now() where id = '13e94754-325d-49d5-ae7b-de480ebefa3d' and md5(patterns_json) = '1f2368da080863ba44f8353ae5e6b170';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"lessons":[{"example":{"ko":"게임도 해요?","en":"Do you play games too?","parts":[{"ko":"게임","en":"game (noun)"},{"ko":"도","en":"also/too (inclusive particle)"},{"ko":"해요","en":"do/play (present tense polite)"},{"ko":"?","en":"question marker"}],"grammar_note":"~도 adds the meaning ''also/too'' to include something in addition to what was already mentioned."},"practice":{"ko":"영화도 봐요?","en":"Do you watch movies too?","choices":["Do you watch movies too?","Do you watch only movies?","Do you watch movies instead?","Does the movie also watch you?"]}},{"example":{"ko":"게임을 해요.","en":"I play games.","parts":[{"ko":"게임","en":"game (noun)"},{"ko":"을","en":"direct object particle (after consonant)"},{"ko":"해요","en":"do/play (present tense polite)"},{"ko":".","en":"period"}],"grammar_note":"~을/를 marks the direct object of a verb; use ~을 after consonants and ~를 after vowels."},"practice":{"ko":"밥을 먹어요.","en":"I eat rice.","choices":["I eat rice.","I eat with rice.","Rice eats me.","I am eating rice today."]}},{"example":{"ko":"게임을 하고 싶어요.","en":"I want to play games.","parts":[{"ko":"게임","en":"game (noun)"},{"ko":"을","en":"direct object particle"},{"ko":"하고","en":"do (connective form)"},{"ko":"싶어요","en":"want to (desiderative + polite)"}],"grammar_note":"~고 싶어요 expresses desire or want; the verb takes ~고 form before 싶어요."},"practice":{"ko":"노래를 부르고 싶어요.","en":"I want to sing a song.","choices":["I want to sing a song.","I want to listen to a song.","I sang a song and wanted to.","I want a song to sing me."]}}]}', qa_at = now() where id = '6476af21-e1dc-44c1-aac4-9472e997391b' and md5(patterns_json) = '75c532a92d08938e5bceba641636292f';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"lessons":[{"example":{"ko":"저는 클래식 음악을 좋아해요.","en":"I like classical music.","parts":[{"ko":"저는","en":"I (topic marker)"},{"ko":"클래식","en":"classical (noun)"},{"ko":"음악을","en":"music (direct object marker ~을/를)"},{"ko":"좋아해요","en":"like (present tense polite)"}],"grammar_note":"~을/를 marks the direct object of an action verb; use ~을 after consonants, ~를 after vowels."},"practice":{"ko":"제 친구는 한국 영화를 좋아해요.","en":"My friend likes Korean movies.","choices":["My friend likes Korean movies.","My friend likes Korean movies with me.","My friend is watching Korean movies.","Korean movies like my friend."]}},{"example":{"ko":"록 음악도 좋아해요.","en":"I like rock music too.","parts":[{"ko":"록","en":"rock (noun)"},{"ko":"음악도","en":"music + also/too particle ~도"},{"ko":"좋아해요","en":"like (present tense polite)"}],"grammar_note":"~도 means ''also'' or ''too'' and adds inclusion; it attaches directly to the noun without a space."},"practice":{"ko":"아침에도 음악을 들어요.","en":"I listen to music in the morning too.","choices":["I listen to music in the morning too.","I also listen to music instead of the morning.","The morning also listens to music.","I listened to music in the morning."]}},{"example":{"ko":"좋아하는 음악이 뭐예요?","en":"What music do you like?","parts":[{"ko":"좋아하는","en":"like (verb + present modifier -는)"},{"ko":"음악이","en":"music (subject marker)"},{"ko":"뭐예요","en":"what is it (뭐 + 예요)"}],"grammar_note":"-는 turns an action verb into a present-tense modifier for the noun after it: 좋아하다 → 좋아하는 음악 ''the music (that) you like''. Action verbs always take -는 in the present (ㄹ-stems drop the ㄹ: 만들다 → 만드는); adjectives take -(으)ㄴ instead (좋은 음악)."},"practice":{"ko":"지금 먹는 음식이 정말 맛있어요.","en":"The food I''m eating now is really delicious.","choices":["The food I''m eating now is really delicious.","I want to eat delicious food now.","Eating food now is really delicious.","The food I ate earlier was really delicious."]}}]}', qa_at = now() where id = 'ffb1cad8-36d6-4432-b879-ef626ba501a4' and md5(patterns_json) = '3c526b0d0d1c45dcfefdc4e1701b7b01';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"lessons":[{"example":{"ko":"운동을 자주 해요?","en":"Do you exercise often?","parts":[{"ko":"운동을","en":"exercise + direct object particle"},{"ko":"자주","en":"often (adverb)"},{"ko":"해요","en":"do + present tense polite ending"},{"ko":"?","en":"question marker (intonation)"}],"grammar_note":"The particle ~을/를 marks the direct object of an action verb. Here it shows that ''운동'' (exercise) is what you do."},"practice":{"ko":"커피를 매일 마셔요?","en":"Do you drink coffee every day?","choices":["Do you drink coffee every day?","Do you drink coffee every day with someone?","Does coffee drink you every day?","Do you often drink coffee?"]}},{"example":{"ko":"운동도 하고 공부도 해요.","en":"I both exercise and study.","parts":[{"ko":"운동도","en":"exercise + also/even particle"},{"ko":"하고","en":"do + connective ending -고 (and)"},{"ko":"공부도","en":"study + also/even particle"},{"ko":"해요","en":"do + present tense polite ending"}],"grammar_note":"The particle ~도 means ''also'' or ''even'' and emphasizes inclusion. ~고 is a conjunctive ending that connects two actions. Together they show: ''both A and B.''"},"practice":{"ko":"한국어도 배우고 영어도 배워요.","en":"I learn both Korean and English.","choices":["I learn both Korean and English.","I learn only Korean and English.","Korean also teaches English to me.","I teach Korean and learn English."]}},{"example":{"ko":"운동을 하면 건강해져요.","en":"If you exercise, you become healthy.","parts":[{"ko":"운동을","en":"exercise + direct object particle"},{"ko":"하면","en":"do + conditional ending (if)"},{"ko":"건강해져요","en":"become healthy (건강하다 + -아/어지다) + present tense polite ending"}],"grammar_note":"The conditional ending ~(으)면 means ''if'' and expresses cause-and-effect: ''if A happens, then B results.'' Use ~면 after vowels and ㄹ, ~으면 after other consonants."},"practice":{"ko":"물을 많이 마시면 피부가 좋아져요.","en":"If you drink a lot of water, your skin gets better.","choices":["If you drink a lot of water, your skin gets better.","Your skin drinks a lot of water, so it''s good.","If your skin drinks water, you become good.","Because your skin is good, you drink a lot of water."]}}]}', qa_at = now() where id = '3db98b75-cff1-4f9c-aa9d-73e611579f7b' and md5(patterns_json) = '70070e841896fc962b81b3698771ecf9';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"lessons":[{"example":{"ko":"책을 자주 읽어요?","en":"Do you read books often?","parts":[{"ko":"책을","en":"book + direct object particle"},{"ko":"자주","en":"often (adverb)"},{"ko":"읽어요","en":"read + present tense + polite ending"},{"ko":"?","en":"question marker"}],"grammar_note":"~을/를 marks the direct object of an action verb; the particle changes based on whether the preceding noun ends in a consonant (을) or vowel (를)."},"practice":{"ko":"영화를 좋아해요?","en":"Do you like movies?","choices":["Do you like movies?","Do you make movies?","Does the movie like you?","Do movies like you?"]}},{"example":{"ko":"책을 읽고 싶어요.","en":"I want to read a book.","parts":[{"ko":"책을","en":"book + direct object particle"},{"ko":"읽고","en":"read + connective form"},{"ko":"싶어요","en":"want to + present tense + polite ending"}],"grammar_note":"~고 싶어요 expresses desire or wish; the verb takes the ~고 form before 싶다, meaning ''want to [do something]''."},"practice":{"ko":"커피를 마시고 싶어요.","en":"I want to drink coffee.","choices":["I want to drink coffee.","I want to coffee drink.","I drink coffee and want to.","Coffee wants to drink me."]}},{"example":{"ko":"책을 많이 읽으면 똑똑해져요.","en":"If you read many books, you become smart.","parts":[{"ko":"책을","en":"book + direct object particle"},{"ko":"많이","en":"a lot (adverb)"},{"ko":"읽으면","en":"read + conditional form"},{"ko":"똑똑해져요","en":"become smart + present tense + polite ending"}],"grammar_note":"~(으)면 creates a conditional clause meaning ''if'' or ''when''; use ~으면 after consonants and ~면 after vowels or ㄹ (살다 → 살면)."},"practice":{"ko":"열심히 공부하면 좋은 점수를 받아요.","en":"If you study hard, you get a good grade.","choices":["If you study hard, you get a good grade.","When you study hard, you got a good grade.","Study hard, so you get a good grade.","If a good grade studies hard, you receive it."]}}]}', qa_at = now() where id = '7fcdd4ad-f708-435a-a7cb-3078a4e1a454' and md5(patterns_json) = '823f409dc5a721f91604f696d8d261f3';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"lessons":[{"example":{"ko":"핸드폰 배경화면이 뭐예요?","en":"What is your phone''s wallpaper?","parts":[{"ko":"핸드폰","en":"phone (noun)"},{"ko":"배경화면","en":"wallpaper / background (noun)"},{"ko":"이","en":"subject particle (marks what the question is about)"},{"ko":"뭐","en":"what (question word)"},{"ko":"예요","en":"is (copula, polite present)"}],"grammar_note":"Use 이 after a noun ending in a consonant (배경화면이) and 가 after a vowel to mark the subject of a ''what is ~?'' question. The word order is [noun] + 이/가 + 뭐 + 예요."},"practice":{"ko":"사진이 뭐예요?","en":"What is the photo?","parts":[{"ko":"사진","en":"photo (noun)"},{"ko":"이","en":"subject particle (marks subject of question)"},{"ko":"뭐","en":"what (question word)"},{"ko":"예요","en":"is (copula, polite present)"}],"choices":["사진이 뭐예요?","사진을 뭐예요?","사진가 뭐예요?","사진이 뭐이에요?"]}},{"example":{"ko":"제 배경화면에는 산과 나무가 있어요.","en":"My wallpaper has mountains and trees.","parts":[{"ko":"제","en":"my (possessive)"},{"ko":"배경화면","en":"wallpaper (noun)"},{"ko":"에는","en":"on/in (location 에 + topic 는)"},{"ko":"산과 나무","en":"mountains and trees (noun + and + noun)"},{"ko":"가","en":"subject particle (marks what exists)"},{"ko":"있어요","en":"there is / have (polite present)"}],"grammar_note":"Use ~과 (after consonant) or ~와 (after vowel) to mean ''and'' when joining two nouns in a list. It attaches directly to the first noun."},"practice":{"ko":"친구 배경화면에는 별과 달이 있어요.","en":"My friend''s wallpaper has stars and the moon.","parts":[{"ko":"친구","en":"friend(''s) (noun used as a possessive)"},{"ko":"배경화면","en":"wallpaper (noun)"},{"ko":"에는","en":"on/in (location 에 + topic 는)"},{"ko":"별과 달","en":"stars and moon (noun + and + noun)"},{"ko":"이","en":"subject particle"},{"ko":"있어요","en":"there is (polite present)"}],"choices":["별과 달이 있어요","별와 달이 있어요","별과 달을 있어요","별과 달가 있어요"]}},{"example":{"ko":"여름이 좋아서 해변 사진을 배경화면으로 했어요.","en":"Because I like summer, I set a beach photo as my wallpaper.","parts":[{"ko":"여름","en":"summer (noun)"},{"ko":"이","en":"subject particle"},{"ko":"좋아서","en":"because (I) like (reason: adjective + ~아/어서)"},{"ko":"해변 사진","en":"beach photo (compound noun)"},{"ko":"을","en":"object particle (marks direct object)"},{"ko":"배경화면으로","en":"as wallpaper (noun + ~(으)로 ''as / into'')"},{"ko":"했어요","en":"set / made (past polite)"}],"grammar_note":"~아/어서 connects a reason to a result: ''because ~, (so) ~''. It attaches to a verb or adjective stem (좋다 → 좋아서). Past tense goes only on the final verb, never before 아/어서 (좋아했어서 ✗)."},"practice":{"ko":"고양이를 좋아해서 귀여운 고양이 그림을 배경화면으로 했어요.","en":"Because I like cats, I set a cute cat drawing as my wallpaper.","parts":[{"ko":"고양이","en":"cat (noun)"},{"ko":"를","en":"object particle (after vowel)"},{"ko":"좋아해서","en":"because (I) like (reason: psych-verb + ~아/어서)"},{"ko":"귀여운 고양이 그림","en":"cute cat drawing (adjective + noun compound)"},{"ko":"을","en":"object particle (after consonant)"},{"ko":"배경화면으로","en":"as wallpaper (noun + ~(으)로)"},{"ko":"했어요","en":"set / made (past polite)"}],"choices":["고양이를 좋아해서 귀여운 고양이 그림을 배경화면으로 했어요","고양이를 좋아하지만 귀여운 고양이 그림을 배경화면으로 했어요","고양이를 좋아했어서 귀여운 고양이 그림을 배경화면으로 했어요","고양이를 좋아해도 귀여운 고양이 그림을 배경화면으로 했어요"]}}]}', qa_at = now() where id = 'f74f6bc8-303a-4a94-8443-69a184afcd9c' and md5(patterns_json) = '139af313640c02d622e41ae6636db2f5';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"lessons":[{"example":{"ko":"어릴 때 저는 의사가 되고 싶었어요.","en":"When I was little, I wanted to become a doctor.","parts":[{"ko":"어릴","en":"little/young (descriptive form)"},{"ko":"때","en":"time/when"},{"ko":"저는","en":"I (topic particle)"},{"ko":"의사가","en":"doctor (subject particle)"},{"ko":"되고","en":"become (connective form)"},{"ko":"싶었어요","en":"wanted to (past polite)"}],"grammar_note":"~고 싶다 attaches to a verb stem to express ''want to do [action]''. The ~고 싶 part stays the same; add past tense ~었어요 to say what you wanted in the past."},"practice":{"ko":"어릴 때 저는 운동선수가 되고 싶었어요.","en":"When I was young, I wanted to become an athlete.","parts":[{"ko":"어릴","en":"little/young (descriptive form)"},{"ko":"때","en":"time/when"},{"ko":"저는","en":"I (topic particle)"},{"ko":"운동선수가","en":"athlete (subject particle)"},{"ko":"되고","en":"become (connective form)"},{"ko":"싶었어요","en":"wanted to (past polite)"}],"choices":["어릴 때 저는 운동선수가 되고 싶었어요.","어릴 때 저는 운동선수가 되고 싶어요.","어릴 때 저는 운동선수를 되고 싶었어요.","어릴 때 저는 운동선수가 되기 싶었어요."]}},{"example":{"ko":"언니는 노래도 하고 춤도 추고 싶어 했어요.","en":"My older sister wanted to sing *and* dance too.","parts":[{"ko":"언니는","en":"older sister (topic particle)"},{"ko":"노래도","en":"singing also (additive particle)"},{"ko":"하고","en":"do (connective form)"},{"ko":"춤도","en":"dancing also (additive particle)"},{"ko":"추고","en":"dance (connective form)"},{"ko":"싶어 했어요","en":"wanted to (3rd person past polite)"}],"grammar_note":"~도 means ''also/too'' — attach it to each item in a list to say multiple things are included. Use ~고 between items to connect them naturally."},"practice":{"ko":"형은 그림도 그리고 책도 쓰고 싶어 했어요.","en":"My older brother wanted to draw pictures *and* write books too.","parts":[{"ko":"형은","en":"older brother (topic particle)"},{"ko":"그림도","en":"pictures also (additive particle)"},{"ko":"그리고","en":"draw (connective form)"},{"ko":"책도","en":"books also (additive particle)"},{"ko":"쓰고","en":"write (connective form)"},{"ko":"싶어 했어요","en":"wanted to (3rd person past polite)"}],"choices":["형은 그림도 그리고 책도 쓰고 싶어 했어요.","형은 그림도 그리고 책도 쓰기 싶어 했어요.","형은 그림도 그리고 책을도 쓰고 싶어 했어요.","형은 그림도 그리고 책도 쓰고 싶어요."]}},{"example":{"ko":"꿈을 이루려면 공부를 열심히 해야 돼요.","en":"To achieve your dream, you have to study hard.","parts":[{"ko":"꿈을","en":"dream (object particle)"},{"ko":"이루려면","en":"in order to achieve (conditional form ''if/to'')"},{"ko":"공부를","en":"studying (object particle)"},{"ko":"열심히","en":"hard/diligently (adverb)"},{"ko":"해야","en":"have to do (necessity)"},{"ko":"돼요","en":"becomes/is necessary (polite)"}],"grammar_note":"~(으)려면 means ''in order to / if you want to'' — attach it to a verb to show purpose or condition. Use it when explaining what''s necessary to reach a goal."},"practice":{"ko":"음악가가 되려면 악기를 연습해야 돼요.","en":"To become a musician, you have to practice an instrument.","parts":[{"ko":"음악가가","en":"musician (subject particle)"},{"ko":"되려면","en":"in order to become (conditional form ''if/to'')"},{"ko":"악기를","en":"instrument (object particle)"},{"ko":"연습해야","en":"have to practice (necessity)"},{"ko":"돼요","en":"becomes/is necessary (polite)"}],"choices":["음악가가 되려면 악기를 연습해야 돼요.","음악가가 되려면 악기를 연습하고 싶어요.","음악가를 되려면 악기를 연습해야 돼요.","음악가가 되으려면 악기를 연습해야 돼요."]}}]}', qa_at = now() where id = 'f417f8c5-fed3-468b-8dd6-d7dd322e50bb' and md5(patterns_json) = 'dd312169a8d2a21f7700a74a5013aebc';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '{"lessons":[{"example":{"ko":"저는 책도 좋아해요.","en":"I like books too.","parts":[{"ko":"저는","en":"I (topic)"},{"ko":"책","en":"book (marked with 도 ''also/too'', not an object particle)"},{"ko":"도","en":"also/too (particle — adds to a list)"},{"ko":"좋아해요","en":"like (present polite)"}],"grammar_note":"~도 means ''also'' or ''too'' — attach it to a noun to add that item to an existing list. Used when the speaker likes or does multiple things."},"practice":{"ko":"영화도 재미있어요.","en":"Movies are fun too.","parts":[{"ko":"영화","en":"movie (marked with 도 ''also/too'', not an object particle)"},{"ko":"도","en":"also/too (particle)"},{"ko":"재미있어요","en":"is fun/interesting (present polite)"}],"choices":["영화도 재미있어요.","영화를 재미있어요.","영화가도 재미있어요.","영화도를 재미있어요."]}},{"example":{"ko":"책 읽는 것을 좋아해요.","en":"I like reading books.","parts":[{"ko":"책","en":"books (object of the verb)"},{"ko":"읽는","en":"reading (present participle, modifying the noun 것)"},{"ko":"것","en":"thing/act (nominalizer — turns a verb phrase into a noun)"},{"ko":"을","en":"object particle (attaches to 것 because 것 ends in a consonant)"},{"ko":"좋아해요","en":"like (present polite)"}],"grammar_note":"~는 것을 좋아하다 = ''to like doing [verb]'' — use ~는 것을 when you want to say ''I like the act of [verb]ing''. The 것 (thing) turns the verb into a noun that can take 을/를."},"practice":{"ko":"음악 듣는 것을 좋아해요.","en":"I like listening to music.","parts":[{"ko":"음악","en":"music (object of the verb)"},{"ko":"듣는","en":"listening (present participle)"},{"ko":"것","en":"thing/act (nominalizer)"},{"ko":"을","en":"object particle"},{"ko":"좋아해요","en":"like (present polite)"}],"choices":["음악 듣는 것을 좋아해요.","음악 듣는 것이 좋아해요.","음악 듣은 것을 좋아해요.","음악 들는 것을 좋아해요."]}},{"example":{"ko":"책을 많이 읽으면 똑똑해져요.","en":"If you read many books, you become smart.","parts":[{"ko":"책을","en":"books (object)"},{"ko":"많이","en":"a lot/many (adverb)"},{"ko":"읽으면","en":"if you read (conditional ~(으)면)"},{"ko":"똑똑해져요","en":"become smart (present polite, ~아/어지다 = ''become'')"}],"grammar_note":"~(으)면 means ''if'' — attach to the verb stem. Use it to state a result or consequence: ''If [you do this], then [this happens].'' Shows a general condition, not a time sequence."},"practice":{"ko":"매일 공부하면 성공할 거예요.","en":"If you study every day, you will succeed.","parts":[{"ko":"매일","en":"every day (adverb)"},{"ko":"공부하면","en":"if you study (conditional ~(으)면)"},{"ko":"성공할","en":"will succeed (future tense, ~ㄹ 거예요)"},{"ko":"거예요","en":"것이에요 (contracted); as part of ~ㄹ 거예요, expresses future intention or expectation"}],"choices":["매일 공부하면 성공할 거예요.","매일 공부하으면 성공할 거예요.","매일 공부면 성공할 거예요.","매일 공부하면 성공하을 거예요."]}}]}', qa_at = now() where id = '2a8bbc7a-3af9-433b-b7a0-352ccd550dff' and md5(patterns_json) = 'e4b225312743a345804bd510ac7b6769';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"Verb endings: 아요 / 어요","level":"Beginner (A1)","exp":"The most common polite present tense endings in Korean. 아요 is used when the last vowel of the stem is ㅏ or ㅗ; 어요 is used after any other vowel. (Vowel-final stems usually contract: 가 + 아요 → 가요, 마시 + 어요 → 마셔요.)","structure":"Verb stem + 아요 / 어요","when_to_use":"Use this ending when speaking politely to people you don''t know well, in casual professional settings, or with acquaintances. It''s the standard polite conversational form.","watch_out":"Pay attention to the final vowel of the verb stem to choose between 아요 and 어요. The vowel harmony rule is crucial. Also note that 하다 verbs use 해요 instead.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 학교에 가요.","en":"I go to school."},{"ko":"매일 커피를 마셔요.","en":"I drink coffee every day."},{"ko":"친구를 만나요.","en":"I meet my friend."},{"ko":"숙제를 해요.","en":"I do homework."},{"ko":"책을 읽어요.","en":"I read a book."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 학교에 가어요.","en":"I go to school. (WRONG)","error_type":"Wrong vowel harmony - used 어요 after ㅏ vowel","correction":"저는 학교에 가요. (Use 아요 after ㅏ)"},{"ko":"밥을 먹아요.","en":"I eat. (WRONG)","error_type":"Wrong vowel harmony - used 아요 after ㅓ vowel","correction":"밥을 먹어요. (먹 has ㅓ, so use 어요)"},{"ko":"의자에 앉어요.","en":"I sit on the chair. (WRONG)","error_type":"Wrong vowel harmony - used 어요 after ㅏ vowel","correction":"의자에 앉아요. (앉 has ㅏ, so use 아요)"}]},{"name":"Vowel harmony rule with 아요 / 어요","level":"Beginner (A1)","exp":"A fundamental rule in Korean: 아요 follows stems whose last vowel is ㅏ or ㅗ, while 어요 follows all other vowels (ㅓ, ㅜ, ㅡ, ㅣ, ㅐ, etc.).","structure":"After ㅏ or ㅗ → 아요 / After other vowels → 어요","when_to_use":"Always apply this rule when conjugating regular verbs and adjectives into the polite present tense. 하다 verbs (해요) and irregular verbs are the exceptions.","watch_out":"Check the last vowel of the stem and ignore a final consonant (놀- has ㅗ, 먹- has ㅓ). Stems ending in a vowel contract (보아요 → 봐요, 마시어요 → 마셔요, 쓰어요 → 써요 with ㅡ dropping), and irregular verbs change the stem first (듣다 → 들어요, 돕다 → 도와요, 짓다 → 지어요).","examples":[{"ko":"살아요 (ㅏ) - to live","en":"I live."},{"ko":"놀아요 (ㅗ) - to play","en":"I play."},{"ko":"먹어요 (ㅓ) - to eat","en":"I eat."},{"ko":"읽어요 (ㅣ) - to read","en":"I read."},{"ko":"웃어요 (ㅜ) - to laugh","en":"I laugh."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"놀어요","en":"I play. (WRONG)","error_type":"Applied 어요 after ㅗ vowel","correction":"놀아요 (ㅗ takes 아요)"},{"ko":"먹아요","en":"I eat. (WRONG)","error_type":"Applied 아요 after ㅓ vowel","correction":"먹어요 (ㅓ takes 어요)"},{"ko":"웃아요","en":"I laugh. (WRONG)","error_type":"Applied 아요 after ㅜ vowel","correction":"웃어요 (ㅜ takes 어요)"}]},{"name":"Special case: 하다 verbs with 해요","level":"Beginner (A1)","exp":"Verbs ending in 하다 (do, make, etc.) have a special conjugation. Instead of following regular vowel harmony, they use 해요 as the polite present form, which comes from 하 + 여요.","structure":"Noun/root + 하다 → replace 하다 with 해요 (공부하다 → 공부해요)","when_to_use":"Apply this pattern whenever you encounter a verb ending in 하다. Common examples include 공부하다 (study), 일하다 (work), 청소하다 (clean), 요리하다 (cook).","watch_out":"Don''t apply regular vowel harmony rules to 하다 verbs. Always use 해요: replace 하다 with 해요 (공부하다 → 공부해요).","examples":[{"ko":"공부해요.","en":"I study."},{"ko":"일해요.","en":"I work."},{"ko":"청소해요.","en":"I clean."},{"ko":"요리해요.","en":"I cook."},{"ko":"운동해요.","en":"I exercise."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"공부하아요.","en":"I study. (WRONG)","error_type":"Applied regular vowel harmony instead of special 하다 rule","correction":"공부해요. (All 하다 verbs use 해요)"},{"ko":"일하어요.","en":"I work. (WRONG)","error_type":"Applied regular vowel harmony instead of special 하다 rule","correction":"일해요. (Use 해요 for 하다 verbs)"},{"ko":"청소하다요.","en":"I clean. (WRONG)","error_type":"Forgot to remove 다 before adding ending","correction":"청소해요. (Replace 하다 with 해요)"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'e21cd25d-9a5a-44b9-8166-9a3dd50ca9ae' and md5(patterns_json) = '4e64e7549177e53fb7b938ba1098b703';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"게 되다 — End up / Come to","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Expresses an unintended or gradual change in state/situation. The speaker didn''t plan for something to happen, but it naturally occurred or was forced upon them.","structure":"Verb stem + -게 되다","when_to_use":"When describing situations that happen naturally, unexpectedly, or through circumstances beyond your control. Often used with past tense to show how you ended up in a current situation.","watch_out":"Don''t confuse with ''-게 하다'' (make someone do). ''-게 되다'' describes a change that happens to the subject, while ''-게 하다'' means causing someone to act. Don''t use it for something you simply chose to do — use ''-기로 하다'' for decisions.","examples":[{"ko":"어쩌다 보니 서울에 살게 되었어요.","en":"I ended up living in Seoul (through circumstances)."},{"ko":"병에 걸려서 회사에 못 가게 되었다.","en":"I ended up not being able to go to work because I got sick."},{"ko":"시간이 지나면서 자연스럽게 친해지게 돼요.","en":"As time passes, we naturally come to become close."},{"ko":"졸업 후에 다양한 사람들을 만나게 됐어요.","en":"After graduation, I came to meet various people."},{"ko":"너무 자주 보다 보니 싫어하게 되었다.","en":"I ended up disliking it from seeing it too often."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"엄마가 저를 매일 운동하게 됐어요.","en":"Mom made me exercise every day.","error_type":"게 되다 used with a causer — use 게 하다","correction":"엄마가 저를 매일 운동하게 했어요. (Mom made me exercise every day) or 엄마 때문에 매일 운동하게 됐어요. (Because of Mom, I ended up exercising every day)"},{"ko":"제가 그 책을 읽게 되었다고 결정했어요.","en":"I decided to end up reading that book.","error_type":"Intentional decision with 되다","correction":"''되다'' implies unintentional change. Use ''읽기로 결정했어요'' (I decided to read it) instead. Or ''우연히 그 책을 읽게 되었어요'' (I accidentally ended up reading that book)."},{"ko":"우리 같이 그 일을 하게 됩시다.","en":"Let''s end up doing that work together.","error_type":"''-게 되다'' with a suggestion or command","correction":"''-게 되다'' describes what happens to you, so it doesn''t take suggestions or commands (-ㅂ시다, -(으)세요). Say ''우리 같이 그 일을 합시다.'' (Let''s do that work together.)"}]},{"name":"게 되어 있다 — Be set up / Arranged to","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Expresses a state that has been arranged, prepared, or set up beforehand. The result of prior arrangement or preparation. It can also mean something is bound to happen: 열심히 하면 잘하게 되어 있어요 (If you work hard, you''re bound to get good).","structure":"Verb stem + -게 되어 있다","when_to_use":"When describing situations that are already arranged, prepared, or designed to function a certain way. Often used for systems, schedules, or planned arrangements.","watch_out":"This pattern emphasizes that something IS arranged (state), not the process of becoming arranged. Don''t use for temporary situations or spontaneous occurrences.","examples":[{"ko":"이 기계는 자동으로 작동하게 되어 있어요.","en":"This machine is designed to operate automatically."},{"ko":"회의는 내일 오후 3시에 시작하게 되어 있어요.","en":"The meeting is set to start at 3 PM tomorrow."},{"ko":"모든 학생은 학교 규칙을 따르게 되어 있어요.","en":"All students are required to follow the school rules."},{"ko":"계약서에 따르면 월급은 매달 10일에 나오게 되어 있어요.","en":"According to the contract, salary is arranged to come out on the 10th every month."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"어제 급하게 약속이 잡혀있게 되어 있었어요.","en":"A sudden appointment was arranged yesterday.","error_type":"Using ''-게 되어 있다'' for a sudden one-off event (and stacking it on 잡혀 있다)","correction":"''-게 되어 있다'' implies prior, deliberate arrangement. Use ''약속이 잡혔어요'' (An appointment was made) or ''급하게 약속이 생겼어요'' (A sudden appointment came up)."}]},{"name":"게 되다 with time/gradual change — Eventually / Over time","level":"Intermediate-Advanced","exp":"Emphasizes gradual change or progression over time. Something that wasn''t immediate but happened as time passed and circumstances accumulated.","structure":"Verb stem + -게 되다 (often with time expressions like 시간이 지나면서, 점점, 자꾸, 자주)","when_to_use":"When narrating how situations changed gradually or evolved over a period of time. Common in storytelling, explanations of personal growth, or describing natural progressions.","watch_out":"The gradual meaning comes from time expressions such as 점점, 시간이 지나면서 or -다 보니; ''-게 되다'' itself can also describe a one-time outcome. Don''t confuse it with ''-게 하다'' (make someone do).","examples":[{"ko":"처음엔 어려웠지만 시간이 지나면서 잘하게 됐어요.","en":"At first it was hard, but over time I got good at it."},{"ko":"자꾸 마주치다 보니 친해지게 되었다.","en":"Running into each other frequently, we ended up becoming close."},{"ko":"점점 더 관심이 생기게 되었어요.","en":"I gradually came to develop more interest."},{"ko":"한국에 오래 살면서 한국 음식을 좋아하게 됐어요.","en":"Living in Korea for a long time, I came to like Korean food."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"한국에 오래 살면서 한국 음식을 좋아하게 했어요.","en":"Living in Korea for a long time, I came to like Korean food.","error_type":"Used ''-게 하다'' (make someone do) instead of ''-게 되다''","correction":"한국에 오래 살면서 한국 음식을 좋아하게 됐어요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'd5c977c0-a875-401a-9aa2-b5189034650e' and md5(patterns_json) = '5bd2ae17610ccdf35fbf05f89200a2db';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"겠 — Intention/Guess","level":"Intermediate (A2-B1)","exp":"겠 is a suffix attached to verb stems to express the speaker''s intention, plan, or guess about the future. It conveys what someone will do or what is likely to happen.","structure":"Verb stem + -겠 + ending (다, 어요, etc.)","when_to_use":"When expressing your intention to do something, making a guess or assumption, or stating what will likely happen. It shows stronger intention than -ㄹ 거예요.","watch_out":"Intention 겠 is for the speaker (statements) or the listener (questions). For a guess about the past, use -았/었겠- (힘들었겠어요 ''that must have been hard''), but don''t use 겠 to state your own past action. -ㄹ 거예요 is the more neutral way to talk about plans and predictions.","examples":[{"ko":"내일 일찍 일어나겠어요.","en":"I will (intend to) wake up early tomorrow."},{"ko":"이 문제는 어렵겠네요.","en":"This problem seems like it will be difficult."},{"ko":"날씨가 좋겠어요.","en":"The weather will probably be nice."},{"ko":"제가 도와드리겠습니다.","en":"I will help you (with intention/determination)."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"어제 친구를 만나겠어요.","en":"I met a friend yesterday. (incorrect)","error_type":"Intention -겠- with a past time expression","correction":"어제 친구를 만났어요. (Use the past tense -았/었- for something that already happened)"},{"ko":"어제 시험이 정말 어렵겠어요.","en":"Yesterday''s exam must have been really hard. (incorrect)","error_type":"Guess about the past needs -았/었겠-","correction":"어제 시험이 정말 어려웠겠어요."}]},{"name":"겠 with -ㄹ/-을 (Future Intention Contrast)","level":"Intermediate (B1)","exp":"-ㄹ 거예요 expresses a neutral, objective prediction, while -겠어요 shows personal intention or strong guess. Both refer to the future but differ in nuance.","structure":"-ㄹ 거예요 (neutral prediction) vs. -겠어요 (intention/subjective guess)","when_to_use":"Use 겠 when you want to emphasize your intention or personal determination. Use -ㄹ 거예요 for more objective facts or neutral statements about what will happen.","watch_out":"These are often interchangeable in casual speech, but 겠 implies more personal involvement or conviction, while -ㄹ 거예요 is more detached.","examples":[{"ko":"내일 비가 올 거예요. / 내일 비가 오겠어요.","en":"It will rain tomorrow. (prediction) / It will probably rain tomorrow. (I guess)"},{"ko":"저는 집에 있을 거예요. (factual) / 저는 집에 있겠어요. (intention)","en":"I will be at home. (factual) / I will stay at home. (my plan)"},{"ko":"시간이 걸릴 거야. / 시간이 걸리겠어.","en":"It will take time. / It''s going to take time (I''m guessing)."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"내가 하겠어요.","en":"I will do it. (mixing formal and casual - awkward)","error_type":"Speech level inconsistency","correction":"내가 하겠어. or 제가 하겠어요."}]},{"name":"겠 in Polite/Formal Offers and Promises","level":"Intermediate (B1)","exp":"겠 is frequently used in formal, polite contexts to express offers, promises, or commitments. It softens the tone while showing determination.","structure":"Verb stem + -겠 + 습니다/어요/네요","when_to_use":"In customer service, formal situations, or when making promises. It shows respect and determination simultaneously. Common in business Korean.","watch_out":"겠네요 (added -네요) shows the speaker''s realization or mild surprise, not just intention. Don''t use -겠어? (casual question form) in formal settings.","examples":[{"ko":"열심히 일하겠습니다.","en":"I will work hard. (promise/commitment)"},{"ko":"도와드리겠습니다.","en":"I will help you. (polite offer)"},{"ko":"좋은 서비스를 제공하겠습니다.","en":"We will provide good service. (formal promise)"},{"ko":"비싸겠네요.","en":"That must be expensive. (guess + realization with -네요)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 도와드리겠어?","en":"Will I help you? (incorrect casual question in formal context)","error_type":"Speech level error","correction":"제가 도와드릴까요? or 도와드리겠습니다."},{"ko":"손님, 여기에 앉겠습니다.","en":"Sir/Madam, please sit here. (incorrect)","error_type":"-겠습니다 states the speaker''s own intention; it can''t tell the listener what to do","correction":"손님, 여기에 앉으세요. or 손님, 여기에 앉아 주시겠어요?"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'd290a492-6b4d-46e5-87cf-7fd54a8f797a' and md5(patterns_json) = '090ea427fe12188d0ddf7f0e29b36d0c';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"고 — And (connecting)","level":"Beginner","exp":"고 is a connective ending that links two clauses or actions: it lists related facts or shows actions happening one after another.","structure":"Verb stem + 고 + Verb/Adjective","when_to_use":"When you want to connect multiple actions or states sequentially, or list related items without implying cause-and-effect.","watch_out":"The two clauses can have different subjects (저는 커피를 마시고 동생은 주스를 마셔요). Use 고 for simple connection; use 아서/어서 to show cause, and also after movement verbs (가다, 오다) when the next action happens at the destination (집에 가서 쉬었어요).","examples":[{"ko":"저는 밥을 먹고 학교에 갑니다.","en":"I eat and then go to school."},{"ko":"날씨가 맑고 따뜻합니다.","en":"The weather is clear and warm."},{"ko":"친구를 만나고 영화를 봤어요.","en":"I met a friend and watched a movie."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 학생고 친구는 선생님이에요.","en":"I''m a student, and my friend is a teacher.","error_type":"Noun + 고 without 이다","correction":"저는 학생이고 친구는 선생님이에요. (After a noun, use 이고: noun + 이다 + 고)"},{"ko":"피곤해서 집에 가고 쉬었어요.","en":"Because I was tired, I went home and rested.","error_type":"Used 고 after a movement verb when the next action happens at the destination","correction":"피곤해서 집에 가서 쉬었어요. (가서 = went there and, at home, rested)"}]},{"name":"고 — Listing actions/states (sequential)","level":"Beginner","exp":"고 connects multiple actions that happen in order or multiple descriptive states about the same subject, creating a list-like effect.","structure":"Action/Adjective + 고 + Action/Adjective + 고 + (Final action/adjective)","when_to_use":"When describing a series of consecutive actions or multiple characteristics of one thing in a natural, flowing sequence.","watch_out":"Do not use 고 to show cause-and-effect (use 아서/어서 instead). The final clause determines the tense for the entire sentence.","examples":[{"ko":"아침에 세수하고 밥을 먹고 학교에 갔어요.","en":"In the morning, I washed my face, ate, and went to school."},{"ko":"이 집은 크고 밝고 깨끗합니다.","en":"This house is big, bright, and clean."},{"ko":"노래하고 춤추고 맛있는 음식도 먹었어요.","en":"We sang, danced, and ate delicious food too."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"집에 오고 손을 씻었어요.","en":"I came home and washed my hands.","error_type":"Movement verb + 고 when the next action happens at the destination","correction":"집에 와서 손을 씻었어요. (Use -아/어서 after 오다/가다 when you act at the place you arrive)"},{"ko":"의자에 앉고 친구를 기다렸어요.","en":"I sat on the chair and waited for my friend.","error_type":"Position verb + 고 when the next action happens in that position","correction":"의자에 앉아서 친구를 기다렸어요. (Use -아/어서 after 앉다, 서다, 눕다 when the next action happens in that position)"}]},{"name":"고 vs 지만 — Adding facts vs contrast","level":"Beginner","exp":"고 only adds one fact to another (''and''); it does not express contrast. Two facts that point the same way sound natural with 고. When the second fact clashes with the first, Korean uses -지만 or -(으)ㄴ/는데 instead.","structure":"Stem + 고 (adding: and) / Stem + 지만 (contrast: but)","when_to_use":"Use 고 to list qualities or facts that fit together (cheap and tasty, expensive and poor quality). Switch to 지만 when the second fact is unexpected given the first.","watch_out":"Don''t rely on 고 to imply surprise or contrast — listeners hear it as plain ''and''. 피곤하고 운동했어요 sounds odd; 피곤했지만 운동했어요 is what you mean.","examples":[{"ko":"그 식당은 싸고 맛있어요.","en":"That restaurant is cheap and tasty."},{"ko":"가격이 비싸고 품질도 좋지 않아요.","en":"It''s expensive, and the quality isn''t good either."},{"ko":"그는 똑똑하지만 좀 게을러요.","en":"He is smart, but a bit lazy. (contrast → 지만)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"춥고 밖에 나갔어요.","en":"It was cold, but I went outside.","error_type":"고 used where the two facts contrast","correction":"추웠지만 밖에 나갔어요. (Use 지만 for contrast)"},{"ko":"피곤하고 운동했어요.","en":"I was tired, but I exercised.","error_type":"고 used where the two facts contrast","correction":"피곤했지만 운동했어요. (Use 지만 for contrast)"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '6275e577-0f8a-4474-a96b-561ad995aa2a' and md5(patterns_json) = '91b64c8ccccf900104b85043b5ba7737';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"고 싶다 — Want to","level":"Beginner","exp":"Expresses a desire or wish to do something. Attached to the verb stem to indicate what the speaker wants to do.","structure":"[Verb stem] + 고 싶다","when_to_use":"When you want to express your desire to perform an action. Can be used with various tenses and politeness levels.","watch_out":"Attach 고 싶다 to the verb stem. The tense goes on 싶다 (먹고 싶었어요), not on the verb. With adjectives, Korean usually says -아/어지고 싶다 ''want to become'' (예뻐지고 싶어요), though 행복하고 싶어요 is common. For someone else''s wish, use 고 싶어 하다.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 한국에 가고 싶어요.","en":"I want to go to Korea."},{"ko":"치킨을 먹고 싶습니다.","en":"I want to eat chicken."},{"ko":"영화를 보고 싶어.","en":"I want to watch a movie."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 행복고 싶어요.","en":"I want to be happy.","error_type":"Dropped 하 from the stem (행복하다 → 행복하-)","correction":"저는 행복하고 싶어요. (or 행복해지고 싶어요)"},{"ko":"동생은 게임을 하고 싶어요.","en":"My younger sibling wants to play games.","error_type":"Third-person desire with bare 고 싶다","correction":"동생은 게임을 하고 싶어 해요. (Use 고 싶어 하다 for other people)"}]},{"name":"고 싶어 하다 — To say/show that someone wants to","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Used to report or describe what someone else wants to do. More objective third-person expression than direct observation.","structure":"[Verb stem] + 고 싶어 하다","when_to_use":"When describing what another person wants, based on what they''ve said or shown. Useful for reported speech about desires.","watch_out":"The 하다 verb changes conjugation; 싶다 becomes 싶어. Cannot be used for first-person subjects.","examples":[{"ko":"제 친구는 미국으로 유학 가고 싶어 해요.","en":"My friend wants to study abroad in America."},{"ko":"그녀는 영어를 배우고 싶어 했어요.","en":"She wanted to learn English."},{"ko":"아이가 장난감을 사고 싶어 해요.","en":"The child wants to buy a toy."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 공부하고 싶어 해요.","en":"I want to study.","error_type":"Using 고 싶어 하다 with first person","correction":"저는 공부하고 싶어요"},{"ko":"친구가 커피를 마시고 싶해요.","en":"My friend wants to drink coffee.","error_type":"Incomplete conjugation: 싶다 must become 싶어 before 하다","correction":"친구가 커피를 마시고 싶어 해요."}]},{"name":"고 싶지 않다 — Don''t want to","level":"Beginner","exp":"Negates the desire expressed by 고 싶다. Expresses the opposite of wanting to do something.","structure":"[Verb stem] + 고 싶지 않다 / [Verb stem] + 고 싶어 하지 않다 (for others)","when_to_use":"When expressing that you don''t want to do something, or (with 하다) when describing someone else''s lack of desire.","watch_out":"Negate 싶다, not the main verb: 운동하고 싶지 않아요, not 운동하지 않고 싶어요. In speech, 안 + verb + 고 싶다 is also common (안 가고 싶어요). For other people, use 고 싶어 하지 않다.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 일하고 싶지 않아요.","en":"I don''t want to work."},{"ko":"그 약은 먹고 싶지 않습니다.","en":"I don''t want to take that medicine."},{"ko":"동생은 학교에 가고 싶어 하지 않아요.","en":"My younger sibling doesn''t want to go to school."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 운동하지 않고 싶어요.","en":"I want to not exercise.","error_type":"Incorrect negation structure","correction":"저는 운동하고 싶지 않아요"},{"ko":"민지 씨는 춤추고 싶지 않아요.","en":"Minji doesn''t want to dance.","error_type":"Third-person desire with bare 싶다","correction":"민지 씨는 춤추고 싶어 하지 않아요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '9e6041d8-4c03-45ad-ac95-45368aa96bcb' and md5(patterns_json) = '0f06976b5f0018bf65b3c0f1c7062feb';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"기로 하다 — Decide to","level":"Intermediate (A2-B1)","exp":"기로 하다 expresses a decision or resolution to do something. It indicates that someone has made a deliberate choice about a future action.","structure":"Verb stem + 기로 하다","when_to_use":"When stating that someone has decided, agreed, or made up their mind to perform an action","watch_out":"The decision is typically based on a conscious choice rather than external force. Use with action verbs only, not state verbs.","examples":[{"ko":"내일부터 운동하기로 했어요.","en":"I decided to exercise starting tomorrow."},{"ko":"우리는 함께 영화를 보기로 했습니다.","en":"We decided to watch a movie together."},{"ko":"그는 한국에서 일하기로 결정했어요.","en":"He decided to work in Korea."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 키가 크기로 했어요.","en":"I decided to be tall.","error_type":"State verb used instead of action verb","correction":"기로 하다 only works with action verbs. State verbs like 크다 (to be tall) cannot be used."},{"ko":"운동을 하기를 했어요.","en":"I decided to exercise.","error_type":"Wrong particle: 기로, not 기를","correction":"운동을 하기로 했어요."}]},{"name":"기로 하다 — Agreed Upon Plan","level":"Intermediate (A2-B1)","exp":"기로 하다 can express an agreement or plan that was made between two or more people, often used when discussing what was previously arranged.","structure":"Verb stem + 기로 했다/하다","when_to_use":"When referring to plans, agreements, or decisions that were made beforehand, especially in conversations about what was decided","watch_out":"The past tense (기로 했다) is common when recalling what was agreed. Without a partner named, 기로 했어요 can be a personal decision or a shared plan — add (누구)와/하고 to make an agreement clear (친구하고 만나기로 했어요).","examples":[{"ko":"우리는 매주 목요일에 만나기로 했어요.","en":"We agreed to meet every Thursday."},{"ko":"팀장님과 회의 시간을 4시로 하기로 했습니다.","en":"We agreed with the team leader to have the meeting at 4 o''clock."},{"ko":"다음 달에 여행을 가기로 했어요.","en":"We decided to go on a trip next month."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"다음 주에 친구와 만날 기로 했어요.","en":"I agreed to meet my friend next week.","error_type":"기로 attaches directly to the verb stem","correction":"다음 주에 친구와 만나기로 했어요."},{"ko":"집에서 쉬기로 하고 있어요.","en":"I am deciding to rest at home.","error_type":"Continuous tense misuse","correction":"집에서 쉬기로 했어요 (I decided to rest at home) is more appropriate."}]},{"name":"기로 하다 — Official Decision/Policy","level":"Intermediate-Advanced (B1-B2)","exp":"기로 하다 can be used in formal contexts to express official decisions, policies, or organizational resolutions that have been formally decided.","structure":"Verb stem + 기로 하다/하였다","when_to_use":"In formal announcements, news reports, official statements, or organizational decisions where something has been officially decided or determined","watch_out":"This formal usage often appears in written Korean (news, formal documents). 하였다 is simply the uncontracted written form of 했다 — it is not a different tense. In spoken news, 하기로 했습니다 is standard.","examples":[{"ko":"정부는 새로운 정책을 시행하기로 했습니다.","en":"The government decided to implement a new policy."},{"ko":"회사는 원격근무를 허용하기로 하였다.","en":"The company decided to allow remote work."},{"ko":"학교에서 온라인 수업을 계속하기로 했어요.","en":"The school decided to continue online classes."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"정부가 새 법을 만들기로 하는 중이에요.","en":"The government is in the process of deciding to make a new law.","error_type":"Incorrect tense for official decision","correction":"정부가 새 법을 만들기로 했습니다 (The government decided to make a new law) is more appropriate."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '78d5d6d4-3d09-4673-9267-d6c651c3e84f' and md5(patterns_json) = '80ba932a5ad0316a28f502fba3e41a1d';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"네요 — Surprise/Realization","level":"Beginner","exp":"네요 expresses the speaker''s surprise, realization, or newfound understanding about something. It shows that the speaker has just noticed or learned something. It''s polite and commonly used in everyday conversation.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + 네요 / Noun + (이)네요","when_to_use":"When you discover something unexpected, notice something new, or realize something you didn''t know before. It conveys a sense of ''Oh, I see!'' or ''Wow, I didn''t know that!''","watch_out":"Use 네요 for something you notice right now, first-hand. Don''t use it to tell people facts you already knew (your own name, job) — use -아/어요 then. Past -았/었네요 is fine for a result you have just noticed (벌써 다 먹었네요, 새로 생겼네요). 네요 attaches straight to the stem: 하네요, not 하는네요.","examples":[{"ko":"오늘 날씨가 정말 좋네요!","en":"The weather today is really nice! (I just realized it!)"},{"ko":"이 카페가 새로 생겼네요.","en":"Oh, this café is newly opened! (I just noticed it!)"},{"ko":"한국말을 정말 잘하시네요!","en":"Wow, you speak Korean really well! (I''m just realizing this!)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"제 이름은 민수네요.","en":"My name is Minsu.","error_type":"네요 for information you already knew","correction":"제 이름은 민수예요. (Use 네요 only for something you have just noticed)"},{"ko":"너 학생이네요?","en":"You''re a student? (mixed speech levels)","error_type":"Speech level mismatch: 너 is casual, 요 is polite","correction":"학생이시네요! (polite) or 너 학생이네? (casual)"},{"ko":"공부를 많이 하는네요.","en":"You study a lot.","error_type":"Extra 는 before 네요","correction":"공부를 많이 하네요. (네요 attaches directly to the stem)"}]},{"name":"군요 — Realization/Emphasis","level":"Beginner","exp":"군요 is similar to 네요: it expresses realization, often right after hearing new information (아, 그랬군요). It can carry sympathy or understanding, and it sounds a little more bookish than 네요. It is still polite with 요.","structure":"Adjective stem / -았/었- + 군요; action verb stem + 는군요; noun + (이)군요","when_to_use":"When new information has just sunk in — often as a reply to what someone has told you (아, 그랬군요) — or to show sympathy along with the realization.","watch_out":"Action verbs in the present take -는군요 (먹는군요); adjectives and the past tense take -군요 (바쁘군요, 바빴군요); nouns take -(이)군요 (학생이군요, 의사군요). Keep the speech level consistent: 요 forms go with polite address, not 너.","examples":[{"ko":"정말 바쁘셨군요.","en":"Oh, you were really busy (I see now)! / You must have been really busy."},{"ko":"이게 그렇게 어려웠군요.","en":"Oh, so this was that difficult! (I realize now)"},{"ko":"그 사람이 좋은 사람이었군요.","en":"Oh, that person was a good person (I finally realize)!"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"학생군요.","en":"So you''re a student.","error_type":"Consonant-final noun needs 이군요","correction":"학생이군요."},{"ko":"동생이 매운 음식을 잘 먹군요.","en":"Your younger sibling eats spicy food well, I see.","error_type":"Present action verb needs -는군요","correction":"동생이 매운 음식을 잘 먹는군요."}]},{"name":"는군요 — Realization about Ongoing Actions","level":"Intermediate","exp":"는군요 is used specifically with action verbs to express realization about something that is currently happening or an ongoing state. It emphasizes ''Oh, I see you''re doing that!'' or ''So, that''s what''s happening!''","structure":"Action verb stem + 는군요","when_to_use":"When you notice someone is currently doing something, or when you realize an ongoing action/situation. Different from 네요 because it specifically marks the progressive or habitual nature.","watch_out":"Only use with action verbs, not adjectives or state verbs (unless describing state of action). Don''t confuse with regular 는 present tense marker.","examples":[{"ko":"아, 공부하고 있는군요!","en":"Oh, so you are studying! (I just realized!)"},{"ko":"다들 지금 떠나는군요.","en":"Oh, everyone''s leaving now! (I see it happening!)"},{"ko":"매일 조깅하시는군요.","en":"Oh, you jog every day! (I just realized this habit!)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"그 꽃이 예쁜군요.","en":"So that flower is pretty!","error_type":"Modifier form 예쁜 before 군요","correction":"그 꽃이 예쁘군요. (Adjectives take -군요 directly) or 그 꽃이 예쁘네요."},{"ko":"정말 똑똑하는군요.","en":"So you''re really smart!","error_type":"-는군요 used with an adjective","correction":"정말 똑똑하군요. or 정말 똑똑하네요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '878a9a4b-c49b-460f-a0f5-2b289ec5a616' and md5(patterns_json) = 'd68a637f961efdfeb1b502c97a34af3b';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"는 것 — Nominalization","level":"Beginner","exp":"Turns an action verb into a noun phrase. -는 is the present modifier ending: attached to an action verb stem with 것, it means ''the act of ~'' or ''the thing that ~''. (Adjectives use -(으)ㄴ 것 instead.)","structure":"[Verb stem] + 는 것","when_to_use":"When you want to treat an action or state as a noun, such as making it the subject or object of a sentence, or discussing an activity as a concept.","watch_out":"Use only with present tense verb stems. For past tense, use ''은/ㄴ 것''. For future tense, use ''을/ㄹ 것''. The ''것'' (thing) is necessary; ''는'' alone cannot nominalize a verb.","examples":[{"ko":"읽는 것이 재미있어요.","en":"Reading (the act of reading) is fun."},{"ko":"수영하는 것을 좋아합니다.","en":"I like swimming (the act of swimming)."},{"ko":"그 영화를 보는 것은 시간 낭비예요.","en":"Watching that movie is a waste of time."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"읽는이 재미있어요.","en":"Reading is fun. (Missing ''것'')","error_type":"Missing nominalization marker","correction":"읽는 것이 재미있어요."},{"ko":"읽었는 것을 좋아해요.","en":"I like having read. (Mixing past tense with present nominalization)","error_type":"Incorrect tense marker","correction":"읽은 것을 좋아해요. (OR: 읽는 것을 좋아해요.)"}]},{"name":"은/ㄴ 것 — Past Nominalization","level":"Beginner","exp":"With action verbs, -(으)ㄴ 것 refers to a completed (past) action: ''the thing that was ~ed'' or ''the fact that ~ happened''. Use 은 after consonants and ㄴ after vowels. (With adjectives, -(으)ㄴ is the present form: 좋은 것 ''a good thing''.)","structure":"[Verb/Adjective stem] + 은/ㄴ 것","when_to_use":"When discussing completed actions or states as nouns, describing past events or experiences, or referring to something that already happened.","watch_out":"Choose 은 or ㄴ by whether the stem ends in a consonant or vowel. ㄹ-stems drop the ㄹ (만들다 → 만든 것). With adjectives this same form is present, not past (좋은 것 = the good thing).","examples":[{"ko":"어제 본 것이 정말 재미있었어요.","en":"What I saw yesterday was really fun."},{"ko":"한국에 간 것이 제 인생을 바꿨어요.","en":"Going to Korea changed my life."},{"ko":"어제 먹은 것 때문에 배탈이 났어요.","en":"I got an upset stomach because of what I ate yesterday."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"좋는 것을 샀어요.","en":"I bought what is good. (Using present ''는'' with adjective)","error_type":"Wrong nominalization form for adjective","correction":"좋은 것을 샀어요."},{"ko":"어제 만들은 것을 먹었어요.","en":"I ate what I made yesterday.","error_type":"ㄹ-stem: the ㄹ drops before -ㄴ","correction":"어제 만든 것을 먹었어요."}]},{"name":"을/ㄹ 것 — Future Nominalization","level":"Beginner","exp":"Nominalizes a verb with a future or prospective meaning. Use ''을 것'' after consonants and ''ㄹ 것'' after vowels. Expresses ''what will ~'' or ''the thing that is going to ~'', and it is the base of -(으)ㄹ 것 같다 (guess) and -(으)ㄹ 거예요 (future).","structure":"[Verb stem] + 을/ㄹ 것","when_to_use":"When talking about future plans, things to be done, or predictions.","watch_out":"Use -을 after a consonant, -ㄹ after a vowel; ㄹ-stems add nothing (만들다 → 만들 것). Don''t mix it up with -(으)ㄹ 수 있다 (can), which uses 수, not 것. In speech, 것 is often shortened to 거 (할 거예요).","examples":[{"ko":"내일 할 것은 숙제예요.","en":"What I will do tomorrow is homework."},{"ko":"비가 올 것 같아요.","en":"It looks like it will rain."},{"ko":"너는 성공할 거야.","en":"You will succeed."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"내일 할것을 말했어요.","en":"I told (what I will do tomorrow). (Missing space, unclear meaning)","error_type":"Spacing error / unclear structure","correction":"내일 할 것을 말했어요."},{"ko":"내일 만들을 것을 생각해 봤어요.","en":"I thought about what I''ll make tomorrow.","error_type":"ㄹ-stem: no extra 을","correction":"내일 만들 것을 생각해 봤어요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'dc1ef389-a870-459f-a512-62ac739f8200' and md5(patterns_json) = 'dac5858d12186da6c757ceda63558b2a';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"더라(고요) — I noticed that","level":"Intermediate","exp":"더라 is a retrospective past tense marker used to express something you discovered, realized, or noticed through direct observation or experience. When used with 고요, it conveys a casual, conversational tone of sharing what you found out.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + 더라 / Verb/Adjective stem + 더라고요","when_to_use":"When recounting something you personally observed or discovered in the past; when sharing a realization with a conversational, slightly surprised tone; commonly used in informal storytelling or casual conversation","watch_out":"더라 is only for things YOU directly saw or experienced, not hearsay; 더라고요 is the polite form of casual 더라(고). It isn''t used for your own deliberate actions (저는 어제 공부하더라고요 ✗) — only for what you observed, including your own feelings or discoveries (먹어 보니까 맛있더라고요).","examples":[{"ko":"어제 그 카페에 가봤는데 정말 조용하더라고요.","en":"I went to that café yesterday and I noticed it was really quiet."},{"ko":"한국 음식이 생각보다 맛있더라고요.","en":"I noticed Korean food was more delicious than I thought."},{"ko":"그 영화는 정말 재미있더라고요.","en":"I noticed that movie was really fun."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"제 친구가 한국에 갔다고 더라고요.","en":"My friend went to Korea (I noticed that).","error_type":"Using 더라고요 for hearsay","correction":"더라고요 cannot be used for things you didn''t directly experience. Use: ''제 친구가 한국에 갔다고 했어요'' (My friend told me they went to Korea)"},{"ko":"저는 어제 도서관에서 공부하더라고요.","en":"I studied at the library yesterday.","error_type":"Using 더라 for your own deliberate action","correction":"저는 어제 도서관에서 공부했어요. (더라 reports what you observed, not what you did)"}]},{"name":"더라 — Direct past observation (narrative form)","level":"Intermediate","exp":"더라 (without 고요) is the casual form used with friends, in storytelling and in narration to vividly describe something you directly observed. It creates a retrospective, experiential tone.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + 더라","when_to_use":"In casual speech with friends, or in narration and inner monologue, when describing what you personally witnessed","watch_out":"더라 is the casual (반말) form — use it with friends or in narration; 더라고요 is the polite form for everyday conversation. Still only for personal observations, not hearsay or your own deliberate actions.","examples":[{"ko":"그 순간 하늘이 갑자기 어두워지더라.","en":"At that moment, the sky suddenly darkened (I observed)."},{"ko":"아이가 정말 열심히 공부하더라.","en":"The child was studying really hard (I noticed)."},{"ko":"그 집은 생각보다 훨씬 크더라.","en":"That house was much bigger than I thought (I saw)."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"어제 나는 일찍 자더라.","en":"I went to bed early yesterday.","error_type":"Using 더라 for your own deliberate action","correction":"어제 나는 일찍 잤어."}]},{"name":"더라고 말하다 — To tell/mention what you noticed","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Quoting with 더라고 (하다/말하다) reports someone''s first-hand observation — ''X said that (they found) ~''. It can also refer back to your own earlier comment (내가 말했잖아).","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + 더라고 + 하다/말하다 (했어, 했어요, 말했어요)","when_to_use":"When passing on what someone told you they noticed, or reminding someone of what you said you had noticed","watch_out":"When quoting, drop 요: 맛있더라고 했어요, not 맛있더라고요 했어요. 더라고 했어 can report someone else''s observation, so make the speaker clear from context.","examples":[{"ko":"그 시험 어렵더라고 내가 말했잖아.","en":"I told you that test was hard (from my experience)."},{"ko":"친구가 그 영화 정말 재미있더라고 했어.","en":"My friend said the movie was really fun (that''s what they found)."},{"ko":"직접 가 본 사람들이 다 깨끗하더라고 말했어요.","en":"People who had been there all said it was clean."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"그것이 있을 거더라고 말했어.","en":"I said it would probably exist.","error_type":"Mixing future speculation with 더라","correction":"Use: ''그것이 있을 거 같더라고 말했어'' or simply ''있더라고 말했어'' (I said I noticed it existed)"},{"ko":"친구가 그 식당이 맛있더라고요 했어요.","en":"My friend said that restaurant was delicious.","error_type":"Kept 요 inside the quote","correction":"친구가 그 식당이 맛있더라고 했어요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'e8a8adfd-3986-4fd4-b6b3-16bbc21fb3d6' and md5(patterns_json) = '150320cbac0c2a08e9b6bdb236c805b7';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"도 — Also/Too","level":"Beginner","exp":"도 is a particle that means ''also'' or ''too''. It attaches to nouns to indicate that something is included in addition to something else.","structure":"Noun + 도","when_to_use":"When you want to add something to what has already been mentioned; to show that multiple things share the same quality or action.","watch_out":"도 attaches directly to the noun, whether it ends in a consonant or a vowel. It replaces 이/가 and 을/를 (저도, 커피도 — not 저가도 or 커피를도), but it can follow other particles (학교에서도, 친구한테도). 도 can also mean ''even'' (see below).","examples":[{"ko":"저도 학생이에요.","en":"I am also a student."},{"ko":"커피도 마시고 차도 마셔요.","en":"I drink coffee and also drink tea."},{"ko":"그 영화 봤어? 나도 봤어.","en":"Did you watch that movie? I watched it too."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저 도 학생이에요.","en":"I am also a student. (with space)","error_type":"spacing error","correction":"저도 학생이에요."},{"ko":"학생이 도 공부해요.","en":"The student also studies.","error_type":"particle placement","correction":"학생도 공부해요."}]},{"name":"도 with Multiple Items","level":"Beginner","exp":"When listing multiple items that all share the same quality or action, 도 can be repeated with each noun to show that all of them are included.","structure":"Noun1 + 도 + Verb/Adjective, Noun2 + 도 + Verb/Adjective","when_to_use":"When you want to emphasize that several different things all have the same quality or perform the same action.","watch_out":"Each noun needs 도 attached individually. This is different from using commas alone in English. Make sure the verb/adjective at the end agrees with the sentence structure.","examples":[{"ko":"사과도 맛있고 딸기도 맛있어요.","en":"Apples are delicious and strawberries are also delicious."},{"ko":"한국도 가고 일본도 가고 싶어요.","en":"I want to go to Korea and also go to Japan."},{"ko":"엄마도 오고 아빠도 왔어요.","en":"Mom also came and Dad came too."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"사과도 딸기를도 좋아해요.","en":"I like both apples and strawberries.","error_type":"도 stacked on the object particle 를","correction":"사과도 딸기도 좋아해요. (도 replaces 을/를)"},{"ko":"한국도 일본도 가서 싶어요.","en":"I want to go to Korea and Japan.","error_type":"incorrect verb form","correction":"한국도 가고 일본도 가고 싶어요."}]},{"name":"도 — Even (Emphatic meaning)","level":"Intermediate","exp":"도 can also mean ''even'' when used to emphasize that something unexpected or extreme is included. This gives an emphatic tone rather than simply adding to a list.","structure":"Noun + 도 + Verb/Adjective","when_to_use":"When you want to express surprise or emphasis that even something unexpected is true; often used with negative statements or extreme situations.","watch_out":"This meaning of 도 relies on context. The same structure can mean ''also'' or ''even'' depending on the surrounding words and tone. Be careful not to overuse this—context must make the emphatic meaning clear.","examples":[{"ko":"아이도 할 수 있어요.","en":"Even a child can do it."},{"ko":"그걸 모르는 사람도 있어요.","en":"There are even people who don''t know that."},{"ko":"그는 밤 12시에도 일해요.","en":"He even works at midnight."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"그는 밤 12시도 일해요.","en":"He even works at midnight.","error_type":"Dropped 에 from a time expression","correction":"그는 밤 12시에도 일해요. (Keep 에 and add 도: 에도)"},{"ko":"아이에도 할 수 있어요.","en":"Even a child can do it.","error_type":"incorrect particle combination","correction":"아이도 할 수 있어요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '191204ff-a46d-4886-a1f3-ac7b9ffb41a9' and md5(patterns_json) = '23e0e1b65dd70660965f732cb9c239e9';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"동사 + -는 모습 / 형용사 + -(으)ㄴ 모습 (moseup)","level":"Intermediate","exp":"This pattern describes the appearance, scene, or state of someone doing an action or being in a condition. It captures a visual moment or the way something looks while an action is happening or a state exists. It emphasizes observation of the actual scene or appearance.","structure":"Verb stem + -는 모습 / Adjective stem + -(으)ㄴ 모습 (past scene: -(으)ㄴ / -던 모습)","when_to_use":"When describing what something looks like while doing an action, or the appearance/state of being in a condition","watch_out":"For a scene in the past, use -(으)ㄴ or -던 모습 (예전에 웃던 모습), never -았/었는 모습. 모습 means ''appearance/figure'', so this pattern is about what you can see.","examples":[{"ko":"아이가 열심히 공부하는 모습이 참 예쁘다.","en":"The sight of the child studying hard is really beautiful."},{"ko":"그녀가 춤을 추는 모습을 봤어요.","en":"I saw her dancing (watched the scene of her dancing)."},{"ko":"친구가 행복한 모습을 보니 저도 기뻤어요.","en":"Seeing my friend look happy made me happy too."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"아이가 열심히 공부했는 모습이 예쁘다.","en":"The sight of the child studied hard is beautiful.","error_type":"TENSE","correction":"아이가 열심히 공부하는 모습이 예쁘다."},{"ko":"그녀가 춤을 추는 모습을 봤은 것 같아요.","en":"I think I saw the scene of her dancing.","error_type":"CONJUGATION","correction":"그녀가 춤을 추는 모습을 본 것 같아요. (봤은 ✗ → 본)"}]},{"name":"동사 + -는 모양 / 형용사 + -(으)ㄴ 모양 (moyang)","level":"Intermediate","exp":"This pattern makes a guess from what you see or hear: ''it seems that ~''. Unlike -는 모습 (a scene you look at), -는 모양이다 expresses an inference.","structure":"Verb stem + -는 모양이다 / Adjective stem + -(으)ㄴ 모양이다 / Past: Verb stem + -(으)ㄴ 모양이다","when_to_use":"When describing how something appears to be, looks like, or seems to be doing something (with an element of assumption or similarity)","watch_out":"Don''t confuse with -는 모습. Use -는 모양이다 for inferences about other people or situations, not about yourself (저는 배가 고픈 모양이에요 ✗).","examples":[{"ko":"그는 화난 모양이다.","en":"He seems to have gotten angry."},{"ko":"밖에 비가 오는 모양이에요. 우산을 가져가세요.","en":"It seems to be raining outside. Take an umbrella."},{"ko":"일이 잘 진행되는 모양이에요.","en":"It seems like the work is progressing well."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"그 사람이 요즘 바쁘는 모양이에요.","en":"That person seems busy these days.","error_type":"CONJUGATION","correction":"그 사람이 요즘 바쁜 모양이에요. (Adjectives take -(으)ㄴ 모양)"},{"ko":"저는 배가 고픈 모양이에요.","en":"I seem to be hungry.","error_type":"Inference about yourself","correction":"저는 배가 고파요. (You know your own state; -는 모양이다 is for guesses about others)"}]},{"name":"동사 + -는 중 / 명사 + 중 (jung)","level":"Intermediate","exp":"This pattern indicates that an action is currently in progress or in the middle of happening. It describes an ongoing process or state at a specific moment. It focuses on the temporal aspect (during/in the process of) rather than the visual appearance.","structure":"Verb stem + -는 중이다 / Noun + 중이다","when_to_use":"When describing something that is happening right now or in the middle of an action; emphasizes the ongoing process","watch_out":"Use with action verbs (공부하는 중) or activity nouns (회의 중, 수업 중, 통화 중). It doesn''t work with adjectives (바쁘는 중 ✗) or with verbs that aren''t a process, like 알다 or 있다.","examples":[{"ko":"지금 회의 중입니다.","en":"We''re in a meeting right now (the meeting is in progress)."},{"ko":"밥을 먹는 중에 전화가 왔어요.","en":"A call came while I was eating (in the middle of eating)."},{"ko":"그는 숙제를 하는 중이에요.","en":"He is in the middle of doing his homework."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"요즘 바쁘는 중이에요.","en":"I''m busy these days.","error_type":"-는 중 with an adjective","correction":"요즘 바빠요. (Use the adjective on its own)"},{"ko":"지금 밥을 먹은 중이에요.","en":"I''m eating right now.","error_type":"CONJUGATION","correction":"지금 밥을 먹는 중이에요. (-는 중, not -(으)ㄴ 중)"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'ca5f782d-dc2a-48b2-b8a8-b8f509fb335b' and md5(patterns_json) = '88789a33216d67577c299c21f6c9c28a';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"든지 — Whatever/Whenever","level":"Intermediate","exp":"든지 is a suffix that attaches to verbs, adjectives, or nouns to express an indefinite choice or condition. It means ''whatever,'' ''whenever,'' ''whichever,'' or ''any.'' It indicates that the outcome or action applies regardless of which option is chosen.","structure":"[Verb/Adjective stem] + 든지 / [Noun] + 이든지/든지","when_to_use":"Use 든지 when you want to say something applies to any choice, any time, any place, or any person. It''s often used in conditional or general statements where the specific option doesn''t matter.","watch_out":"When attaching to nouns, use ''이든지'' after consonants and ''든지'' after vowels. 든지 is often shortened to 든 (언제든, 누구든). For ''not ... any'', use 아무 ~도 with a negative, not 든지. Don''t confuse 든지 with ''-거나'' (or); 든지 emphasizes ''any/whatever'' while ''-거나'' presents alternatives.","examples":[{"ko":"언제든지 전화하세요.","en":"Call me whenever (at any time)."},{"ko":"가고 싶은 곳이 있으면 어디든지 가세요.","en":"Wherever you want to go, go ahead."},{"ko":"누구든지 이 문제를 풀 수 있어요.","en":"Anyone can solve this problem."},{"ko":"먹고 싶은 거 뭐든지 먹어.","en":"Eat whatever you want."},{"ko":"빨간색이든 파란색이든 괜찮아요.","en":"Whether it''s red or blue (any color), it''s fine."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"책든지 잡지든지 다 괜찮아요.","en":"A book or a magazine — either is fine.","error_type":"Consonant-final noun needs 이든지","correction":"책이든지 잡지든지 다 괜찮아요."},{"ko":"어디든지 안 가고 싶어요.","en":"I don''t want to go anywhere.","error_type":"든지 used with a negative meaning ''not anywhere''","correction":"아무 데도 가고 싶지 않아요. (Use 아무 ~도 + negative for ''not ... any'')"}]},{"name":"No matter what/who/when — question word + -아/어도 or -든지","level":"Upper-Intermediate","exp":"To say ''no matter what/who/when/which'', use a question word with -아/어도 (뭘 먹어도, 누가 와도) or with -든지 (어느 쪽을 선택하든지). The two endings are alternatives — they are not stacked on the same verb.","structure":"Question word ... + Verb stem + -아/어도 / Question word ... + Verb stem + -든지","when_to_use":"Use this pattern to express that a result or statement is true regardless of which condition or choice is made. It''s common in statements about inevitable outcomes or unchangeable situations.","watch_out":"-아/어도 follows vowel harmony (먹어도, 가도, 해도); -든지 attaches straight to the stem (오든지, not 와든지). Don''t add both to one verb.","examples":[{"ko":"뭘 먹어도 소화가 잘 돼요.","en":"No matter what I eat, my digestion is good."},{"ko":"누가 와도 똑같아요.","en":"It''s the same no matter who comes."},{"ko":"어느 쪽을 선택하든지 결과는 같아요.","en":"Whichever side you choose, the result is the same."},{"ko":"언제 와도 환영이에요.","en":"You''re welcome no matter when you come."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"뭘 먹어 소화가 잘 돼요.","en":"No matter what I eat, my digestion is good.","error_type":"Missing -도 (the ''even if'' marker)","correction":"뭘 먹어도 소화가 잘 돼요."},{"ko":"누가 와든지 결과는 같아.","en":"No matter who comes, the result is the same.","error_type":"든지 attached to the conjugated form 와 instead of the stem 오-","correction":"누가 오든지 결과는 같아. (or 누가 와도 결과는 같아)"}]},{"name":"든지 in compound expressions (Multiple options with 든지)","level":"Upper-Intermediate","exp":"든지 can be repeated in a single sentence to list multiple independent options or choices, creating a sense of ''whether...or...whether.'' This creates a comprehensive expression covering all possibilities without specifying which is chosen.","structure":"[Option 1] + 든지, [Option 2] + 든지... [Main clause]","when_to_use":"Use this pattern when listing several alternatives and indicating that the statement applies to any or all of them. It''s useful for comprehensive statements that cover multiple scenarios.","watch_out":"When using multiple 든지, maintain consistent structure and grammar. Each option should be properly formatted before 든지. Don''t mix 든지 with other connectors like -거나 in the same list.","examples":[{"ko":"차든지, 커피든지, 뭐든지 괜찮습니다.","en":"Whether it''s tea, coffee, or whatever—anything is fine."},{"ko":"버스든지, 지하철이든지, 택시든지 타고 와도 돼요.","en":"Whether you take a bus, subway, or taxi, it''s all okay."},{"ko":"한국 음식이든지, 중국 음식이든지, 일식이든지 다 좋아해요.","en":"Whether it''s Korean, Chinese, or Japanese food—I like them all."},{"ko":"주말이든지, 평일이든지 언제든지 와도 된다.","en":"Whether it''s a weekend or weekday, anytime is fine."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"차든지, 커피거나 뭐든지 괜찮아요.","en":"Whether it''s tea, or coffee, or whatever—it''s fine.","error_type":"Mixing 든지 with -거나 (and 거나 after a noun would need 이거나)","correction":"차든지, 커피든지, 뭐든지 괜찮아요. (Use 든지 consistently throughout)"},{"ko":"버스든지, 지하철든지, 택시든지 타고 와도 돼요.","en":"Bus, subway, or taxi — any is fine.","error_type":"Consonant-final noun needs 이든지","correction":"버스든지, 지하철이든지, 택시든지 타고 와도 돼요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '1cdb10c8-b8b2-4582-8b74-efb891435252' and md5(patterns_json) = 'f6ebfae2efbff6d9c4546964953cdc7d';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"때문에 — Because of","level":"Intermediate","exp":"A noun-based grammar pattern used to express the reason or cause of something. It attaches to nouns to indicate ''because of'' or ''due to''.","structure":"Noun + 때문에","when_to_use":"When you want to explain why something happened or the reason behind an action or situation, focusing on external causes or circumstances.","watch_out":"Nouns take 때문에 directly (날씨 때문에). Verbs and adjectives take -기 때문에 (비가 오기 때문에, 바쁘기 때문에). Don''t use 때문에 before a command or suggestion — use -(으)니까 there.","examples":[{"ko":"날씨 때문에 소풍이 취소되었어요.","en":"The picnic was cancelled because of the weather."},{"ko":"교통 체증 때문에 늦었어요.","en":"I was late because of traffic congestion."},{"ko":"병 때문에 학교에 못 갔어요.","en":"I couldn''t go to school because of illness."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"비가 와요 때문에 우산을 가져갔어요.","en":"I brought an umbrella because it rains.","error_type":"Using verb directly with 때문에","correction":"비 때문에 우산을 가져갔어요. or 비가 오기 때문에 우산을 가져갔어요. (Use the noun 비, or -기 때문에 with the verb)"},{"ko":"바쁘 때문에 못 왔어요.","en":"I couldn''t come because I was busy.","error_type":"Adjective stem attached directly to 때문에","correction":"바빴기 때문에 못 왔어요. or 바빠서 못 왔어요. (Use -기 때문에 or -아/어서)"}]},{"name":"Verb/Adjective + -(으)ㄴ/는 것 + 때문에 — Because of (doing/being)","level":"Advanced","exp":"An extended pattern where verbs or adjectives are nominalized using -(으)ㄴ/는 것, then followed by 때문에 to express cause and effect with actions or states.","structure":"Verb + -(으)ㄴ/는 것 + 때문에 or Adjective + -(으)ㄴ/는 것 + 때문에","when_to_use":"When the cause is an action or a state expressed by a verb or adjective, and you need to explain why something happened as a result of that action or state.","watch_out":"Use -(으)ㄴ 것 for past actions and -는 것 for present/habitual actions. For a good result, 덕분에 sounds more natural than 때문에 (열심히 공부한 덕분에). In many cases plain -기 때문에 is simpler (늦게 자기 때문에).","examples":[{"ko":"너무 늦게 자는 것 때문에 피곤해요.","en":"I''m tired because of sleeping too late."},{"ko":"약속을 잊어버린 것 때문에 친구가 화가 났어요.","en":"My friend got angry because I forgot our appointment."},{"ko":"운동을 하지 않은 것 때문에 살이 쪘어요.","en":"I gained weight because of not exercising."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"늦게 자 때문에 피곤해요.","en":"I''m tired because of sleep late.","error_type":"Missing nominalization with -ㄴ/는 것","correction":"늦게 자는 것 때문에 피곤해요. (Need to nominalize the verb)"},{"ko":"어제 늦게 자는 것 때문에 오늘 피곤해요.","en":"I''m tired today because I went to bed late yesterday.","error_type":"Present -는 것 used for a past action","correction":"어제 늦게 잔 것 때문에 오늘 피곤해요. (Use -(으)ㄴ 것 for a past action)"}]},{"name":"Contrast: 때문에 vs. -아/어서 vs. -니까","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Understanding the differences between 때문에 (because of - noun-focused, emphasis on cause), -아/어서 (because - more natural/narrative), and -니까 (because/since - often implies consequence or assumption).","structure":"Compare usage contexts of 때문에, -아/어서, and -니까 in similar sentences","when_to_use":"Use 때문에 when emphasizing an external cause or circumstance; use -아/어서 for sequential cause-and-effect in conversation; use -니까 when explaining reasoning or when the listener might not know the reason.","watch_out":"They are not always interchangeable. -아/어서 and 때문에 can''t be followed by a command or suggestion (-(으)세요, -(으)ㅂ시다, -자); use -(으)니까 there. -아/어서 also can''t take past tense before it (늦었어서 ✗).","examples":[{"ko":"날씨 때문에 취소됐어요.","en":"It was cancelled because of the weather. (Emphasizes the cause itself)"},{"ko":"날씨가 안 좋아서 취소됐어요.","en":"It was cancelled because the weather was bad. (More natural, sequential cause-effect)"},{"ko":"날씨가 안 좋으니까 취소해야 돼요.","en":"Since the weather is bad, we have to cancel. (Reasoning/consequence-oriented)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"날씨가 안 좋아서 취소합시다.","en":"The weather is bad, so let''s cancel.","error_type":"-아/어서 before a suggestion","correction":"날씨가 안 좋으니까 취소합시다."},{"ko":"비 때문에 우산을 가져가세요.","en":"Take an umbrella because of the rain.","error_type":"때문에 before a command","correction":"비가 오니까 우산을 가져가세요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'd07881e8-08da-4928-bb3a-fdca085a4fa2' and md5(patterns_json) = 'a9088fa9a9c799665d4367251fba5200';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"마다 — Every/Each","level":"Beginner","exp":"마다 is a particle that means ''every'' or ''each''. It attaches directly to nouns to indicate that something happens repeatedly for each instance of that noun.","structure":"[Noun] + 마다","when_to_use":"Use 마다 when you want to express that an action or state applies to every single instance or occurrence of something. It''s commonly used with time expressions, objects, or people.","watch_out":"마다 attaches directly to the noun without particles. Don''t confuse it with 마다가 (incorrect). The noun before 마다 should be singular, even when referring to multiple instances.","examples":[{"ko":"아침마다 커피를 마셔요.","en":"I drink coffee every morning."},{"ko":"주말마다 친구를 만나요.","en":"I meet my friend every weekend."},{"ko":"학생마다 다른 의견이 있어요.","en":"Each student has a different opinion."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"매일마다 커피를 마셔요.","en":"I drink coffee every day. (Double marking)","error_type":"Redundancy: 매- already means ''every''","correction":"매일 커피를 마셔요. OR 날마다 커피를 마셔요."},{"ko":"주말마다가 친구를 만나요.","en":"I meet my friend every weekend. (Incorrect particle attachment)","error_type":"Incorrect attachment","correction":"주말마다 친구를 만나요."}]},{"name":"마다 with Time Expressions","level":"Beginner","exp":"마다 is frequently used with time-related nouns (day, week, month, hour, etc.) to indicate regular recurring intervals or schedules.","structure":"[Time noun] + 마다 + [Verb/Action]","when_to_use":"Use this pattern when describing habitual actions, routines, or events that occur at regular time intervals (daily, weekly, hourly, etc.).","watch_out":"Don''t add 에 to the time noun before 마다 (3시간에 마다 ✗), and don''t add 에 after it (월요일마다에 ✗).","examples":[{"ko":"3시간마다 약을 먹어요.","en":"I take medicine every 3 hours."},{"ko":"월요일마다 회의가 있어요.","en":"There is a meeting every Monday."},{"ko":"여름마다 휴가를 가요.","en":"I go on vacation every summer."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"3시간에 마다 약을 먹어요.","en":"I take medicine every 3 hours. (Incorrect particle usage)","error_type":"Particle misuse","correction":"3시간마다 약을 먹어요."},{"ko":"월요일마다에 회의가 있어요.","en":"There is a meeting every Monday. (Extra particle)","error_type":"Extra particle","correction":"월요일마다 회의가 있어요."}]},{"name":"마다 with Countable Objects/People","level":"Beginner","exp":"마다 can attach to nouns representing countable objects or people to mean ''each'' or ''every one''. This emphasizes that something applies individually to each item or person in a group.","structure":"[Countable noun] + 마다 + [Characteristic/Action]","when_to_use":"Use this pattern when you want to emphasize that each individual item or person in a group shares the same characteristic, receives the same treatment, or performs the same action.","watch_out":"This pattern works best with countable nouns (people, objects, items). With abstract concepts, it may sound unnatural. Don''t use 마다 with plural markers simultaneously.","examples":[{"ko":"학생마다 책 한 권씩 받았어요.","en":"Each student received one book."},{"ko":"사람마다 의견이 달라요.","en":"Every person has a different opinion."},{"ko":"상점마다 가격이 다릅니다.","en":"Each store has different prices."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"학생들마다 책을 받았어요.","en":"Each student received a book. (Mixing plural marker with 마다)","error_type":"Redundant plural marking","correction":"학생마다 책을 받았어요."},{"ko":"사과 마다 무게가 달라요.","en":"Each apple has different weight. (Space between noun and 마다)","error_type":"Spacing error","correction":"사과마다 무게가 달라요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '0574e2ad-d23e-4b3a-9d84-b48a23e064f6' and md5(patterns_json) = '22d2026a77f60793123878d0157c82c9';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"말고 — Not that, but","level":"Intermediate","exp":"말고 is used to present an alternative or contrast between two options. It means ''not that one, but this one instead'' or ''instead of that.''","structure":"Noun + 말고 + Alternative noun/action","when_to_use":"When you want to reject one option and suggest a different one. Used between nouns or with verbs in noun form.","watch_out":"With nouns, 말고 comes straight after the noun (커피 말고). Adjectives must modify a noun first (예쁜 옷 말고). With verbs, use -지 말고 (see below). Don''t confuse it with 말고도, which means ''in addition to''.","examples":[{"ko":"커피 말고 차를 마시고 싶어요.","en":"I want to drink tea, not coffee."},{"ko":"버스 말고 택시를 탈래요.","en":"I''ll take a taxi instead of the bus."},{"ko":"게임 말고 공부해야 해.","en":"You should study instead of playing games."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"예쁜 말고 귀여운 옷을 원해요.","en":"I want cute clothes, not pretty ones. (WRONG)","error_type":"Using 말고 with adjectives","correction":"예쁜 옷 말고 귀여운 옷을 원해요. (Put the noun after the adjective: cute clothes, not pretty ones.)"},{"ko":"밥을 먹고 말고 자고 싶어요.","en":"I want to sleep instead of eating.","error_type":"Verb + 고 말고 instead of -지 말고","correction":"밥 먹지 말고 그냥 자고 싶어요. (or 밥 먹는 것 말고 자고 싶어요)"}]},{"name":"말고 — Offering alternatives in requests/suggestions","level":"Intermediate","exp":"말고 is used in polite requests or suggestions to present what you don''t want and what you prefer instead. It softens the rejection of one option.","structure":"Noun + 말고 + Preferred noun + (verb/request)","when_to_use":"In casual conversations, requests, or suggestions when politely declining one option in favor of another. Common in restaurants, shops, or daily interactions.","watch_out":"The tone stays neutral and polite. Don''t use 말고 alone as a response; say what you want instead. Adjectives need a noun (큰 사이즈 말고).","examples":[{"ko":"라면 말고 우동 주세요.","en":"Not ramen, but udon please."},{"ko":"치즈 케이크 말고 초콜릿 케이크로 할 수 있어요?","en":"Can I get chocolate cake instead of cheesecake?"},{"ko":"주말 말고 평일에 만나자.","en":"Let''s meet on a weekday, not on the weekend."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"크다 말고 작은 사이즈 주세요.","en":"Give me a small size, not big. (WRONG)","error_type":"Using 말고 with an adjective instead of nominalized form","correction":"큰 사이즈 말고 작은 사이즈 주세요. (Use noun forms: big size vs. small size)"}]},{"name":"-지 말고 — Don''t do A, do B instead","level":"Intermediate","exp":"With verbs, use -지 말고 to say what not to do and what to do instead. It comes from 말다 (stop / not do), the same verb as in -지 마세요.","structure":"Verb stem + -지 말고 + alternative action","when_to_use":"When advising or asking someone not to do one thing and to do another instead. The second clause is usually a command or suggestion (-(으)세요, -아/어, -자).","watch_out":"Verbs take -지 말고 — never -는 말고. With a noun, plain 말고 is enough (공부 말고 운동하자).","examples":[{"ko":"떠들지 말고 조용히 해요.","en":"Don''t make noise; be quiet."},{"ko":"자꾸 걱정하지 말고 긍정적으로 생각해.","en":"Stop worrying so much; think positively instead."},{"ko":"남의 말만 듣지 말고 직접 확인해 봐.","en":"Don''t just listen to what others say; check it yourself."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"늦게 자는 말고 일찍 자.","en":"Don''t go to bed late; go to bed early.","error_type":"Verb + 는 말고 instead of -지 말고","correction":"늦게 자지 말고 일찍 자."},{"ko":"공부 하는 말고 일하자.","en":"Let''s work instead of studying.","error_type":"Verb + 는 말고 instead of -지 말고","correction":"공부하지 말고 일하자. or 공부 말고 일하자."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'da572f34-7236-478c-b78c-73ea22d3d370' and md5(patterns_json) = 'd50724c9d38ffc113af28045427c61d7';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"미래 예측 ''-ㄹ 것 같아요'' (Future Prediction ''-ㄹ geot gata-yo'')","level":"Intermediate","exp":"This pattern expresses a prediction or guess about what will happen in the future based on current signs or evidence. It combines the future tense marker ''-ㄹ'' with ''것 같아요'' (seems like) to indicate ''it seems like... will happen'' or ''I think... will happen.''","structure":"Verb stem + -ㄹ 것 같아요 / Verb stem + -을 것 같아요 (if stem ends in consonant)","when_to_use":"When making educated guesses or predictions about future events based on present observations or weather forecasts.","watch_out":"Don''t confuse this with present prediction ''-는 것 같아요'' (present situation) or forget to use ''-ㄹ'' for the future tense marker before ''것 같아요''.","examples":[{"ko":"내일 비가 올 것 같아요.","en":"It seems like it will rain tomorrow."},{"ko":"그 영화는 재미있을 것 같아요.","en":"I think that movie will be interesting."},{"ko":"민지 씨는 늦을 것 같아요.","en":"It looks like Minji will be late."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"내일 비가 오을 것 같아요.","en":"It seems like it will rain tomorrow. (incorrect)","error_type":"CONJUGATION","correction":"내일 비가 올 것 같아요. (Vowel stem 오 + ㄹ → 올)"},{"ko":"내일은 날씨가 춥을 것 같아요.","en":"It looks like it will be cold tomorrow. (incorrect)","error_type":"CONJUGATION","correction":"내일은 날씨가 추울 것 같아요. (ㅂ irregular: 춥다 → 추울)"}]},{"name":"현재 상황 추측 ''-는 것 같아요'' (Present Situation Guess ''-neun geot gata-yo'')","level":"Intermediate","exp":"This pattern expresses a guess or prediction about what is happening right now or a present state based on current evidence. It uses ''-는 것 같아요'' to mean ''it seems like... is happening'' or ''it looks like... right now.''","structure":"Verb stem + -는 것 같아요 / Adjective stem + -(으)ㄴ 것 같아요 (-은 after a consonant: 많은; -ㄴ after a vowel: 바쁜)","when_to_use":"When making observations or guesses about current situations, present conditions, or what someone is doing at this moment.","watch_out":"-는 것 같아요 is for present situations and -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 for future predictions. (-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 can also be a softer present guess with less evidence: 지금 바쁠 것 같아요 ''he''s probably busy''.) Adjectives never take -는, except those ending in 있다/없다 (재미있는 것 같아요, 맛없는 것 같아요).","examples":[{"ko":"그는 지금 바쁜 것 같아요.","en":"He seems to be busy right now."},{"ko":"누가 집 밖에 서 있는 것 같아요.","en":"It looks like someone is standing outside the house."},{"ko":"그 상황은 어려운 것 같아요.","en":"That situation seems to be difficult."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"그는 지금 바쁘는 것 같아요.","en":"He seems to be busy right now. (incorrect)","error_type":"CONJUGATION","correction":"그는 지금 바쁜 것 같아요. (Adjectives take -(으)ㄴ, not -는)"},{"ko":"누가 집 밖에 설 것 같아요.","en":"It looks like someone is standing outside the house. (incorrect)","error_type":"TENSE","correction":"누가 집 밖에 서 있는 것 같아요. (설 것 같아요 means ''will stand'')"}]},{"name":"미래 의도/계획 ''-ㄹ 거예요'' (Future Intent/Plan ''-ㄹ geoyeyo'')","level":"Intermediate","exp":"This pattern states a plan or intention, and also a confident prediction (내일 비가 올 거예요). Compared with ''-ㄹ 것 같아요'', which is a softer, more tentative guess, ''-ㄹ 거예요'' sounds more certain.","structure":"Verb stem + -ㄹ 거예요 / Verb stem + -을 거예요 (if stem ends in consonant)","when_to_use":"When stating your plans, asking about someone''s plans, or making a fairly confident prediction.","watch_out":"For your own plans, ''-ㄹ 거예요'' sounds decided, while ''-ㄹ 것 같아요'' would sound unsure. It is spelled 거예요 (not 거에요).","examples":[{"ko":"내일 서울에 갈 거예요.","en":"I''m going to go to Seoul tomorrow."},{"ko":"이번 주말에 영화를 볼 거예요.","en":"I''m going to watch a movie this weekend."},{"ko":"주말에 뭐 할 거예요?","en":"What are you going to do this weekend?"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"이번 주말에 영화를 보을 거예요.","en":"I''m going to watch a movie this weekend. (incorrect)","error_type":"CONJUGATION","correction":"이번 주말에 영화를 볼 거예요. (Vowel stem 보 + ㄹ → 볼)"},{"ko":"내일 친구를 만날 거에요.","en":"I''m going to meet a friend tomorrow. (incorrect)","error_type":"SPELLING","correction":"내일 친구를 만날 거예요. (거예요, not 거에요)"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'f14d10ed-a233-454e-b84d-fe864ed83c03' and md5(patterns_json) = '95f177dac09a47ca4be936a236e748c3';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"밖에 + negative — Only/Nothing but","level":"Intermediate (A2-B1)","exp":"This pattern uses 밖에 (outside/except) with a negative verb or adjective to mean ''only,'' ''nothing but,'' or ''can only.'' It emphasizes limitation or exclusivity. The particle 밖에 always requires a negative form (안/못) that follows it.","structure":"Noun + 밖에 + negative verb/adjective (안 되다, 못 하다, 없다, 모르다, etc.)","when_to_use":"When you want to emphasize that something is limited to only one option, person, or situation; when expressing ''nothing but'' or ''can only''","watch_out":"Always requires a negative form after 밖에. Cannot be used with affirmative verbs. 밖에 is different from 외에 (in addition to/besides). 밖에 cannot stand alone - must be followed by a negative predicate.","examples":[{"ko":"나는 커피밖에 못 마신다.","en":"I can only drink coffee. (Nothing but coffee.)"},{"ko":"시간이 5분밖에 없어요.","en":"I only have 5 minutes."},{"ko":"이 방법밖에 없다.","en":"There is no way but this method. (This is the only way.)"},{"ko":"그 사람밖에 못 믿어요.","en":"I can only trust that person."},{"ko":"주말밖에 시간이 없어서 못 갔어요.","en":"I only had time on weekends, so I couldn''t go."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"나는 커피밖에 마신다.","en":"I drink only coffee. (INCORRECT)","error_type":"Missing negative form","correction":"나는 커피밖에 못 마신다. / 나는 커피밖에 안 마신다."},{"ko":"시간이 5분밖에 안 없어요.","en":"I only have 5 minutes. (INCORRECT)","error_type":"Added 안 before 없다 — 없다 is already the negative","correction":"시간이 5분밖에 없어요."},{"ko":"그 사람 밖에 믿어요.","en":"I trust only that person. (INCORRECT)","error_type":"Missing negative form","correction":"그 사람밖에 못 믿어요. / 그 사람밖에 안 믿어요."}]},{"name":"밖에 + 없다 — Only/Nothing but (existence negation)","level":"Intermediate (A2-B1)","exp":"A specific use of the 밖에 pattern where 없다 (doesn''t exist/don''t have) is the negative form. This emphasizes the exclusive existence or availability of something.","structure":"Noun + 밖에 + 없다 (in various tenses/forms)","when_to_use":"When stating that only one thing exists, is available, or is possible; expressing ''there is only'' or ''we only have''","watch_out":"밖에 없다 is very common but always requires the negative 없다. Don''t confuse with 있다 (affirmative). The subject can be plural or singular depending on context.","examples":[{"ko":"우리는 선택지가 두 개밖에 없어.","en":"We only have two options."},{"ko":"지갑에 천 원밖에 없어.","en":"I only have 1,000 won in my wallet."},{"ko":"남은 음식이 밥밖에 없습니다.","en":"The only food left is rice."},{"ko":"이 문제를 풀 수 있는 사람은 민수밖에 없어요.","en":"Minsu is the only one who can solve this problem."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"우리는 선택지가 두 개밖에 있어.","en":"We only have two options. (INCORRECT)","error_type":"Used affirmative 있다 instead of negative 없다","correction":"우리는 선택지가 두 개밖에 없어."}]},{"name":"-(으)ㄹ 수밖에 없다 — Have no choice but to","level":"Intermediate (A2-B1)","exp":"Combines -(으)ㄹ 수 (the possibility of doing) with 밖에 없다 to say that one action is the only option left: ''have no choice but to ~''.","structure":"Verb stem + -(으)ㄹ 수밖에 없다","when_to_use":"When explaining that circumstances leave only one possible action; showing restriction or inevitability","watch_out":"It is always 수밖에 없다 — the ending must be 없다, and the verb takes -(으)ㄹ (기다릴 수밖에, 먹을 수밖에). For a simple ''only'' with a noun, use Noun + 밖에 + negative (see above).","examples":[{"ko":"이 상황에서는 기다릴 수밖에 없다.","en":"In this situation, we have no choice but to wait."},{"ko":"그는 거짓말을 할 수밖에 없었어.","en":"He had no choice but to lie."},{"ko":"날씨가 나쁘면 집에 있을 수밖에 없어요.","en":"If the weather is bad, I have no choice but to stay home."},{"ko":"부모님 말씀을 들을 수밖에 없었어요.","en":"I had no choice but to listen to my parents."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"이 상황에서는 기다릴 수밖에 한다.","en":"In this situation, we can only wait. (INCORRECT)","error_type":"Used affirmative 한다 instead of 없다","correction":"이 상황에서는 기다릴 수밖에 없다."},{"ko":"기다리는 수밖에 없어요.","en":"I have no choice but to wait.","error_type":"Needs -(으)ㄹ before 수","correction":"기다릴 수밖에 없어요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '0b38928a-5730-4b34-b20f-9e276d830773' and md5(patterns_json) = '2e3986c6feb8ce016c907afaa97f2a7d';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"부터 ~ 까지 — From ~ To","level":"Beginner (A1)","exp":"부터 (from) and 까지 (to/until) are used together to indicate a range in time, distance, quantity, or sequence. 부터 marks the starting point and 까지 marks the ending point.","structure":"Noun + 부터 Noun + 까지","when_to_use":"When expressing a range or span of something (time periods, distances, numbers, groups of people, etc.)","watch_out":"Both 부터 and 까지 attach directly to nouns without spaces, with 부터 on the start and 까지 on the end. For places, 에서 ~ 까지 is more natural (서울에서 부산까지); 부터 ~ 까지 is mainly for time, order and amounts.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 월요일부터 금요일까지 회사에 갑니다.","en":"I go to the company from Monday to Friday."},{"ko":"오전 9시부터 오후 6시까지 일합니다.","en":"I work from 9 AM to 6 PM."},{"ko":"서울에서 부산까지 기차로 3시간 걸립니다.","en":"It takes 3 hours by train from Seoul to Busan. (places: 에서 ~ 까지)"},{"ko":"1부터 10까지 세어 보세요.","en":"Count from 1 to 10."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 월요일까지 금요일부터 일합니다.","en":"I work from Friday to Monday. (Incorrect word order)","error_type":"Wrong particle order","correction":"월요일부터 금요일까지 (부터 must come first)"},{"ko":"9시부터 6시까지에 일합니다.","en":"I work from 9 o''clock to 6 o''clock. (Extra particle)","error_type":"Extra particle usage","correction":"9시부터 6시까지 일합니다 (remove ~에)"},{"ko":"월요일부터 금요일에 공부합니다.","en":"I study from Monday to Friday.","error_type":"End point marked with 에 instead of 까지","correction":"월요일부터 금요일까지 공부합니다."}]},{"name":"부터 ~ 까지 with Actions/Durations","level":"Beginner (A1)","exp":"When expressing the duration of an action or state, 부터 and 까지 work with time expressions to show when something starts and when it ends or how long it continues.","structure":"Time noun + 부터 Time noun + 까지 + Verb/Adjective","when_to_use":"When describing the span of time during which an action takes place or a state exists. This is commonly used in daily life for schedules and durations.","watch_out":"Make sure the time expressions match in scale (both should be specific times, dates, or both should be relative times). Don''t add extra particles like ~동안 unless emphasizing duration.","examples":[{"ko":"작년부터 올해까지 열심히 공부했습니다.","en":"I studied hard from last year to this year."},{"ko":"7월부터 8월까지 휴가입니다.","en":"I''m on vacation from July to August."},{"ko":"아침 7시부터 밤 10시까지 깨어 있습니다.","en":"I am awake from 7 AM to 10 PM."},{"ko":"2020년부터 2024년까지 5년 동안 일했습니다.","en":"I worked for 5 years from 2020 to 2024."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"작년부터 올해 공부했습니다.","en":"I studied from last year. (Missing ending point)","error_type":"Missing 까지","correction":"작년부터 올해까지 공부했습니다."},{"ko":"7월부터까지 8월 휴가입니다.","en":"Vacation from to August in July. (Incorrect placement)","error_type":"Incorrect particle placement","correction":"7월부터 8월까지 휴가입니다."}]},{"name":"부터 ~ 까지 with Ranges (Numbers, Quantities, Groups)","level":"Beginner-Elementary (A1-A2)","exp":"부터 and 까지 can express ranges with numbers, quantities, ages, price ranges, and groups. This shows inclusivity of both the starting and ending points.","structure":"Number/Quantity/Item + 부터 Number/Quantity/Item + 까지","when_to_use":"When specifying a range of quantities, prices, ages, or selecting items in a sequence. Useful in shopping, describing demographics, and listing items.","watch_out":"Both endpoints are normally included in the range. Use the right counter for what you''re counting (개, 명, 살, 원, etc.).","examples":[{"ko":"5개부터 10개까지 살 수 있습니다.","en":"You can buy from 5 to 10 pieces."},{"ko":"10대부터 50대까지 모두 왔습니다.","en":"Everyone from their 10s to 50s came."},{"ko":"예산은 1만 원부터 10만 원까지예요.","en":"The budget is from 10,000 won to 100,000 won."},{"ko":"첫 번째부터 다섯 번째까지 문제를 풀어요.","en":"I solve problems from the 1st to the 5th."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"5개부터 10개 살 수 있습니다.","en":"You can buy from 5 to 10 pieces. (Missing 까지)","error_type":"Incomplete range expression","correction":"5개부터 10개까지 살 수 있습니다."},{"ko":"1만 원부터 10만 원을까지 가능합니다.","en":"It''s possible from 10,000 won to 100,000 won. (Incorrect particle)","error_type":"Wrong particle before 까지","correction":"1만 원부터 10만 원까지 가능합니다. (remove 을)"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '95e2b0c8-dfd3-431a-8aaf-3eb05c87b8ca' and md5(patterns_json) = '94223541abe524fc27fae36e7c481ebf';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"아/어 보다 — Try doing","level":"Beginner","exp":"Expresses the action of trying or attempting something, or experiencing something for the first time. It suggests giving something a try to see what happens.","structure":"Verb stem + 아/어 + 보다\n\n• If stem ends in ㅏ or ㅗ → + 아 보다\n• If stem ends in other vowels → + 어 보다\n• After 하다 verbs → 해 보다","when_to_use":"When you want to express trying an action, attempting to do something, or experiencing something new. Common in suggestions, requests, and describing personal experiences.","watch_out":"Don''t confuse with 보다 (to see/to look). Here 보다 is an auxiliary verb meaning ''try''. Conjugate the first verb with 아/어 by vowel harmony (먹어 보다, 입어 보다). 보다 itself can combine with it: 한번 봐 봐 (try watching it) is fine.","examples":[{"ko":"이 음식을 먹어 봤어?","en":"Have you tried eating this food?"},{"ko":"한번 해 봐.","en":"Give it a try."},{"ko":"새 카페에 가 봐야겠다.","en":"I should try going to the new cafe."},{"ko":"영어로 말해 봐.","en":"Try speaking in English."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"이 옷을 입아 봐.","en":"Try on these clothes.","error_type":"Wrong vowel harmony (입 has ㅣ → 어)","correction":"이 옷을 입어 봐."},{"ko":"먹 보다.","en":"Try eating","error_type":"Missing vowel connector (아/어)","correction":"먹 보다 → 먹어 보다"},{"ko":"나는 이것을 시도해 봤었었어.","en":"I tried this","error_type":"Excessive past tense stacking","correction":"나는 이것을 시도해 봤었었어 → 나는 이것을 시도해 봤어"}]},{"name":"아/어 보니(까) — Upon trying, I found that...","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Expresses what you discovered or realized after actually trying or experiencing something. The result or finding comes as a surprise or revelation.","structure":"Verb stem + 아/어 + 보니(까) + [discovery/result]\n\n• 보니까 (everyday spoken form)\n• 보니 (shorter; common in writing)\n• Both convey the same meaning","when_to_use":"When describing what you found out after attempting something or experiencing it yourself. Good for sharing personal discoveries, unexpected findings, or recommendations based on experience.","watch_out":"The clause before 보니까 shows the action tried, and the clause after shows what you found — a fact, not a plan or guess. A similar pattern is -아/어 봤더니. Don''t use this pattern if you''re just asking someone to try something.","examples":[{"ko":"해 보니까 생각보다 쉬웠어.","en":"When I tried it, it was easier than I thought."},{"ko":"먹어 보니까 정말 맛있더라.","en":"I tried it, and it was really delicious."},{"ko":"가 보니까 문이 잠겨 있었어.","en":"When I went to check, the door was locked."},{"ko":"신발을 신어 보니까 딱 맞았어.","en":"I tried on the shoes and they fit perfectly."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"해 봐니까 생각보다 쉬웠어.","en":"When I tried it, it was easier than I thought.","error_type":"Wrong form: 보다 + -니까 is 보니까, not 봐니까","correction":"해 보니까 생각보다 쉬웠어. (-니까 attaches to the stem 보-)"},{"ko":"가 봤더니까 그곳이 아주 좋았어.","en":"When I went, that place was very nice","error_type":"Using both 더니 and 까 together","correction":"가 봤더니 그곳이 아주 좋았어. or 가 보니까 그곳이 아주 좋았어."}]},{"name":"아/어 보기로 하다 — Decide to try doing","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Expresses a decision or plan to try doing something. It suggests a resolution or commitment to attempt an action.","structure":"Verb stem + 아/어 + 보기로 + 하다\n\nOften conjugated as:\n• 보기로 했다 (decided to try)\n• 보기로 했습니다 (decided to try - formal)","when_to_use":"When talking about a decision or plan to give something a try — personal resolutions, plans with friends, or announcements.","watch_out":"This pattern emphasizes the decision. Don''t use it if you''re simply asking someone to try something casually. 기로 하다 is fixed: 보기로 했어요, not 보기를/보기에 했어요.","examples":[{"ko":"내일부터 운동을 해 보기로 했어.","en":"I decided to try exercising starting tomorrow."},{"ko":"그 방법을 써 보기로 했어.","en":"I decided to try using that method."},{"ko":"새로운 취미를 시작해 보기로 했습니다.","en":"I decided to try starting a new hobby."},{"ko":"이번엔 다르게 해 보기로 했어요.","en":"This time I decided to try doing it differently."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"해 보기 하다.","en":"Try doing","error_type":"Missing 로 postposition","correction":"해 보기 하다 → 해 보기로 하다"},{"ko":"그 방법을 써 보기를 했어.","en":"I decided to try that method.","error_type":"Wrong particle: 기로, not 기를","correction":"그 방법을 써 보기로 했어."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'ec9d962c-ce55-4c0d-a3a5-34bbaf76308d' and md5(patterns_json) = '7274b1093cee06d4132962912e034ede';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"아/어 주다 — Do for someone","level":"Beginner (A2)","exp":"This pattern expresses doing an action for someone else''s benefit. It shows the speaker''s intention to help or do a favor.","structure":"Verb stem + 아/어 주다\n- Use 아 주다 after stems ending in ㅏ or ㅗ\n- Use 어 주다 after stems ending in other vowels\n- Use 해 주다 for 하다 verbs","when_to_use":"When you want to express doing something as a favor or for someone else''s benefit. Common in everyday conversation when asking or offering help.","watch_out":"Don''t confuse with 아/어 드리다 (formal version used for elders). The subject must be actively doing something; it''s not just giving an object. Pay attention to vowel harmony.","examples":[{"ko":"제가 짐을 들어 드릴게요.","en":"I will carry the luggage for you. (humble form for someone you respect)"},{"ko":"친구가 숙제를 도와줬어.","en":"My friend helped me with my homework."},{"ko":"엄마가 저녁을 만들어 주셨어요.","en":"Mom made dinner for us."},{"ko":"이 문제를 풀어 줄래?","en":"Will you solve this problem for me?"},{"ko":"언니가 옷을 사 줬어.","en":"My older sister bought clothes for me."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"제가 짐을 들 줄게요.","en":"I will carry the luggage for you. (incorrect form)","error_type":"Verb conjugation error","correction":"제가 짐을 들어 줄게요. — the verb needs 아/어 before 주다 (들다 → 들어)"},{"ko":"친구가 도움을 주어 줬어.","en":"Friend helped me (double 주다 error)","error_type":"Redundant usage","correction":"친구가 도와줬어. — don''t use 도움을 주다 with 아/어 주다 together"}]},{"name":"아/어 주다 — Requests and offers (casual)","level":"Intermediate (B1)","exp":"In everyday speech, 아/어 주다 turns a plain command into a request (와 줘, 기다려 줘) and turns ''I''ll do it'' into an offer (해 줄게). It makes the action sound like a favor.","structure":"Verb stem + 아/어 주다\nRequests: ~아/어 줄 수 있어? Can you do this for me?\nOffers: ~아/어 줄게. I''ll do it for you.","when_to_use":"In conversations involving promises, agreements, or when someone is requesting your help. Also used to soften requests or show politeness in casual settings.","watch_out":"In requests, 주다 shows the listener is doing you a favor; in offers, you''re doing them one. Use 주세요 / 주시겠어요? with people you should be polite to.","examples":[{"ko":"내일 와 줄 수 있어?","en":"Can you come tomorrow (for me/the group)?"},{"ko":"실수를 하지 말아 줘.","en":"Please don''t make a mistake."},{"ko":"이번엔 내가 낼게. 다음엔 네가 사 줘.","en":"I''ll pay this time. Next time you treat me."},{"ko":"빨리 끝내 줄게.","en":"I''ll finish it quickly for you."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"실수를 하지 않아 주다.","en":"Don''t make a mistake (incorrect negation form)","error_type":"Negation structure error","correction":"실수하지 말아 줘. — use 말다, not 않다, for ''please don''t''"},{"ko":"다음엔 네가 낼 줘.","en":"Next time you pay. (wrong form)","error_type":"Verb form error","correction":"다음엔 네가 내 줘. — use the 아/어 form (내다 → 내) before 주다"}]},{"name":"아/어 주다 — Social politeness and relationship building","level":"Intermediate (B1)","exp":"This pattern demonstrates consideration for others and is deeply embedded in Korean social interaction. Using 아/어 주다 shows awareness of relationships and hierarchy, making it essential for natural communication.","structure":"Verb stem + 아/어 주다 / 아/어 드리다 (formal for elders)\nCan combine with other endings: 아/어 주고, 아/어 줄게, 아/어 줬어 등","when_to_use":"In any situation where you want to emphasize the benefit to others or show respect through service. Essential when declining politely or when making requests sound less demanding. Builds rapport in both formal and informal contexts.","watch_out":"Overusing it can sound insincere. Choose appropriate formality level (주다 vs 드리다) based on social context. Don''t use 주다 with elders; use 드리다 instead. Be aware that some actions don''t logically fit this pattern.","examples":[{"ko":"잠깐만 기다려 주실래요?","en":"Would you mind waiting a moment (for me)? (Very polite request)"},{"ko":"이 일을 끝내 주면 고맙겠어.","en":"If you finish this work for me, I''d appreciate it."},{"ko":"할머니께 사진을 보여 드렸어요.","en":"I showed Grandma the photos (humble form for elders)."},{"ko":"혼자는 못 할 것 같으니까 도와줄래?","en":"I don''t think I can do it alone, so will you help me?"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"할머니께 사진을 보여 줬어요.","en":"I showed Grandma the photos. (disrespectful form)","error_type":"Formality/respect level error","correction":"할머니께 사진을 보여 드렸어요. — use 드리다 for elders, not 주다"},{"ko":"선생님, 이것 좀 설명해 줘.","en":"Teacher, please explain this. (too casual)","error_type":"Speech level: casual 줘 to a teacher","correction":"선생님, 이것 좀 설명해 주세요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '33c0719f-d6d8-478b-8cff-d603687e651a' and md5(patterns_json) = 'a946df4e3e49d0267de01b5a876dc4c8';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"아/어서 — So/Because","level":"A2","exp":"Connects two clauses to show cause and effect. The first clause is the reason, and the second clause is the result. It expresses natural consequence or sequential actions.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + 아/어서 + Result clause","when_to_use":"When explaining why something happens or what follows as a consequence. Use 아 after stems ending in ㅏ, ㅗ; use 어 for all others.","watch_out":"The second clause can''t be a command or suggestion — use -(으)니까 there (피곤하니까 쉬세요). Don''t put past tense on the verb before 아/어서 (왔어서 ✗); the final verb carries the tense. For a reason, the two clauses may have different subjects (비가 와서 경기가 취소됐어요).","examples":[{"ko":"날씨가 좋아서 공원에 갔어요.","en":"The weather was nice, so I went to the park."},{"ko":"배가 고파서 밥을 먹었어요.","en":"I was hungry, so I ate."},{"ko":"비가 와서 집에 있어요.","en":"It''s raining, so I''m staying home."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"피곤해서 집에서 쉬세요.","en":"You''re tired, so rest at home.","error_type":"Command after -아/어서","correction":"피곤하니까 집에서 쉬세요. (Use -(으)니까 before a command)"},{"ko":"어제 비가 왔어서 집에 있었어요.","en":"It rained yesterday, so I stayed home.","error_type":"Past tense before -아/어서","correction":"어제 비가 와서 집에 있었어요. (Only the final verb takes past tense)"}]},{"name":"아/어서 — Sequential Actions","level":"A2","exp":"Used for two actions in order where the first sets up the second: you go somewhere and do something there, or handle something and then use it. The first action is completed before the second.","structure":"Verb stem + 아/어서 + Next action","when_to_use":"When showing that one action is followed by another in chronological order, particularly for natural or expected sequences.","watch_out":"Use -아/어서 when the second action happens at the place reached or with the thing handled in the first (학교에 가서 친구를 만났어요, 사과를 씻어서 먹었어요). For unrelated actions in a row, use -고 (밥을 먹고 학교에 갔어요). It can''t show simultaneous actions (use -(으)면서).","examples":[{"ko":"학교에 가서 친구를 만났어요.","en":"I went to school and met my friend."},{"ko":"친구를 만나서 같이 영화를 봤어요.","en":"I met my friend and we watched a movie together."},{"ko":"사과를 씻어서 먹었어요.","en":"I washed the apple and ate it."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"책을 읽어서 음악을 들어요.","en":"I read a book and listen to music.","error_type":"Unrelated or simultaneous actions with -아/어서","correction":"책을 읽고 음악을 들어요. (one after the other) or 책을 읽으면서 음악을 들어요. (at the same time)"},{"ko":"밥을 먹어서 학교에 갔어요.","en":"I ate and then went to school.","error_type":"Unrelated sequential actions with -아/어서","correction":"밥을 먹고 학교에 갔어요. (Use -고 when the first action doesn''t set up the second)"}]},{"name":"아/어서 — Reasons in apologies and thanks","level":"A2","exp":"-아/어서 gives the reason in everyday apologies and thanks: 늦어서 죄송해요 (sorry for being late), 도와줘서 고마워요 (thanks for helping). These set phrases use -아/어서, not -(으)니까.","structure":"Verb/Adjective stem + 아/어서 + 죄송하다 / 미안하다 / 고맙다 / 감사하다","when_to_use":"When apologizing for something or thanking someone for something they did.","watch_out":"Don''t use -(으)니까 here (늦었으니까 죄송합니다 ✗), and don''t put past tense before 아/어서 (와 줬어서 ✗) — 와 줘서 고마워요 already refers to the past.","examples":[{"ko":"늦어서 죄송합니다.","en":"I''m sorry for being late."},{"ko":"도와줘서 고마워요.","en":"Thanks for helping me."},{"ko":"연락을 못 드려서 죄송해요.","en":"I''m sorry I couldn''t get in touch."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"늦었으니까 죄송합니다.","en":"I''m sorry for being late.","error_type":"-(으)니까 used in an apology","correction":"늦어서 죄송합니다."},{"ko":"와 줬어서 고마워요.","en":"Thank you for coming.","error_type":"Past tense before -아/어서","correction":"와 줘서 고마워요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'd1b0b337-a936-478f-b10c-045fbae19292' and md5(patterns_json) = '1b0525bb5636c70d911a7dbf9ac7629f';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"았/었/였 — Past tense","level":"Beginner","exp":"The most basic past tense marker in Korean. Add 았, 었, or 였 to verb stems to express completed actions or states in the past.","structure":"Verb stem + 았/었/였\n\n- Use 았 when the last vowel of the stem is ㅏ or ㅗ (갔, 봤)\n- Use 었 after any other vowel (먹었, 마셨, 썼)\n- 하다 → 했 (from 하였)\n- Nouns: 이었 after a consonant (학생이었어요), 였 after a vowel (의사였어요)","when_to_use":"When describing actions or states that were completed in the past. Used in both formal and informal speech.","watch_out":"Vowel harmony is crucial—choosing the wrong 았/었/였 will sound incorrect. Pay attention to the final vowel of the verb stem, not the meaning.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 어제 영화를 봤어요.","en":"I watched a movie yesterday."},{"ko":"그는 밥을 먹었어요.","en":"He ate rice."},{"ko":"우리는 학교에 갔어요.","en":"We went to school."},{"ko":"언니는 편지를 썼어요.","en":"My older sister wrote a letter."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 어제 영화를 보었어요.","en":"I watched a movie yesterday.","error_type":"Vowel harmony error","correction":"After 보 (stem ending in ㅗ), use 았, not 었: 보았어요 → 봤어요."},{"ko":"그는 밥을 먹았어요.","en":"He ate rice.","error_type":"Vowel harmony error","correction":"After 먹 (stem ending in ㅓ), use 었, not 았. Correct: 먹었어요."},{"ko":"그는 숙제를 하았어요.","en":"He did his homework.","error_type":"하다 conjugation error","correction":"하다 becomes 했 in the past: 그는 숙제를 했어요."}]},{"name":"Past tense with descriptive verbs (adjectives)","level":"Beginner","exp":"Descriptive verbs (adjectives) in Korean also conjugate with 았/었/였 to express past states or qualities.","structure":"Descriptive verb stem + 았/었/였\n\nFollows the same vowel harmony rules as action verbs.","when_to_use":"When describing how someone/something was in the past (appearance, condition, emotion, etc.).","watch_out":"Remember that Korean adjectives are technically verbs and conjugate like action verbs. Don''t confuse with English adjectives which don''t conjugate.","examples":[{"ko":"그 사람은 매우 착했어요.","en":"That person was very kind."},{"ko":"날씨가 좋았어요.","en":"The weather was nice."},{"ko":"이 책이 재미있었어요.","en":"This book was interesting."},{"ko":"방이 깨끗했어요.","en":"The room was clean."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"날씨가 좋었어요.","en":"The weather was nice.","error_type":"Vowel harmony error","correction":"After 좋 (stem ending in ㅗ), use 았, not 었. Correct: 좋았어요."},{"ko":"어제는 날씨가 춥었어요.","en":"It was cold yesterday.","error_type":"ㅂ irregular not applied","correction":"춥다 is ㅂ-irregular: ㅂ → 우 before 어 → 추웠어요."}]},{"name":"Past tense negation (didn''t do / wasn''t)","level":"Beginner","exp":"To negate a past action or state, put the past tense on 않다 or 못하다: -지 않았- (didn''t) and -지 못했- (couldn''t).","structure":"Verb stem + 지 않았어요 (didn''t)\n\nOr: Verb stem + 지 못했어요 (couldn''t do)\n\nShort forms: 안 + past verb (안 봤어요), 못 + past verb (못 봤어요)","when_to_use":"When expressing that something didn''t happen or someone wasn''t in a certain state in the past.","watch_out":"않다 always becomes 않았 (않 has ㅏ), whatever the main verb is. Put the past tense on 않다/못하다, not on the main verb. 못했 means ''couldn''t'' (inability).","examples":[{"ko":"저는 어제 영화를 보지 않았어요.","en":"I didn''t watch a movie yesterday."},{"ko":"동생은 학교에 가지 않았어요.","en":"My younger sibling didn''t go to school."},{"ko":"저는 숙제를 하지 못했어요.","en":"I couldn''t do my homework."},{"ko":"날씨가 좋지 않았어요.","en":"The weather wasn''t nice."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 어제 영화를 보지 않었어요.","en":"I didn''t watch a movie yesterday.","error_type":"Vowel harmony error","correction":"After 않 (stem ending in ㅏ), use 았, not 었. Correct: 보지 않았어요."},{"ko":"어제 영화를 봤지 않았어요.","en":"I didn''t watch a movie yesterday.","error_type":"Past tense on the main verb as well as 않다","correction":"어제 영화를 보지 않았어요. (Only 않다 takes the past tense)"},{"ko":"저는 숙제를 할 수 없았어요.","en":"I couldn''t do my homework.","error_type":"Vowel harmony error","correction":"After 없 (stem ending in ㅓ), use 었, not 았. Correct: 할 수 없었어요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'e77676b6-13c6-4b1f-ad6f-89777eba1fce' and md5(patterns_json) = '342c0c9db5bf0196d839ea62871fee91';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"에 vs 에서","level":"beginner","exp":"에 marks destination/location of existence, while 에서 marks location of action/origin","structure":"Noun + 에 (destination/static location) vs Noun + 에서 (action location/origin)","when_to_use":"Use 에 for ''to/at'' (going somewhere, being somewhere statically). Use 에서 for ''at/from'' (doing an action, coming from a place)","watch_out":"Both can mean ''at'' but express different meanings. 에 is about location/direction, 에서 is about where action happens or origin point","examples":[{"ko":"나는 학교에 간다.","en":"I go to school. (destination)"},{"ko":"나는 학교에서 공부한다.","en":"I study at school. (action location)"},{"ko":"책상에 책이 있다.","en":"There is a book on the desk. (static existence)"},{"ko":"책상에서 책을 꺼냈다.","en":"I took out a book from the desk. (action from location)"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"나는 학교에서 간다.","en":"I go at school.","error_type":"wrong particle for direction","correction":"나는 학교에 간다. (Use 에 for destination of movement)"},{"ko":"나는 도서관에 책을 읽었다.","en":"I read a book at the library.","error_type":"wrong particle for action","correction":"나는 도서관에서 책을 읽었다. (Use 에서 for action location)"}]},{"name":"에 for time","level":"beginner","exp":"에 is used to mark specific times, days, dates and years when something happens","structure":"Time word + 에","when_to_use":"Use with specific times (3시에), days (월요일에), dates and years (내년에). Deadlines use 까지 (내일까지). Often with verbs like 있다 (exist/happen at a time), 하다 (do at a time)","watch_out":"Use 에 with specific time points. Don''t use 에 with 오늘, 내일, 어제, 지금, 매일 (내일 만나요, not 내일에). Don''t use 에서 for time points. Duration uses 동안 instead","examples":[{"ko":"회의는 3시에 시작한다.","en":"The meeting starts at 3 o''clock."},{"ko":"월요일에 만나자.","en":"Let''s meet on Monday."},{"ko":"내년에 졸업한다.","en":"I will graduate next year."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"회의는 3시에서 시작한다.","en":"The meeting starts at 3 o''clock.","error_type":"wrong particle for time","correction":"회의는 3시에 시작한다. (Use only 에 for specific time)"},{"ko":"월요일에서 만나자.","en":"Let''s meet on Monday.","error_type":"wrong particle for date","correction":"월요일에 만나자. (Use 에 for dates)"},{"ko":"내일에 만나자.","en":"Let''s meet tomorrow.","error_type":"에 used with 내일","correction":"내일 만나자. (오늘, 내일, 어제, 지금 don''t take 에)"}]},{"name":"에서 for origin/range/source","level":"intermediate","exp":"에서 marks the origin point, source, or starting point of a range, showing where something comes from","structure":"Noun + 에서 (origin/source/range)","when_to_use":"Use for ''from'' (origin: 집에서 출발했다), range (여기에서 저기까지), or source (신문에서 읽었다). Material takes (으)로, not 에서 (나무로 만들었다)","watch_out":"에서 expresses movement FROM a place or starting point. Don''t confuse with 에 which shows destination. Some verbs are more common with 에서 (나오다, 나가다, 떨어지다)","examples":[{"ko":"한국에서 미국으로 이사했다.","en":"I moved from Korea to America."},{"ko":"신문에서 그 뉴스를 봤어요.","en":"I saw that news in the newspaper."},{"ko":"여기에서 저기까지 거리가 얼마나 돼요?","en":"How far is it from here to there?"},{"ko":"그 사람은 부산에서 왔어요.","en":"That person came from Busan."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"한국에 미국으로 이사했다.","en":"I moved from Korea to America.","error_type":"wrong particle for origin","correction":"한국에서 미국으로 이사했다. (Use 에서 for origin point)"},{"ko":"신문에 그 뉴스를 봤어요.","en":"I saw that news in the newspaper.","error_type":"wrong particle for source","correction":"신문에서 그 뉴스를 봤어요. (Use 에서 for source)"},{"ko":"나무에서 책상을 만들었다.","en":"They made a desk from wood.","error_type":"에서 used for material","correction":"나무로 책상을 만들었다. (Use (으)로 for material)"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '802ac187-476b-4a4a-9e60-c3ca3ec7da8a' and md5(patterns_json) = '5bf239bda19680402640784f720b4780';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"은/는 vs 이/가","level":"beginner","exp":"은/는 marks the topic of a sentence (what you''re talking about), while 이/가 marks the subject performing an action or being described. 은 is used after consonants and 는 after vowels; 이 after consonants and 가 after vowels.","structure":"Noun + 은/는 (topic) vs Noun + 이/가 (subject)","when_to_use":"Use 은/는 when introducing or contrasting a topic. Use 이/가 when identifying who/what performs an action, answers 누가/뭐가 (who/what), or describes a specific characteristic.","watch_out":"은/는 shifts focus to contrast or a general statement; 이/가 points to the specific subject (and answers 누가/뭐가). Don''t use both on the same noun. Inside a modifying (relative) clause, use 이/가, not 은/는 (제가 만난 사람). Note 저/나 + 가 → 제가/내가.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 학생입니다. (I am a student.)","en":"Topic: talking about ''me'' → use 는"},{"ko":"누가 학생입니까? 제가 학생입니다. (Who is a student? I am.)","en":"Answering 누가 (who) → use 가 (저 + 가 → 제가)"},{"ko":"비가 옵니다. (It''s raining.)","en":"비 is the subject performing the action → use 가"},{"ko":"커피는 쓰다. (Coffee is bitter [in general].)","en":"Topic statement about coffee → use 는"},{"ko":"어떤 커피가 씁니까? (Which coffee is bitter?)","en":"Asking which specific coffee → use 가"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"나가 학생입니다.","en":"I am a student. (incorrect)","error_type":"Wrong form: 나 + 가 becomes 내가","correction":"저는 학생입니다. (For a self-introduction use 저는/나는; 나가 is never correct — the subject form is 내가/제가)"},{"ko":"비는 옵니다.","en":"It''s raining. (unnatural as a simple report of the weather)","error_type":"Wrong particle choice","correction":"비가 옵니다. (Use 가 for subject of action)"},{"ko":"이 사람은 저는 어제 만난 친구예요.","en":"This is the friend I met yesterday. (incorrect)","error_type":"은/는 inside a modifying clause","correction":"이 사람은 제가 어제 만난 친구예요. (Use 이/가 inside the clause before the noun)"}]},{"name":"Topic vs Subject in Contrast","level":"intermediate","exp":"은/는 often implies contrast (others may differ), while 이/가 presents a neutral, factual statement about the subject.","structure":"Noun + 은/는 (contrasting topic) vs Noun + 이/가 (factual subject)","when_to_use":"Use 은/는 when you want to emphasize ''as for this topic...'' or suggest contrast with other options. Use 이/가 for stating facts without contrast.","watch_out":"Context determines meaning. The same sentence can change nuance depending on which particle you choose. 은/는 can imply ''others are different.''","examples":[{"ko":"저는 커피를 좋아합니다. (As for me, I like coffee.)","en":"Topic ''as for me'' → use 는 (it can hint that others may differ)"},{"ko":"저는 영어는 잘하지만 한국어는 못합니다. (I''m good at English, but not at Korean.)","en":"Contrasting two abilities → use 는 for both"},{"ko":"누가 커피를 좋아합니까? 제가 좋아합니다. (Who likes coffee? I do.)","en":"Direct answer identifying the subject → use 가"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"누가 커피를 좋아합니까? 저는 좋아합니다.","en":"Who likes coffee? I like it. (awkward)","error_type":"Wrong particle for direct answer","correction":"제가 좋아합니다. (Use 가 to directly answer 누가)"},{"ko":"제가 영어를 잘하고 제가 한국어를 못합니다.","en":"I do English well and I don''t do Korean. (unnatural contrast)","error_type":"Missing contrast marking","correction":"저는 영어를 잘하지만 한국어는 못합니다. (Use 는 to show contrast)"}]},{"name":"이/가 in Relative Clauses & Double-Subject Sentences","level":"intermediate","exp":"Inside a modifying clause (the part before a noun), the subject takes 이/가, never 은/는: 우리가 만난 친구. In ''A은/는 B이/가 + adjective'' sentences, 은/는 marks who you''re talking about and 이/가 marks the part being described: 저는 키가 커요. Outside these structures, adjectives can take either particle.","structure":"[Noun + 이/가 + Verb]-modifier + Noun / Noun + 은/는 + Noun + 이/가 + Adjective","when_to_use":"Use 이/가 for the subject inside any clause that modifies a noun, and for the described part in ''topic + subject + adjective'' sentences (저는 키가 커요, 이 식당은 음식이 맛있어요).","watch_out":"Don''t say 저는 만난 친구 — say 제가 만난 친구. Main-clause adjectives are not restricted: 이 집은 커요 (talking about this house) and 이 집이 커요 (this house is the big one) are both correct.","examples":[{"ko":"우리가 만난 친구가 유명합니다. (The friend we met is famous.)","en":"우리가 is inside the modifying clause → must be 가"},{"ko":"엄마가 만든 김치가 제일 맛있어요. (The kimchi Mom made is the best.)","en":"엄마가 is inside the modifying clause → must be 가"},{"ko":"저는 키가 커요. (I''m tall.)","en":"Topic 저는 + subject 키가 + adjective"},{"ko":"이 집은 커요. / 이 집이 커요. (This house is big.)","en":"Both correct: 은 = talking about this house; 이 = this house (not another) is the big one"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"이것은 저는 산 책이에요.","en":"This is the book I bought. (incorrect)","error_type":"Using 은/는 inside a modifying clause","correction":"이것은 제가 산 책이에요."},{"ko":"이것은 엄마는 만든 김치예요.","en":"This is the kimchi Mom made. (incorrect)","error_type":"Using 은/는 inside a modifying clause","correction":"이것은 엄마가 만든 김치예요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'e6b62eda-fa6d-47bf-81b7-a88b8187f837' and md5(patterns_json) = '752348fd0f61a5364d36e5fac247ed86';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"을/를 — Object marker","level":"Beginner","exp":"The object particle that marks the direct object of a transitive verb. Use 을 after consonants and 를 after vowels.","structure":"Noun + 을/를 + Verb","when_to_use":"When indicating what or who receives the action of a verb. Essential for expressing transitive actions.","watch_out":"Don''t confuse with 이/가 (subject marker). Remember: 을 follows consonants, 를 follows vowels. In casual speech 을/를 is often dropped (영화 봐요), but learn to use it correctly first.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 밥을 먹어요.","en":"I eat (a meal)."},{"ko":"그는 영화를 봐요.","en":"He watches a movie."},{"ko":"우리는 한국어를 배워요.","en":"We learn Korean."},{"ko":"제 친구는 책을 읽어요.","en":"My friend reads a book."},{"ko":"엄마가 저를 도와줘요.","en":"Mom helps me."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 밥이 먹어요.","en":"I eat rice. (WRONG)","error_type":"Wrong particle","correction":"저는 밥을 먹어요. (Use 을 for the object, not 이)"},{"ko":"저는 빵를 먹어요.","en":"I eat bread. (WRONG)","error_type":"Wrong vowel/consonant rule","correction":"저는 빵을 먹어요. (Use 을 after a consonant)"},{"ko":"우리는 한국어을 배워요.","en":"We learn Korean. (WRONG)","error_type":"Wrong vowel/consonant rule","correction":"우리는 한국어를 배워요. (Use 를 after vowel 어)"}]},{"name":"을/를 — Time and Duration expression","level":"Intermediate","exp":"을/를 can mark duration or time periods that span across an action, indicating ''for/during'' a certain time.","structure":"Time noun + 을/를 + Verb","when_to_use":"When expressing duration of an action (how long something takes) or a time span during which an action occurs.","watch_out":"The particle is optional and often dropped: 2시간 기다렸어요 / 2시간 동안 기다렸어요 are also natural. Don''t use 에 for a length of time.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 어제 세 시간을 공부했어요.","en":"I studied for three hours yesterday."},{"ko":"언니는 일주일을 여행했어요.","en":"My older sister traveled for a week."},{"ko":"우리는 2시간을 기다렸어요.","en":"We waited for 2 hours."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 어제 세 시간에 공부했어요.","en":"I studied for three hours yesterday. (WRONG)","error_type":"Wrong particle","correction":"저는 어제 세 시간을 공부했어요. or 세 시간 동안 공부했어요. (Use 을/를, 동안 or no particle for duration, not 에)"}]},{"name":"을/를 with movement verbs (place as object)","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Some movement verbs take a place as their object: the place you leave (떠나다), or the path or area you move across or through (건너다, 올라가다, 걷다, 산책하다). With 가다/오다, the destination normally takes 에 (학교에 가요); 학교를 가요 is heard in casual speech, but 에 is the standard.","structure":"Place + 을/를 + 떠나다 / 건너다 / 올라가다 / 걷다 / 산책하다","when_to_use":"With verbs of leaving (떠나다), crossing (건너다), climbing (오르다, 올라가다), or moving through an area (걷다, 산책하다, 지나가다).","watch_out":"Don''t replace 에 with 을/를 for ordinary destinations: 학교에 가요 is the standard form.","examples":[{"ko":"그는 서울을 떠났어요.","en":"He left Seoul."},{"ko":"우리는 산을 올라갔어요.","en":"We climbed up the mountain."},{"ko":"길을 건널 때 조심하세요.","en":"Be careful when you cross the street."},{"ko":"공원을 산책했어요.","en":"I took a walk in the park."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"길에 건너요.","en":"I cross the street. (WRONG)","error_type":"건너다 takes the place as its object","correction":"길을 건너요. (Use 을/를 with 건너다)"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'a7ee532b-7dba-42f4-ab83-93426bc4ea5f' and md5(patterns_json) = '0ded7897efe7959ceee2fcf6ed4090b9';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"잖아(요) — You know / Obviously","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Expresses that something is obvious, well-known, or something the listener already knows. It''s used to remind someone of an obvious fact or to point out what should be common knowledge.","structure":"[Verb/Adjective stem] + 잖아(요) / [Noun] + (이)잖아(요)","when_to_use":"When you want to remind someone of something obvious, make a point about what''s well-known, or express slight exasperation that the listener should already know something. Common in casual conversation.","watch_out":"The tone can sound slightly condescending or impatient, so use carefully. 잖아 (casual) vs 잖아요 (polite). 잖아 attaches straight to the stem (바쁘잖아, 먹잖아). For the past, add 았/었 before it: 바빴잖아(요).","examples":[{"ko":"나 지금 바쁘잖아. 나중에 연락해.","en":"I''m busy right now, you know. Contact me later."},{"ko":"이건 비싼 물건이잖아요. 조심해서 다뤄야 해요.","en":"This is an expensive item, you know. You need to handle it carefully."},{"ko":"날씨가 안 좋잖아. 우산 가져가.","en":"The weather''s bad, you know. Take an umbrella."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"어제 바쁘잖았어.","en":"I was busy yesterday, you know.","error_type":"Incorrect past tense form","correction":"어제 바빴잖아. — the past tense goes before 잖아 (았/었잖아)."},{"ko":"그것을 먹는 잖아요.","en":"You eat that, you know.","error_type":"Extra 는 before 잖아","correction":"그것을 먹잖아요. — Remove the 는 between the verb stem and 잖아."},{"ko":"나 지금 바쁜 잖아.","en":"I''m busy right now, you know.","error_type":"Modifier form before 잖아","correction":"나 지금 바쁘잖아. — 잖아 attaches directly to the stem."}]},{"name":"잖아(요) with Negative Statements","level":"Intermediate","exp":"When used with negation, 잖아(요) emphasizes that something obviously is not the case or shouldn''t be done. This is very common to point out what should be obvious.","structure":"안 + Verb stem + 잖아(요) / Verb stem + 지 않잖아(요) / Noun + 이/가 아니잖아(요)","when_to_use":"To remind someone of an obvious negative fact or to point out what obviously shouldn''t be true. Adds emphasis and slight exasperation.","watch_out":"Make sure negation comes before 잖아(요). Don''t confuse with simple negation; this structure emphasizes the obvious nature of what''s NOT the case.","examples":[{"ko":"저는 의사가 아니잖아요. 제가 어떻게 진단해요?","en":"I''m not a doctor, you know. How would I diagnose that?"},{"ko":"너 그 사람 잘 모르잖아. 다른 사람한테 물어봐.","en":"You don''t really know that person, you know. Ask someone else."},{"ko":"너 어제 안 왔잖아. 뭐 하고 있었어?","en":"You didn''t come yesterday, you know. What were you doing?"}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 의사가 않잖아요.","en":"I''m not a doctor, you know.","error_type":"Nouns are negated with 아니다, not 않다","correction":"저는 의사가 아니잖아요."},{"ko":"날씨가 좋지 않은 잖아.","en":"The weather isn''t good, you know.","error_type":"Modifier form before 잖아","correction":"날씨가 좋지 않잖아. — 잖아 attaches directly to 않-."}]},{"name":"잖아(요) for Asserting Common Knowledge","level":"Intermediate-Advanced","exp":"Used to assert that what you''re saying is common knowledge or should be obvious to everyone. Often used in arguments or when making a point about widely accepted facts.","structure":"[Statement] + 잖아(요). [Additional point]","when_to_use":"In debates, explanations, or when you''re trying to establish a baseline fact that both people should agree on before moving forward in conversation.","watch_out":"Can sound dismissive or condescending if overused. The listener might feel talked down to. Adjust tone and context carefully. Works better in informal settings.","examples":[{"ko":"사람은 누구나 실수를 하잖아. 그래서 용서가 필요한 거야.","en":"Everyone makes mistakes, you know. That''s why forgiveness is necessary."},{"ko":"돈이 없으면 살 수 없잖아요. 그러니까 저축해야 해요.","en":"You can''t buy things without money, obviously. That''s why you need to save."},{"ko":"물은 0도 이하에서 얼잖아. 그래서 겨울에 조심해야 해.","en":"Water freezes below 0 degrees, as you know. That''s why you need to be careful in winter."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"선생님, 내일 시험이잖아.","en":"Teacher, the exam is tomorrow, you know.","error_type":"Speech level: casual 잖아 to a teacher","correction":"선생님, 내일 시험이잖아요."},{"ko":"내일은 주말잖아요.","en":"Tomorrow''s the weekend, you know.","error_type":"Consonant-final noun needs 이잖아요","correction":"내일은 주말이잖아요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'e01d1498-8063-499e-a32a-d11a990fc9ca' and md5(patterns_json) = 'ebf7e5eeab27fec5f68e580a11495230';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"지 마세요 — Don''t","level":"Beginner","exp":"Polite negative imperative form used to tell someone not to do something in a respectful manner","structure":"Verb stem + 지 마세요","when_to_use":"When giving polite commands or requests to avoid doing something, commonly used in instructions, signs, or speaking to people you need to be respectful to","watch_out":"Works with action verbs, not adjectives (a few set wishes are exceptions, e.g. 아프지 마세요 ''don''t get sick''). The verb stem is the base form without -다. This is more polite than 지 마 but less formal than 지 마십시오","examples":[{"ko":"여기에 앉지 마세요.","en":"Please don''t sit here."},{"ko":"늦지 마세요.","en":"Don''t be late."},{"ko":"물을 낭비하지 마세요.","en":"Don''t waste water."},{"ko":"담배를 피우지 마세요.","en":"Please don''t smoke."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"여기에 앉지 않으세요.","en":"Please don''t sit here. (incorrect)","error_type":"Negative command uses -지 마세요, not -지 않으세요","correction":"여기에 앉지 마세요."},{"ko":"아름답지 마세요.","en":"Don''t be beautiful (trying to use with adjective)","error_type":"Using with descriptive verb/adjective","correction":"Use only with action verbs; this structure doesn''t work with adjectives"},{"ko":"늦지 말으세요.","en":"Please don''t be late. (incorrect)","error_type":"말다 + -(으)세요 → 마세요 (ㄹ drops)","correction":"늦지 마세요."}]},{"name":"지 마 — Don''t (Casual/Informal)","level":"Beginner","exp":"Casual negative imperative form used among close friends, family members, or people of equal or lower social status","structure":"Verb stem + 지 마","when_to_use":"In informal conversations with friends, family, or peers; in casual writing like text messages; when the relationship allows for informal speech","watch_out":"Much less polite than 지 마세요. Can sound rude if used with someone you should show respect to. Still only works with action verbs","examples":[{"ko":"거기 가지 마.","en":"Don''t go there."},{"ko":"걱정하지 마.","en":"Don''t worry."},{"ko":"말하지 마.","en":"Don''t speak/say anything."},{"ko":"잊지 마.","en":"Don''t forget."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"선생님, 걱정하지 마.","en":"Teacher, don''t worry. (too casual)","error_type":"Speech level: casual 지 마 to a teacher","correction":"선생님, 걱정하지 마세요."},{"ko":"춥지 마.","en":"Don''t be cold (using with adjective)","error_type":"Using with descriptive verb","correction":"Adjectives can''t take 지 마. Say what to do instead: 따뜻하게 입어. (Dress warmly.)"}]},{"name":"지 마십시오 — Don''t (Formal/Official)","level":"Intermediate","exp":"Formal negative imperative form used in official announcements, written instructions, formal settings, or with people requiring high respect","structure":"Verb stem + 지 마십시오","when_to_use":"In formal written notices (signs, official documents), announcements, public service messages, or when speaking formally to someone of high status; very common in public spaces and official contexts","watch_out":"This is the most formal and respectful negative command form. Not commonly used in everyday conversation. Still limited to action verbs only","examples":[{"ko":"이곳에서 흡연하지 마십시오.","en":"Do not smoke in this area."},{"ko":"쓰레기를 버리지 마십시오.","en":"Do not litter."},{"ko":"잔디에 들어가지 마십시오.","en":"Keep off the grass."},{"ko":"음식을 낭비하지 마십시오.","en":"Do not waste food."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"여기에서 담배를 피우지 마십시요.","en":"Do not smoke here. (incorrect spelling)","error_type":"Wrong ending form","correction":"여기에서 담배를 피우지 마십시오. — Use -시오, not -시요"}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'd8e2a451-249f-4c54-8d9a-582d48e041a5' and md5(patterns_json) = 'c16064232273d62c09f8675eb4b5235c';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"지만 — But/However","level":"Beginner","exp":"Connects two contrasting clauses where the second clause contradicts or opposes the first. The most common way to express ''but'' or ''however'' in Korean.","structure":"Verb/Adjective + 지만 + contrasting clause","when_to_use":"When you want to show contrast between two ideas, situations, or statements. Used in both formal and informal contexts.","watch_out":"The clause before 지만 should be a complete thought. Don''t confuse with similar patterns like ''-으나'' (formal) or ''-는데'' (softer contrast).","examples":[{"ko":"날씨가 맑지만 춥습니다.","en":"The weather is clear, but it''s cold."},{"ko":"그는 똑똑하지만 게으릅니다.","en":"He is smart, but he is lazy."},{"ko":"가고 싶지만 시간이 없습니다.","en":"I want to go, but I don''t have time."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"날씨가 좋습니다지만 춥습니다.","en":"The weather is nice, but it''s cold. (incorrect form)","error_type":"Incorrect stem form","correction":"Use the base form: 날씨가 좋지만 춥습니다. (Drop 습니다 before 지만)"},{"ko":"저는 학생지만 일도 해요.","en":"I''m a student, but I also work.","error_type":"Noun + 지만 without 이다","correction":"저는 학생이지만 일도 해요. (After a consonant-final noun, use 이지만)"}]},{"name":"-지만 with Past Tense — But (in past context)","level":"Beginner","exp":"지만 can be used with past tense to show contrast about past events or states. The structure remains the same, just add past tense before 지만.","structure":"Verb/Adjective + -았/었- + 지만 + contrasting clause","when_to_use":"When contrasting past actions or states. Common in narratives, stories, or describing past experiences.","watch_out":"Remember to attach -았/었- before 지만, not after. The contrast typically appears in the second clause.","examples":[{"ko":"공부했지만 시험에 떨어졌습니다.","en":"I studied, but I failed the exam."},{"ko":"날씨가 좋았지만 사람들이 많이 안 왔습니다.","en":"The weather was nice, but not many people came."},{"ko":"열심히 일했지만 보수가 적었습니다.","en":"I worked hard, but the salary was low."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"공부하지만 시험에 떨어졌습니다.","en":"I study, but I failed the exam. (tense mismatch)","error_type":"Missing past tense marker","correction":"Add -았/었-: 공부했지만 시험에 떨어졌습니다."},{"ko":"날씨가 좋았습니다지만 사람들이 많이 안 왔습니다.","en":"The weather was nice, but many people didn''t come. (incorrect)","error_type":"Incorrect stem form with 지만","correction":"Use the base form: 날씨가 좋았지만 사람들이 많이 안 왔습니다."}]},{"name":"-지만 with Negation — But (negative contrast)","level":"Beginner-Intermediate","exp":"지만 is frequently used with negative statements to create contrast. This pattern shows what something is NOT, but what it actually IS.","structure":"Verb/Adjective + -지 않다/negative + 지만 + positive contrasting clause","when_to_use":"When emphasizing contrast between a negative statement and a positive one. Very common in comparisons and clarifications.","watch_out":"Make sure the negation is clear and grammatically correct. The contrast should logically follow the negation.","examples":[{"ko":"비싸지 않지만 품질이 좋습니다.","en":"It''s not expensive, but the quality is good."},{"ko":"어렵지 않지만 시간이 걸립니다.","en":"It''s not difficult, but it takes time."},{"ko":"저는 의사가 아니지만 건강에 대해 잘 압니다.","en":"I''m not a doctor, but I know a lot about health."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"저는 의사가 않지만 건강에 대해 잘 압니다.","en":"I''m not a doctor, but I know a lot about health. (incorrect negative form)","error_type":"Incorrect negation of a noun","correction":"Nouns are negated with 아니다: 저는 의사가 아니지만 건강에 대해 잘 압니다."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = '38eaa9ff-f958-490f-9bc9-7c926eceabbd' and md5(patterns_json) = '0d782139c54258aacba928b0b3b56b88';
update public.grammar_examples_cache set patterns_json = '[{"name":"처럼 / 같이 — Like/As","level":"A2","exp":"Used to indicate similarity or to make comparisons between two things. Both can mean ''like'' or ''as,'' but they have subtle differences in usage.","structure":"Noun + 처럼 / 같이 + Verb/Adjective","when_to_use":"When you want to say something is similar to something else, or to compare how someone does something with how another person does it. ''처럼'' is more commonly used for ''like/as'' comparisons, while ''같이'' can also mean ''together with.''","watch_out":"처럼 emphasizes similarity in manner or appearance. 같이 can mean both ''like'' and ''together'', so context matters. As ''like'', 같이 is a particle and attaches to the noun (아버지같이); as ''together'', it is an adverb written separately (친구랑 같이). Don''t confuse 같이 with 같다 (to be the same).","examples":[{"ko":"그는 아버지처럼 키가 크다.","en":"He is tall like his father (in the same way his father is tall)."},{"ko":"새처럼 날고 싶어요.","en":"I want to fly like a bird."},{"ko":"우리 같이 공부해요.","en":"Let''s study together. (Here 같이 is the adverb ''together'', not ''like''.)"},{"ko":"별처럼 반짝이는 눈.","en":"Eyes that sparkle like stars."},{"ko":"너처럼 똑똑해지고 싶어.","en":"I want to become smart like you."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"새같다 날고 싶어요.","en":"I want to fly like a bird.","error_type":"Wrong word form","correction":"Use 처럼 or 같이, not 같다. Correct: 새처럼 날고 싶어요."},{"ko":"새 처럼 날고 싶어요.","en":"I want to fly like a bird.","error_type":"Spacing error","correction":"새처럼 날고 싶어요. (처럼 is a particle and attaches to the noun)"}]},{"name":"처럼 — As/Like (Manner/Appearance)","level":"A2","exp":"처럼 specifically marks a comparison of manner, behavior, or appearance. It emphasizes ''in the way that'' or ''looking/acting like.''","structure":"Noun + 처럼 + Adjective/Verb","when_to_use":"When comparing how something appears, behaves, or is done. More formal and specific for manner-based similarities than 같이.","watch_out":"처럼 should not be used to mean ''together''—use 같이 or 함께 for that. 처럼 is always about similarity/comparison.","examples":[{"ko":"왕처럼 행동했어요.","en":"He acted like a king (in a kingly manner)."},{"ko":"마술사처럼 사라졌어요.","en":"He disappeared like a magician (in a magical way)."},{"ko":"이 꽃은 장미처럼 아름다워요.","en":"This flower is beautiful like a rose (has rose-like beauty)."},{"ko":"어린아이처럼 울었어요.","en":"She cried like a child."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"친구처럼 영화를 봤어요.","en":"I watched a movie with my friend. (intended meaning)","error_type":"처럼 means ''like'', not ''together/with''","correction":"친구랑 같이 영화를 봤어요. (친구처럼 means ''like a friend'')"}]},{"name":"같이 — Together/With (Primary meaning: Companionship)","level":"A1","exp":"같이 primarily means ''together'' or ''with,'' indicating joint action. It can also mean ''like'' in comparison, but this is secondary. It''s more casual and commonly used in everyday speech.","structure":"Noun + 와/과·하고·(이)랑 + 같이 + Verb (together) / Noun + 같이 (like)","when_to_use":"When indicating that two or more people/things do something together, or in casual speech when making ''like'' comparisons. More colloquial than 처럼.","watch_out":"같이''s primary meaning is ''together''—only use it for ''like'' comparisons in casual contexts. For formal writing or clear manner comparisons, prefer 처럼. ''Like'' 같이 attaches to the noun (엄마같이); ''together'' 같이 follows 와/과, 하고 or (이)랑 with a space (친구랑 같이). Don''t confuse with the adjective 같다 (to be the same).","examples":[{"ko":"친구들이랑 같이 영화 봤어요.","en":"I watched a movie together with my friends."},{"ko":"엄마같이 요리를 잘하고 싶어요.","en":"I want to cook well like Mom (casual comparison)."},{"ko":"너같이 멋진 사람이 되고 싶어.","en":"I want to become a cool person like you."},{"ko":"언니랑 같이 가자.","en":"Let''s go together with my older sister."}],"incorrect_examples":[{"ko":"친구 같이 가요.","en":"Let''s go together with a friend.","error_type":"Missing 와/과, 하고 or (이)랑 before 같이 ''together''","correction":"친구랑 같이 가요. (친구같이 would mean ''like a friend'')"},{"ko":"그는 나같이예요.","en":"He is like me.","error_type":"Wrong conjugation (mixing 같이 with 같다)","correction":"Use either: 그는 나 같아요 (he is like me/same as me) or compare with 처럼/같이: 그는 나처럼 해요."}]}]', qa_at = now() where id = 'c55aa5ca-4215-479e-8f21-5bc22b57bbc5' and md5(patterns_json) = '9f03940147c1cb5eb6ce96ace07c1c66';
